window.LEARN_DATA = {"config":{"label":"Exam 1","key":"e1-learn-v1","map":"Exam 1 Map.html","test":"Exam 1 Map.html#test","mhPrefix":"e1-","scope":"Perioperative care, immunity, cancer and infection: every currently authored topic and clinical reasoning condition for Exam 1.","weightNote":"No professor question-count blueprint is encoded here; recommendations balance chapter coverage.","page":"Exam 1 Learn.html","chapters":["9","16","17","18","19"]},"weights":{"9":1,"16":1,"17":1,"18":1,"19":1},"lessons":[{"id":"t-preop","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Before surgery (preoperative)","notes":["t-preop"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The preoperative phase runs from the decision to operate until the patient enters the OR. The nurse’s jobs: find the risks that could cause complications, make sure the patient is informed and safely prepared, and teach the exercises that prevent post-op problems. Many surgeries are now same-day, so teaching often happens in a short visit.","Assessment: who is at higher risk: Older adults (less reserve; delirium, falls, skin injury), obesity, obstructive sleep apnea (screen with STOP-BANG), smoking (stop at least 4–8 weeks before if possible), diabetes, heart, lung, kidney or liver disease, malnutrition, alcohol or substance use (withdrawal).; Personal or family history of problems with anesthesia (malignant hyperthermia).; Allergies: drugs, latex, tape, iodine or chlorhexidine.; Labs: CBC, electrolytes, glucose, coagulation (PT/INR, aPTT), kidney function, type and screen, pregnancy test for those who could be pregnant; ECG and chest x-ray as needed.","Medications and supplements: Ask about all drugs, OTC products and herbs. Garlic, ginkgo, ginseng, fish oil, vitamin E and St. John’s wort can raise bleeding risk or interact with anesthesia; usually stopped about 1–2 weeks before.; Anticoagulants and antiplatelets (warfarin, DOACs, clopidogrel, aspirin) are often held days before, exactly as the surgeon or prescriber directs.; Diabetes drugs are adjusted (insulin dose often reduced; some oral drugs held); beta blockers are usually continued.; Long-term corticosteroids may need a stress dose (the adrenal glands can’t respond to surgery).","Fasting intervals such as clear liquids up to 2 hours before anesthesia apply to selected healthy patients having elective procedures. Confirm the individual anesthesia instructions and aspiration risk; a general interval is not permission to give intake against the patient’s orders."],"checks":["Who explains the surgery and obtains consent?","How long before surgery may an adult drink clear liquids?","Why use clippers instead of a razor?","Name three herbs that increase bleeding risk."],"recallAnswers":["The surgeon. The nurse witnesses the signature and notifies the surgeon if the patient doesn’t understand.","Usually up to 2 hours before.","Razors make tiny nicks that raise the risk of surgical site infection.","Garlic, ginkgo, ginseng (also fish oil, vitamin E)."],"requires":[],"terms":["Informed consent","NPO","Advance directive"],"termDefinitions":{"Informed consent":"The patient signs a form after the surgeon explains the procedure, risks, benefits and alternatives. Explaining and getting consent is the surgeon’s legal responsibility; the patient must be an adult (or emancipated), competent, and not sedated. The nurse witnesses the signature, checks that the patient understands, and if he doesn’t, stops and notifies the surgeon (don’t explain the surgery yourself). Give no preop sedatives until the form is signed.","NPO":"Nothing by mouth for a set time before anesthesia. Anesthesia relaxes the protective reflexes, so stomach contents can be aspirated into the lungs. Usual guidance: clear liquids up to 2 hours before, a light meal 6 hours, fatty or fried foods 8 hours. Some protocols give a carbohydrate drink 2 hours before (enhanced recovery).","Advance directive":"A living will or durable power of attorney for health care. Surgery carries risks; the patient’s wishes must be known. Ask whether one exists and place a copy in the chart."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-pre-2","sourceId":"q1-pre-2","q":"Which findings in a preoperative assessment should the nurse report to the surgeon or anesthesia provider? Select all that apply.","o":["Takes ginkgo biloba daily","Her brother had a high fever and rigidity during anesthesia","Potassium 3.0 mEq/L","Drank clear apple juice 4 hours ago","Uses a CPAP machine at night","Takes metoprolol each morning"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Ginkgo raises bleeding risk; a family history suggests malignant hyperthermia; low potassium raises dysrhythmia risk; sleep apnea raises airway and opioid risks. Clear liquids are allowed up to 2 hours before; beta blockers are usually continued.","Supported. Ginkgo raises bleeding risk; a family history suggests malignant hyperthermia; low potassium raises dysrhythmia risk; sleep apnea raises airway and opioid risks. Clear liquids are allowed up to 2 hours before; beta blockers are usually continued.","Supported. Ginkgo raises bleeding risk; a family history suggests malignant hyperthermia; low potassium raises dysrhythmia risk; sleep apnea raises airway and opioid risks. Clear liquids are allowed up to 2 hours before; beta blockers are usually continued.","Not supported for this question. Ginkgo raises bleeding risk; a family history suggests malignant hyperthermia; low potassium raises dysrhythmia risk; sleep apnea raises airway and opioid risks. Clear liquids are allowed up to 2 hours before; beta blockers are usually continued.","Supported. Ginkgo raises bleeding risk; a family history suggests malignant hyperthermia; low potassium raises dysrhythmia risk; sleep apnea raises airway and opioid risks. Clear liquids are allowed up to 2 hours before; beta blockers are usually continued.","Not supported for this question. Ginkgo raises bleeding risk; a family history suggests malignant hyperthermia; low potassium raises dysrhythmia risk; sleep apnea raises airway and opioid risks. Clear liquids are allowed up to 2 hours before; beta blockers are usually continued."],"phys":"Ginkgo raises bleeding risk; a family history suggests malignant hyperthermia; low potassium raises dysrhythmia risk; sleep apnea raises airway and opioid risks. Clear liquids are allowed up to 2 hours before; beta blockers are usually continued.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-pre-3","sourceId":"q1-pre-3","q":"Which instruction about incentive spirometry is correct?","o":["“Blow out as hard and fast as you can.”","“Breathe in slowly and deeply, hold it for a few seconds, then breathe out.”","“Use it once each morning.”","“Lie flat while using it.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Slow, deep inhalation with a breath hold opens the alveoli. Use it about 10 times an hour while awake, sitting upright.","Supported. Slow, deep inhalation with a breath hold opens the alveoli. Use it about 10 times an hour while awake, sitting upright.","Not supported for this question. Slow, deep inhalation with a breath hold opens the alveoli. Use it about 10 times an hour while awake, sitting upright.","Not supported for this question. Slow, deep inhalation with a breath hold opens the alveoli. Use it about 10 times an hour while awake, sitting upright."],"phys":"Slow, deep inhalation with a breath hold opens the alveoli. Use it about 10 times an hour while awake, sitting upright.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-pre-4","sourceId":"q1-pre-4","q":"Hair must be removed from the surgical site. Which method should the nurse use?","o":["A new disposable razor","Electric clippers","A depilatory cream applied the night before","Shaving with soap and water"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Clippers avoid the tiny nicks razors cause, which let bacteria in and raise the risk of surgical site infection.","Supported. Clippers avoid the tiny nicks razors cause, which let bacteria in and raise the risk of surgical site infection.","Not supported for this question. Clippers avoid the tiny nicks razors cause, which let bacteria in and raise the risk of surgical site infection.","Not supported for this question. Clippers avoid the tiny nicks razors cause, which let bacteria in and raise the risk of surgical site infection."],"phys":"Clippers avoid the tiny nicks razors cause, which let bacteria in and raise the risk of surgical site infection.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-pre-5","sourceId":"q1-pre-5","q":"A patient received IV midazolam 10 minutes ago and has not yet signed the surgical consent. What is the nurse’s best action?","o":["Have the patient sign quickly before the drug takes full effect","Notify the surgeon; the consent is not valid while the patient is sedated","Have the spouse sign instead","Sign the form as a witness to the verbal consent"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Consent must be given by a competent, unsedated patient. Tell the surgeon; surgery may be delayed or the legal procedure changed.","Supported. Consent must be given by a competent, unsedated patient. Tell the surgeon; surgery may be delayed or the legal procedure changed.","Not supported for this question. Consent must be given by a competent, unsedated patient. Tell the surgeon; surgery may be delayed or the legal procedure changed.","Not supported for this question. Consent must be given by a competent, unsedated patient. Tell the surgeon; surgery may be delayed or the legal procedure changed."],"phys":"Consent must be given by a competent, unsedated patient. Tell the surgeon; surgery may be delayed or the legal procedure changed.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-pre-6","sourceId":"q1-pre-6","q":"Which preoperative teaching best reduces the risk of venous thromboembolism after surgery?","o":["Keeping the knees bent on a pillow","Leg exercises, early walking and sequential compression devices","Staying in bed for 48 hours","Massaging the calves daily"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Movement and SCDs keep blood flowing. Pillows under the knees and calf massage are avoided.","Supported. Movement and SCDs keep blood flowing. Pillows under the knees and calf massage are avoided.","Not supported for this question. Movement and SCDs keep blood flowing. Pillows under the knees and calf massage are avoided.","Not supported for this question. Movement and SCDs keep blood flowing. Pillows under the knees and calf massage are avoided."],"phys":"Movement and SCDs keep blood flowing. Pillows under the knees and calf massage are avoided.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Fasting intervals such as clear liquids up to 2 hours before anesthesia apply to selected healthy patients having elective procedures. Confirm the individual anesthesia instructions and aspiration risk; a general interval is not permission to give intake against the patient’s orders.","studio":"t-preop"},{"id":"t-intraop","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"During surgery (intraoperative)","notes":["t-intraop"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The intraoperative phase runs from entering the OR to arriving in the PACU. The patient can’t protect himself, so the team’s focus is safety: the right patient, procedure and site, sterile technique, safe positioning, preventing retained items and hypothermia, and catching anesthesia complications such as malignant hyperthermia early.","Types of anesthesia: General: unconsciousness, amnesia, pain relief and muscle relaxation (inhaled and IV drugs); airway is managed with a tube or supraglottic device.; Moderate (procedural) sedation: the patient breathes on his own and responds to voice; needs continuous monitoring and a provider trained in airway rescue.; Regional: a spinal or epidural block, or a peripheral nerve block. Watch for hypotension and bradycardia (sympathetic block), urinary retention, and a post-dural puncture headache (worse sitting up).; Local: numbs a small area; watch for local anesthetic toxicity (ringing ears, metallic taste, perioral numbness, seizures, dysrhythmias).","Keeping the patient safe: Positioning: pad bony prominences and protect nerves and skin; long cases risk pressure injuries and nerve damage.; Unplanned hypothermia (cold room, open body cavity, anesthesia): use warming devices and warmed fluids. Hypothermia raises infection, bleeding and cardiac risk.; Counts of sponges, sharps and instruments to prevent retained surgical items.; Fire safety (oxygen + an ignition source + alcohol prep), electrosurgical grounding pads, and fall prevention on the narrow table."],"checks":["Who marks the surgical site?","What is the earliest sign of malignant hyperthermia?","Which complication of spinal anesthesia is worse when sitting up?"],"recallAnswers":["The surgeon (or the person doing the procedure), ideally with the patient awake and taking part.","An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂ (with tachycardia and rigidity).","Post-dural puncture headache (CSF leak)."],"requires":[],"terms":["Time-out (Universal Protocol)","Circulating nurse vs scrub nurse","dantrolene"],"termDefinitions":{"Time-out (Universal Protocol)":"Right before the incision, the whole team stops and confirms the patient’s identity, the procedure, the site and side, consent, allergies and that needed equipment is present. Prevents wrong-site, wrong-procedure and wrong-patient surgery. Part of a 3-step protocol: preprocedure verification, site marking (by the surgeon, with the patient awake when possible), and the time-out. Anyone can stop the process if something doesn’t match.","Circulating nurse vs scrub nurse":"Circulator: an RN, not scrubbed, who manages the room. Scrub: sterile, hands instruments to the surgeon. The circulator can move freely between sterile and non-sterile areas. The circulator coordinates, advocates, documents, does counts with the scrub person, and monitors the sterile field."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-in-5","sourceId":"q1-in-5","q":"After spinal anesthesia, which finding should the nurse watch for most closely in the first hour?","o":["Hypertension","Hypotension","Hyperthermia","Diarrhea"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. A spinal block blocks sympathetic nerves, so vessels dilate and BP falls (sometimes with bradycardia).","Supported. A spinal block blocks sympathetic nerves, so vessels dilate and BP falls (sometimes with bradycardia).","Not supported for this question. A spinal block blocks sympathetic nerves, so vessels dilate and BP falls (sometimes with bradycardia).","Not supported for this question. A spinal block blocks sympathetic nerves, so vessels dilate and BP falls (sometimes with bradycardia)."],"phys":"A spinal block blocks sympathetic nerves, so vessels dilate and BP falls (sometimes with bradycardia).","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-in-6","sourceId":"q1-in-6","q":"Which task belongs to the circulating nurse?","o":["Passing sterile instruments to the surgeon","Performing the surgical incision","Coordinating the room, documenting and performing counts with the scrub person","Administering the general anesthetic"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. The circulator is unscrubbed and manages the room, advocacy, documentation and counts.","Not supported for this question. The circulator is unscrubbed and manages the room, advocacy, documentation and counts.","Supported. The circulator is unscrubbed and manages the room, advocacy, documentation and counts.","Not supported for this question. The circulator is unscrubbed and manages the room, advocacy, documentation and counts."],"phys":"The circulator is unscrubbed and manages the room, advocacy, documentation and counts.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-intraop"},{"id":"t-postop","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"After surgery (postoperative)","notes":["t-postop"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The postoperative phase starts in the PACU and continues on the unit and at home. The order of assessment: airway, breathing, circulation, then level of consciousness, pain, the surgical site and drains, fluids and urine output. Most complications have a typical time window, so know what to look for and when.","Complications by system: Respiratory: airway obstruction, hypoxemia (restlessness or confusion may be the first sign), atelectasis, pneumonia, opioid respiratory depression, PE.; Cardiovascular: hypotension and hemorrhage (tachycardia, falling BP, a soaked dressing or high drain output), dysrhythmias, DVT (calf swelling, warmth, pain; prevent with SCDs, anticoagulants, early walking).; GI: post-op nausea and vomiting; paralytic ileus (absent bowel sounds, distension, no flatus; NG tube and NPO if severe; walking helps).; Neuro: post-op delirium in older adults (acute, fluctuating confusion).; Skin and wound: infection (usually 3–6 days), dehiscence and evisceration (about 5–10 days).","Pain control: Assess with a scale; reassess after each dose (about 30 minutes after IV, 60 after oral).; Multimodal: acetaminophen, NSAIDs (if allowed), nerve blocks, plus opioids at the lowest effective dose.; PCA: only the patient presses the button; check sedation and RR regularly.; Treat pain so the patient can breathe deeply, cough and walk."],"checks":["What is the first priority in the PACU?","Minimum hourly urine output after surgery?","When do surgical site infections usually appear?","When should a JP drain be emptied?"],"recallAnswers":["The airway (then breathing and circulation).","About 30 mL/hour (0.5 mL/kg/hour).","About 3–6 days after surgery.","When half full; then compress the bulb before closing."],"requires":[],"terms":["Hand-off from PACU","Urine output"],"termDefinitions":{"Hand-off from PACU":"The PACU nurse reports the procedure, anesthesia, fluids and blood loss, drains, meds given, VS trends and problems. Information lost at hand-off causes errors. Do a full assessment on arrival, then VS often (for example every 15 minutes ×4, every 30 ×2, hourly ×4, then every 4 hours, per policy).","Urine output":"At least 30 mL/hour (about 0.5 mL/kg/hour). Low output is an early sign of hypovolemia or kidney injury; anesthesia and opioids can also cause retention. The patient should void within 6–8 hours; use a bladder scan if he can’t."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-post-5","sourceId":"q1-post-5","q":"Which is the PACU nurse’s first priority when a patient arrives from the OR?","o":["Assess pain","Check the surgical dressing","Assess airway patency and breathing","Call the family"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Airway first, then breathing and circulation.","Not supported for this question. Airway first, then breathing and circulation.","Supported. Airway first, then breathing and circulation.","Not supported for this question. Airway first, then breathing and circulation."],"phys":"Airway first, then breathing and circulation.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-post-6","sourceId":"q1-post-6","q":"Eight hours after surgery, a patient has not voided and has lower abdominal discomfort. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Insert an indwelling catheter","Perform a bladder scan","Restrict fluids","Give a diuretic"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Assess first: a bladder scan confirms retention before straight catheterization as ordered.","Supported. Assess first: a bladder scan confirms retention before straight catheterization as ordered.","Not supported for this question. Assess first: a bladder scan confirms retention before straight catheterization as ordered.","Not supported for this question. Assess first: a bladder scan confirms retention before straight catheterization as ordered."],"phys":"Assess first: a bladder scan confirms retention before straight catheterization as ordered.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-post-7","sourceId":"q1-post-7","q":"How should the nurse re-establish suction in a Jackson-Pratt drain after emptying it?","o":["Leave the port open to air","Squeeze the bulb flat and close the port","Attach it to wall suction","Irrigate it with saline"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Compressing the bulb before closing creates the negative pressure that pulls drainage out.","Supported. Compressing the bulb before closing creates the negative pressure that pulls drainage out.","Not supported for this question. Compressing the bulb before closing creates the negative pressure that pulls drainage out.","Not supported for this question. Compressing the bulb before closing creates the negative pressure that pulls drainage out."],"phys":"Compressing the bulb before closing creates the negative pressure that pulls drainage out.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-post-8","sourceId":"q1-post-8","q":"An 84-year-old on post-op day 2 suddenly cannot follow a conversation and is pulling at her IV. Which action comes first?","o":["Apply wrist restraints","Give haloperidol","Assess for causes such as hypoxia, pain, infection and medications","Tell the family this is normal for her age"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Acute, fluctuating confusion is delirium until proven otherwise. Find and treat the cause; avoid restraints.","Not supported for this question. Acute, fluctuating confusion is delirium until proven otherwise. Find and treat the cause; avoid restraints.","Supported. Acute, fluctuating confusion is delirium until proven otherwise. Find and treat the cause; avoid restraints.","Not supported for this question. Acute, fluctuating confusion is delirium until proven otherwise. Find and treat the cause; avoid restraints."],"phys":"Acute, fluctuating confusion is delirium until proven otherwise. Find and treat the cause; avoid restraints.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-post-9","sourceId":"q1-post-9","q":"Which findings support paralytic ileus after surgery? Select all that apply.","o":["Abdominal distension","Absent bowel sounds","No passing of flatus","Frequent loose stools","Nausea and vomiting"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. An ileus stops movement of the gut: distension, absent bowel sounds, no flatus, nausea. Loose stools mean the bowel is moving.","Supported. An ileus stops movement of the gut: distension, absent bowel sounds, no flatus, nausea. Loose stools mean the bowel is moving.","Supported. An ileus stops movement of the gut: distension, absent bowel sounds, no flatus, nausea. Loose stools mean the bowel is moving.","Not supported for this question. An ileus stops movement of the gut: distension, absent bowel sounds, no flatus, nausea. Loose stools mean the bowel is moving.","Supported. An ileus stops movement of the gut: distension, absent bowel sounds, no flatus, nausea. Loose stools mean the bowel is moving."],"phys":"An ileus stops movement of the gut: distension, absent bowel sounds, no flatus, nausea. Loose stools mean the bowel is moving.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-postop"},{"id":"t-inflam","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Inflammation and the white blood cells","notes":["t-inflam"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The immune system’s first job is to tell self from non-self (cells carry self markers, the HLAs, on their surfaces). Inflammation is the fast, nonspecific (innate) response to any injury or invader: it acts the same way every time and doesn’t create memory. The white blood cells (leukocytes), made in the bone marrow, carry out both inflammation and immunity. A normal WBC count is about 5,000–10,000/mm³.","The five signs of inflammation: Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, loss of function.; Caused by vasodilation and leaky capillaries (histamine, kinins, prostaglandins).; Inflammation happens with or without infection (a sprained ankle, a burn, an insect bite).","The three stages: Stage I, vascular: vessels dilate and leak; redness, warmth and swelling (exudate).; Stage II, cellular exudate: neutrophils rush in (the count rises within hours); pus forms in bacterial infection.; Stage III, tissue repair and replacement: tissue is rebuilt or replaced by scar; this phase needs protein and nutrients."],"checks":["Which WBC is the first to arrive at an infection?","What is a “left shift”?","Name the five signs of inflammation.","Which WBCs rise in allergy and parasite infections?"],"recallAnswers":["The neutrophil.","More bands (immature neutrophils) than normal: a serious bacterial infection.","Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, loss of function.","Eosinophils."],"requires":[],"terms":["Neutrophils","Macrophages (monocytes)","Eosinophils and basophils","Lymphocytes"],"termDefinitions":{"Neutrophils":"The largest share of WBCs (about 55–70%). Mature ones are segs; immature ones are bands. First responders: they swallow and destroy bacteria (phagocytosis) but live only hours. High count = acute bacterial infection; more bands (left shift) = a serious infection; a low count (neutropenia) = high infection risk.","Macrophages (monocytes)":"Monocytes in the blood become macrophages in tissue. They live longer, eat invaders and debris, and present antigens to lymphocytes, linking inflammation to immunity. Important in chronic infection and tissue repair.","Eosinophils and basophils":"Eosinophils about 1–4%; basophils about 0.5–1%. Eosinophils fight parasites and rise in allergies; basophils (and tissue mast cells) release histamine, heparin and other mediators. Raised eosinophils point to allergy or parasites; histamine drives allergic reactions.","Lymphocytes":"About 20–40% of WBCs: B cells, T cells and natural killer (NK) cells. B cells make antibodies; T cells direct and carry out cell-mediated immunity; NK cells kill virus-infected and cancer cells. These give specific immunity with memory (see the next topic)."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-inf-2","sourceId":"q1-inf-2","q":"Which of these are the classic signs of inflammation? Select all that apply.","o":["Redness","Warmth","Swelling","Pain","Loss of function","Pallor"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Supported. Vasodilation and capillary leak cause warmth, redness, swelling, pain and loss of function.","Supported. Vasodilation and capillary leak cause warmth, redness, swelling, pain and loss of function.","Supported. Vasodilation and capillary leak cause warmth, redness, swelling, pain and loss of function.","Supported. Vasodilation and capillary leak cause warmth, redness, swelling, pain and loss of function.","Supported. Vasodilation and capillary leak cause warmth, redness, swelling, pain and loss of function.","Not supported for this question. Vasodilation and capillary leak cause warmth, redness, swelling, pain and loss of function."],"phys":"Vasodilation and capillary leak cause warmth, redness, swelling, pain and loss of function.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-inf-3","sourceId":"q1-inf-3","q":"A patient’s eosinophil count is elevated. Which condition does the nurse expect?","o":["Bacterial pneumonia","Allergic reaction or parasitic infection","Leukemia remission","Dehydration"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Eosinophils rise with allergies and parasites.","Supported. Eosinophils rise with allergies and parasites.","Not supported for this question. Eosinophils rise with allergies and parasites.","Not supported for this question. Eosinophils rise with allergies and parasites."],"phys":"Eosinophils rise with allergies and parasites.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-inf-4","sourceId":"q1-inf-4","q":"An 85-year-old resident with pneumonia has a temperature of 37.2 °C. Which statement by the nurse is most accurate?","o":["“A normal temperature rules out infection.”","“Older adults often have little or no fever; new confusion may be the first sign.”","“Older adults always have higher fevers.”","“Her WBC will be very high.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. With immunosenescence, fever and the classic signs may be blunted.","Supported. With immunosenescence, fever and the classic signs may be blunted.","Not supported for this question. With immunosenescence, fever and the classic signs may be blunted.","Not supported for this question. With immunosenescence, fever and the classic signs may be blunted."],"phys":"With immunosenescence, fever and the classic signs may be blunted.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-inflam"},{"id":"t-immunity","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Antibody-mediated and cell-mediated immunity","notes":["t-immunity"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Specific (adaptive) immunity recognizes one particular invader (antigen) and remembers it, so the next exposure gets a faster, stronger response. Antibody-mediated immunity (AMI, humoral) uses B cells and antibodies (immunoglobulins). Cell-mediated immunity (CMI) uses T cells, which also coordinate the whole immune response. Together with inflammation they give full protection.","Antibody classes: IgG: the most common; the main memory antibody; crosses the placenta.; IgA: in secretions (tears, saliva, mucous membranes, breast milk).; IgM: the first antibody made in a new infection.; IgE: binds mast cells; drives allergic (type I) reactions and fights parasites.; IgD: on B-cell surfaces, helps activate B cells.","Active vs passive immunity: Natural active: you get the disease and make your own antibodies (long-lasting).; Artificial active: a vaccine makes you produce antibodies (long-lasting; may need boosters).; Natural passive: antibodies from mother to baby via placenta or breast milk (temporary).; Artificial passive: someone else’s ready-made antibodies by injection: immune globulin, antivenom, rabies or tetanus immune globulin. Works immediately but lasts only weeks to months."],"checks":["Which antibody crosses the placenta?","Which antibody causes allergic reactions?","A nurse gets hepatitis B immune globulin after a needlestick. Which type of immunity?","Which T cell does HIV destroy?"],"recallAnswers":["IgG.","IgE.","Artificial passive (immediate, short-lived).","The CD4+ helper T cell."],"requires":[],"terms":["How antibodies form","CD4 (helper) and CD8 (cytotoxic) T cells"],"termDefinitions":{"How antibodies form":"Antigen recognition → sensitization (B cells become plasma cells and memory cells) → antibody production → the antibody binds the antigen. Once bound, the antibody marks the invader for destruction (neutralizing it, clumping it, or activating complement). The first response takes days; the memory response is fast. That is how vaccines protect.","CD4 (helper) and CD8 (cytotoxic) T cells":"Helper T cells direct both arms of immunity; cytotoxic T cells kill infected and cancer cells; regulatory (suppressor) T cells calm the response. Losing helper T cells (as in HIV) weakens all of immunity. CMI also drives transplant rejection and type IV (delayed) reactions such as a positive TB skin test."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-imm-2","sourceId":"q1-imm-2","q":"Which antibody is the first to rise in a new infection?","o":["IgG","IgA","IgM","IgE"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. IgM is made first; IgG follows and provides long-term memory.","Not supported for this question. IgM is made first; IgG follows and provides long-term memory.","Supported. IgM is made first; IgG follows and provides long-term memory.","Not supported for this question. IgM is made first; IgG follows and provides long-term memory."],"phys":"IgM is made first; IgG follows and provides long-term memory.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-imm-3","sourceId":"q1-imm-3","q":"A breastfeeding mother asks how breast milk protects her baby. The nurse explains it contains:","o":["IgA antibodies","IgE antibodies","Live vaccine virus","T cells that make antibodies"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. IgA lines mucous membranes and is in breast milk (natural passive immunity).","Not supported for this question. IgA lines mucous membranes and is in breast milk (natural passive immunity).","Not supported for this question. IgA lines mucous membranes and is in breast milk (natural passive immunity).","Not supported for this question. IgA lines mucous membranes and is in breast milk (natural passive immunity)."],"phys":"IgA lines mucous membranes and is in breast milk (natural passive immunity).","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-imm-4","sourceId":"q1-imm-4","q":"Which statement best describes why vaccines give long-lasting protection?","o":["They give the person ready-made antibodies","They cause the body to form memory B and T cells","They kill all bacteria in the body","They work only for a few weeks"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Active immunity creates memory cells that respond quickly to a later exposure.","Supported. Active immunity creates memory cells that respond quickly to a later exposure.","Not supported for this question. Active immunity creates memory cells that respond quickly to a later exposure.","Not supported for this question. Active immunity creates memory cells that respond quickly to a later exposure."],"phys":"Active immunity creates memory cells that respond quickly to a later exposure.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-immunity"},{"id":"t-hyper","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Hypersensitivity (allergy) reactions","notes":["t-hyper"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 17; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["A hypersensitivity reaction is an immune response that is too strong or aimed at something harmless, and it damages the body. The reactions are grouped by how they happen and how fast. Types I–III involve antibodies; type IV involves T cells and is delayed.","Type V (stimulated): Antibodies stimulate a cell receptor instead of destroying the cell: Graves’ disease (antibodies switch on the thyroid).","Latex allergy: Can be type I (hives, anaphylaxis) or type IV (contact dermatitis).; Higher risk: health care workers, people with many surgeries (e.g., spina bifida), and food allergies to banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut (cross-reactions).; Use a latex-free room, equipment and supplies; schedule surgery first in the day if the OR isn’t latex-free."],"checks":["Which hypersensitivity type is a hemolytic transfusion reaction?","Which type is SLE?","Why is a TB skin test read at 48–72 hours?","Which foods cross-react with latex?"],"recallAnswers":["Type II (cytotoxic).","Type III (immune complex).","It is a type IV, delayed T-cell reaction.","Banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut."],"requires":[],"terms":["Type I: rapid, IgE-mediated","Type II: cytotoxic","Type III: immune complex","Type IV: delayed (cell-mediated)"],"termDefinitions":{"Type I: rapid, IgE-mediated":"Minutes after exposure: allergic rhinitis (hay fever), hives, allergic asthma, food, drug, latex and sting allergies, anaphylaxis. IgE on mast cells binds the allergen → histamine release → vasodilation, capillary leak, bronchospasm. Mild: antihistamines, avoiding the allergen. Severe: epinephrine first.","Type II: cytotoxic":"Hemolytic transfusion reaction (ABO mismatch), autoimmune hemolytic anemia, Goodpasture syndrome, some drug reactions. IgG or IgM antibodies attack antigens on the surface of cells, and complement destroys the cells. Stop the trigger (stop a transfusion right away) and support the patient.","Type III: immune complex":"SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, serum sickness, vasculitis, glomerulonephritis. Antigen-antibody complexes circulate and deposit in tissues (kidneys, joints, skin, vessels), triggering inflammation. Treat with anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressant drugs; watch the kidneys.","Type IV: delayed (cell-mediated)":"Positive TB skin test, contact dermatitis (poison ivy, nickel, latex gloves’ chemicals), transplant rejection. Sensitized T cells, not antibodies, cause the reaction, so it takes 24–72 hours. Read a TB skin test at 48–72 hours; topical steroids for contact dermatitis."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-hyp-2","sourceId":"q1-hyp-2","q":"A nurse develops dry, cracked, itchy hands 48 hours after wearing latex gloves. This is which reaction?","o":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. A delayed contact dermatitis is type IV (T-cell mediated).","Not supported for this question. A delayed contact dermatitis is type IV (T-cell mediated).","Not supported for this question. A delayed contact dermatitis is type IV (T-cell mediated).","Supported. A delayed contact dermatitis is type IV (T-cell mediated)."],"phys":"A delayed contact dermatitis is type IV (T-cell mediated).","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-hyp-3","sourceId":"q1-hyp-3","q":"Which patient statement suggests a higher risk of latex allergy?","o":["“I’m allergic to bananas and avocados.”","“I’m allergic to strawberries.”","“I can’t eat dairy.”","“I’m allergic to shellfish.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Banana, avocado, kiwi and chestnut cross-react with latex proteins.","Not supported for this question. Banana, avocado, kiwi and chestnut cross-react with latex proteins.","Not supported for this question. Banana, avocado, kiwi and chestnut cross-react with latex proteins.","Not supported for this question. Banana, avocado, kiwi and chestnut cross-react with latex proteins."],"phys":"Banana, avocado, kiwi and chestnut cross-react with latex proteins.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-hyper"},{"id":"t-autoimm","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Autoimmunity, Lyme disease and transplant rejection","notes":["t-autoimm"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 17; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Autoimmunity happens when the body loses tolerance and attacks its own tissue (examples: SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diabetes, MS, Graves’ disease). Lyme disease is a tick-borne infection whose later stages cause immune-driven joint, heart and nerve problems. Transplant rejection is the immune system doing its normal job against foreign (non-self) tissue.","Lyme disease stages and prevention: Stage 2 (early disseminated): weeks later: more rashes, facial (Bell’s) palsy, meningitis, heart block and palpitations.; Stage 3 (late): months later: arthritis (especially knees), memory and nerve problems.; Prevent: long sleeves and pants tucked into socks, insect repellent (DEET), stay on trails, check skin daily for ticks; remove a tick with fine tweezers pulling straight up steadily, then wash the area.","Immunosuppressants after transplant: Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine): check blood levels; can harm the kidneys and raise BP and glucose.; Mycophenolate (bone marrow suppression, GI upset), corticosteroids, and others.; Taken for life, exactly on schedule; never stopped. Big risks: infection and cancer (especially skin: use sunscreen).; Avoid grapefruit and St. John’s wort with some drugs (they change drug levels); no live vaccines."],"checks":["Which rejection happens within minutes and requires removing the organ?","What is the first-line drug for early Lyme disease?","How do you remove a tick?","Two main risks of lifelong immunosuppression?"],"recallAnswers":["Hyperacute rejection.","Doxycycline.","Fine tweezers close to the skin, pull straight up steadily, then clean the area.","Infection and cancer."],"requires":[],"terms":["Erythema migrans","Hyperacute, acute and chronic rejection"],"termDefinitions":{"Erythema migrans":"An expanding red rash, often with a clear center (“bull’s-eye”), at the tick bite, 3–30 days later, with flu-like symptoms. Borrelia burgdorferi spreading in the skin after a bite from an infected deer (blacklegged) tick. Stage 1 (early localized). Treated with doxycycline (or amoxicillin); early treatment prevents later stages.","Hyperacute, acute and chronic rejection":"Hyperacute: minutes to hours. Acute: usually in the first months. Chronic: months to years, gradual loss of organ function. Hyperacute: pre-existing antibodies (the organ must be removed). Acute: mostly T cells (often reversible with more immunosuppression). Chronic: slow inflammation and fibrosis of vessels (no cure; may need re-transplant). Teach the signs of rejection for that organ: fever, tenderness over the graft, falling function (e.g., rising creatinine and less urine for a kidney)."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-aut-2","sourceId":"q1-aut-2","q":"Which teaching about lifelong immunosuppression after transplant is correct? Select all that apply.","o":["Take the drugs at the same time every day","Report fever or signs of infection promptly","Use sunscreen and get regular skin checks","Stop the drugs if you feel well for a year","Have drug blood levels checked as ordered"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Never stop the drugs. Infection and skin cancer risks rise; levels (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) guide dosing.","Supported. Never stop the drugs. Infection and skin cancer risks rise; levels (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) guide dosing.","Supported. Never stop the drugs. Infection and skin cancer risks rise; levels (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) guide dosing.","Not supported for this question. Never stop the drugs. Infection and skin cancer risks rise; levels (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) guide dosing.","Supported. Never stop the drugs. Infection and skin cancer risks rise; levels (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) guide dosing."],"phys":"Never stop the drugs. Infection and skin cancer risks rise; levels (tacrolimus, cyclosporine) guide dosing.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-aut-3","sourceId":"q1-aut-3","q":"After a hike, a patient has an expanding red rash with a clear center. Which teaching is correct?","o":["This is a type I allergy to the plant","This may be early Lyme disease, which is treated with doxycycline","Squeeze the area to remove the tick’s head","Lyme disease cannot spread beyond the skin"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Erythema migrans = stage 1 Lyme disease. Untreated, it can spread to the heart, nerves and joints.","Supported. Erythema migrans = stage 1 Lyme disease. Untreated, it can spread to the heart, nerves and joints.","Not supported for this question. Erythema migrans = stage 1 Lyme disease. Untreated, it can spread to the heart, nerves and joints.","Not supported for this question. Erythema migrans = stage 1 Lyme disease. Untreated, it can spread to the heart, nerves and joints."],"phys":"Erythema migrans = stage 1 Lyme disease. Untreated, it can spread to the heart, nerves and joints.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-autoimm"},{"id":"t-cancerbio","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Cancer biology, staging and prevention","notes":["t-cancerbio"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Normal cells have contact inhibition (stop dividing when they touch), a specific job (differentiation), and orderly growth and death (apoptosis). Cancer cells have lost these controls: they divide continuously, look and act less like their tissue of origin (anaplasia), ignore contact inhibition, avoid apoptosis, and can invade and spread (metastasize). Benign tumors grow by expansion and stay put; malignant tumors invade and spread.","How cancer spreads: Local invasion → angiogenesis (the tumor grows its own blood supply) → cells break off and travel in blood or lymph → they lodge and grow elsewhere.; Common metastasis sites: bone, liver, lung, brain (and lymph nodes).; Cancers are named by tissue: carcinoma (epithelial), sarcoma (connective tissue), leukemia and lymphoma (blood and lymph).","Causes and risk factors: Chemical (tobacco is the biggest preventable cause; alcohol, asbestos, benzene).; Physical: UV light, ionizing radiation, chronic irritation.; Viral: HPV (cervical, throat, anal), hepatitis B and C (liver), Epstein-Barr (lymphoma), HIV.; Genetic: inherited mutations in tumor suppressor genes (BRCA1/2), oncogene activation; also age, obesity, inactivity, and a weak immune system.; Health equity: income, insurance, neighborhood and racism affect exposure, screening and outcomes."],"checks":["What does the “M” in TNM mean?","Grade 4 tumor cells are…","Which virus causes most cervical cancer, and how is it prevented?","At what age does average-risk colorectal screening start?"],"recallAnswers":["Distant metastasis (M0 none, M1 present).","Undifferentiated (look least like normal cells; most aggressive).","HPV; the HPV vaccine (plus screening).","45."],"requires":[],"terms":["Carcinogenesis","Grading vs staging"],"termDefinitions":{"Carcinogenesis":"Initiation (a carcinogen damages DNA; irreversible) → promotion (promoters such as hormones, obesity or alcohol help initiated cells grow; the latency period) → progression → metastasis. Each step needs more genetic changes, which is why cancer risk rises with age and exposure. Removing promoters (stopping smoking, losing weight, limiting alcohol) can still lower risk after initiation.","Grading vs staging":"Grading: how abnormal the cells look (G1 well differentiated → G4 undifferentiated). Staging: how far the cancer has spread (TNM). Higher grade = more aggressive cells; higher stage = more extensive disease. TNM: T = size of the primary tumor, N = lymph node involvement, M = distant metastasis (M0 none, M1 present). Stages run 0 (in situ) to IV (distant spread)."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-cb-2","sourceId":"q1-cb-2","q":"Which features describe cancer cells? Select all that apply.","o":["Loss of contact inhibition","Rapid or continuous cell division","Anaplasia (loss of normal features)","Ability to metastasize","Normal programmed cell death"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. Cancer cells avoid apoptosis, so they don’t die on schedule.","Supported. Cancer cells avoid apoptosis, so they don’t die on schedule.","Supported. Cancer cells avoid apoptosis, so they don’t die on schedule.","Supported. Cancer cells avoid apoptosis, so they don’t die on schedule.","Not supported for this question. Cancer cells avoid apoptosis, so they don’t die on schedule."],"phys":"Cancer cells avoid apoptosis, so they don’t die on schedule.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-cb-3","sourceId":"q1-cb-3","q":"Which is an example of primary cancer prevention?","o":["Annual mammography","Colonoscopy at age 45","HPV vaccination","A Pap test"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Primary prevention stops cancer from starting (vaccines, avoiding carcinogens). Screening tests are secondary prevention.","Not supported for this question. Primary prevention stops cancer from starting (vaccines, avoiding carcinogens). Screening tests are secondary prevention.","Supported. Primary prevention stops cancer from starting (vaccines, avoiding carcinogens). Screening tests are secondary prevention.","Not supported for this question. Primary prevention stops cancer from starting (vaccines, avoiding carcinogens). Screening tests are secondary prevention."],"phys":"Primary prevention stops cancer from starting (vaccines, avoiding carcinogens). Screening tests are secondary prevention.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-cb-4","sourceId":"q1-cb-4","q":"Which patient meets criteria for low-dose CT lung cancer screening?","o":["A 35-year-old who smokes 1 pack a day","A 62-year-old with a 30 pack-year history who quit 5 years ago","A 70-year-old who never smoked","A 45-year-old with seasonal allergies"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Ages 50–80, 20+ pack-years, currently smoking or quit within 15 years.","Supported. Ages 50–80, 20+ pack-years, currently smoking or quit within 15 years.","Not supported for this question. Ages 50–80, 20+ pack-years, currently smoking or quit within 15 years.","Not supported for this question. Ages 50–80, 20+ pack-years, currently smoking or quit within 15 years."],"phys":"Ages 50–80, 20+ pack-years, currently smoking or quit within 15 years.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-cb-5","sourceId":"q1-cb-5","q":"A tumor is described as grade 4. The nurse understands that the cells are:","o":["Well differentiated and slow-growing","Undifferentiated and aggressive","Benign","Limited to the original tissue layer"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Higher grade = less like normal cells and more aggressive.","Supported. Higher grade = less like normal cells and more aggressive.","Not supported for this question. Higher grade = less like normal cells and more aggressive.","Not supported for this question. Higher grade = less like normal cells and more aggressive."],"phys":"Higher grade = less like normal cells and more aggressive.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-cancerbio"},{"id":"t-cancertx","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Cancer treatment: surgery, radiation, chemotherapy and beyond","notes":["t-cancertx"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Treatment depends on the cancer type, stage and the person’s goals. Most people get a combination. The goal may be cure, control (living with cancer as a chronic disease) or palliation (comfort). Every treatment also harms some normal cells, so the nurse’s work is safety, side-effect management and teaching.","Kinds of cancer surgery: Prophylactic (remove at-risk tissue, e.g., mastectomy for a BRCA mutation), diagnostic (biopsy), curative (remove all the tumor with clear margins), debulking (cytoreductive), palliative (relieve symptoms), reconstructive.","Chemo safety: extravasation: Vesicants can destroy tissue if they leak out of the vein.; Signs: pain, burning, swelling, redness, or no blood return.; Stop the infusion immediately and follow the facility’s extravasation protocol (antidote, warm or cold compresses depending on the drug, notify the provider, document)."],"checks":["Is a patient getting external beam radiation radioactive?","What do you do if a sealed radiation implant falls out?","How long are body fluids hazardous after chemotherapy?","A patient on a checkpoint inhibitor has new diarrhea. Why does it matter?"],"recallAnswers":["No. Only patients with internal (brachytherapy) sources emit radiation.","Don’t touch it; pick it up with long-handled forceps and put it in the lead container; notify radiation safety.","About 48 hours.","It may be immune-related colitis: report it early (often treated with steroids)."],"requires":[],"terms":["External beam radiation (teletherapy)","Brachytherapy (internal radiation)","Chemotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{"External beam radiation (teletherapy)":"A machine aims radiation at marked skin areas, usually daily on weekdays for several weeks. Radiation damages DNA, mostly in fast-dividing cells in the treatment field. The patient is not radioactive. Skin care: wash gently with lukewarm water and mild soap, pat dry, don’t remove the markings, no lotions, powders or deodorant on the area unless approved, avoid sun, heat and tight clothing. Fatigue is common.","Brachytherapy (internal radiation)":"A radioactive source is placed inside or next to the tumor: sealed (implants, seeds) or unsealed (oral or IV radioactive drugs). Delivers a high dose to the tumor while the source is in place, so the patient emits radiation. Private room, a radiation sign, a dosimeter badge for staff, time (limit each visit), distance (stay at least about 6 feet away when not giving care), shielding. No pregnant visitors or children; limited visit time. A dislodged sealed source: never touch it: use long forceps and a lead container. With unsealed sources, body fluids are radioactive.","Chemotherapy":"Drugs that kill dividing cells, given in cycles (rest periods let normal cells recover), often several drugs at once. Some drugs work in a specific phase of the cell cycle; combining them kills more cancer cells and limits resistance. They are hazardous drugs: prepare them in a special hood, wear chemo-rated gloves (two pairs) and a gown, use closed-system devices, and handle the patient’s body fluids as hazardous for about 48 hours (flush the toilet twice with the lid down)."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-tx-1","sourceId":"q1-tx-1","q":"A patient with a sealed cervical radiation implant is on the unit. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.","o":["Place her in a private room with a radiation sign","Wear a dosimeter badge","Limit time spent at the bedside","Allow her 6-year-old to stay overnight","If the implant falls out, pick it up with long forceps and place it in the lead container"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Time, distance, shielding. Children and pregnant visitors are not allowed.","Supported. Time, distance, shielding. Children and pregnant visitors are not allowed.","Supported. Time, distance, shielding. Children and pregnant visitors are not allowed.","Not supported for this question. Time, distance, shielding. Children and pregnant visitors are not allowed.","Supported. Time, distance, shielding. Children and pregnant visitors are not allowed."],"phys":"Time, distance, shielding. Children and pregnant visitors are not allowed.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-tx-3","sourceId":"q1-tx-3","q":"Which skin care teaching is correct for external beam radiation?","o":["Scrub the area to remove dead skin","Wash gently with lukewarm water and mild soap; don’t remove the markings","Apply a heating pad for comfort","Use alcohol-based lotions daily"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Gentle care protects the irritated skin; the markings guide treatment.","Supported. Gentle care protects the irritated skin; the markings guide treatment.","Not supported for this question. Gentle care protects the irritated skin; the markings guide treatment.","Not supported for this question. Gentle care protects the irritated skin; the markings guide treatment."],"phys":"Gentle care protects the irritated skin; the markings guide treatment.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-tx-4","sourceId":"q1-tx-4","q":"During a vesicant chemotherapy infusion, the patient reports burning at the IV site. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Slow the infusion","Stop the infusion","Apply a warm compress","Flush the line"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Stop the infusion right away, then follow the extravasation protocol.","Supported. Stop the infusion right away, then follow the extravasation protocol.","Not supported for this question. Stop the infusion right away, then follow the extravasation protocol.","Not supported for this question. Stop the infusion right away, then follow the extravasation protocol."],"phys":"Stop the infusion right away, then follow the extravasation protocol.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-tx-5","sourceId":"q1-tx-5","q":"A patient on pembrolizumab (a checkpoint inhibitor) reports a new cough and shortness of breath. Why is this important?","o":["It is a normal effect that needs no action","It may be immune-related pneumonitis and needs prompt evaluation","It shows the drug isn’t working","It means an allergy to the drug"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Checkpoint inhibitors can cause the immune system to attack the lungs (and colon, liver, endocrine glands). Report early; steroids are often used.","Supported. Checkpoint inhibitors can cause the immune system to attack the lungs (and colon, liver, endocrine glands). Report early; steroids are often used.","Not supported for this question. Checkpoint inhibitors can cause the immune system to attack the lungs (and colon, liver, endocrine glands). Report early; steroids are often used.","Not supported for this question. Checkpoint inhibitors can cause the immune system to attack the lungs (and colon, liver, endocrine glands). Report early; steroids are often used."],"phys":"Checkpoint inhibitors can cause the immune system to attack the lungs (and colon, liver, endocrine glands). Report early; steroids are often used.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-tx-6","sourceId":"q1-tx-6","q":"How should the nurse handle the urine of a patient who received chemotherapy 12 hours ago?","o":["No precautions are needed","Wear chemotherapy-rated gloves and a gown","Wear a surgical mask only","Add bleach to the urinal"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Body fluids are hazardous for about 48 hours.","Supported. Body fluids are hazardous for about 48 hours.","Not supported for this question. Body fluids are hazardous for about 48 hours.","Not supported for this question. Body fluids are hazardous for about 48 hours."],"phys":"Body fluids are hazardous for about 48 hours.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-cancertx"},{"id":"t-sidefx","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Side effects of cancer treatment","notes":["t-sidefx"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Chemotherapy and radiation hit the body’s fast-dividing normal cells: bone marrow, the GI lining (mouth to rectum), hair follicles and skin. Knowing which cells are hit predicts the side effects. Bone marrow suppression is the most dangerous; fatigue is the most common.","Mucositis (sores along the GI tract): Inspect the mouth daily; frequent oral care with a soft toothbrush and rinses of saline or baking soda solution.; Avoid alcohol-based mouthwash, very hot, spicy or acidic foods, and tobacco.; Topical anesthetic or “magic mouthwash” as ordered; watch for thrush and herpes infections; ensure fluids and nutrition.","Alopecia and skin: Hair loss usually starts 1–3 weeks after treatment and is temporary (it may grow back a different color or texture); radiation hair loss in the field may be permanent at high doses.; Suggest a wig, scarf or hat before it starts, a gentle shampoo, and sun protection for the scalp; scalp cooling may reduce loss for some drugs.; Acknowledge body-image grief."],"checks":["Which side effect of cancer therapy is the most common?","Platelet count where spontaneous bleeding becomes likely?","When should antiemetics for chemotherapy be given?","What mouthwash should a patient with mucositis avoid?"],"recallAnswers":["Fatigue.","Below about 20,000/mm³.","Before chemo and on a schedule afterward.","Alcohol-based mouthwash."],"requires":[],"terms":["Thrombocytopenia","Anemia","Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV)","olanzapine"],"termDefinitions":{"Thrombocytopenia":"Platelets below 150,000/mm³; bleeding risk rises below 50,000, spontaneous bleeding below about 20,000. The marrow makes fewer platelets. Bleeding precautions: soft toothbrush, electric razor, no IM injections or rectal procedures if possible, pressure on puncture sites for at least 5–10 minutes, no aspirin or NSAIDs, prevent constipation and straining, watch for petechiae, bruising, nosebleeds, blood in urine or stool. Platelet transfusion if very low.","Anemia":"Fatigue, pallor, dyspnea on exertion, tachycardia. Fewer red cells carry oxygen. Rest periods; transfusion for low Hgb or symptoms; erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (epoetin) raise the risk of clots and high BP.","Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV)":"Acute (first 24 hours), delayed (days 2–5) or anticipatory (before treatment, a learned response). The drugs stimulate the brain’s vomiting center and the gut (serotonin, substance P). Give antiemetics before chemo and on a schedule, not only when needed: 5-HT3 blockers (ondansetron), NK1 blockers (aprepitant), dexamethasone, olanzapine. Small, bland meals; relaxation for anticipatory nausea."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-se-1","sourceId":"q1-se-1","q":"A patient’s platelet count is 25,000/mm³. Which instructions should the nurse give? Select all that apply.","o":["Use an electric razor","Use a soft-bristle toothbrush","Take aspirin for headaches","Avoid straining with bowel movements","Report blood in the urine or stool"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Supported. Bleeding precautions: avoid trauma and drugs that impair platelets (no aspirin or NSAIDs).","Supported. Bleeding precautions: avoid trauma and drugs that impair platelets (no aspirin or NSAIDs).","Not supported for this question. Bleeding precautions: avoid trauma and drugs that impair platelets (no aspirin or NSAIDs).","Supported. Bleeding precautions: avoid trauma and drugs that impair platelets (no aspirin or NSAIDs).","Supported. Bleeding precautions: avoid trauma and drugs that impair platelets (no aspirin or NSAIDs)."],"phys":"Bleeding precautions: avoid trauma and drugs that impair platelets (no aspirin or NSAIDs).","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-se-3","sourceId":"q1-se-3","q":"A patient with chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy has numb feet. Which teaching is most important?","o":["Walk barefoot to improve sensation","Check bath water temperature with a thermometer or an unaffected body part","Soak the feet in hot water nightly","Avoid wearing shoes at home"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Without sensation she can’t feel a burn. Protective footwear and foot checks prevent injury.","Supported. Without sensation she can’t feel a burn. Protective footwear and foot checks prevent injury.","Not supported for this question. Without sensation she can’t feel a burn. Protective footwear and foot checks prevent injury.","Not supported for this question. Without sensation she can’t feel a burn. Protective footwear and foot checks prevent injury."],"phys":"Without sensation she can’t feel a burn. Protective footwear and foot checks prevent injury.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-se-4","sourceId":"q1-se-4","q":"Which is the most common side effect of cancer treatment?","o":["Alopecia","Fatigue","Mucositis","Neuropathy"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Fatigue is the most common; light exercise and rest help.","Supported. Fatigue is the most common; light exercise and rest help.","Not supported for this question. Fatigue is the most common; light exercise and rest help.","Not supported for this question. Fatigue is the most common; light exercise and rest help."],"phys":"Fatigue is the most common; light exercise and rest help.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-sidefx"},{"id":"t-oncemerg","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Oncologic emergencies","notes":["t-oncemerg"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["An oncologic emergency is a complication of cancer or its treatment that can cause death or permanent harm within hours to days unless it is recognized and treated fast. Nurses often see the first signs, so learn the early cue and the first action for each.","Spinal cord compression: Early cue: back pain (worse lying down or coughing). Late: leg weakness, sensory loss, bowel and bladder dysfunction.; Act: report at once; dexamethasone, MRI, radiation or surgery.","Superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome: A tumor (lung cancer, lymphoma) compresses the SVC: facial, neck and arm swelling, periorbital edema, a tight collar, distended chest veins; late: stridor, cyanosis, confusion.; Act: head up, oxygen, report; radiation, chemo or a stent."],"checks":["Earliest symptom of spinal cord compression?","Which emergency causes a tight shirt collar and facial swelling?","Four electrolyte changes in tumor lysis syndrome?","First treatment for hypercalcemia of malignancy?"],"recallAnswers":["Back pain.","Superior vena cava syndrome.","High potassium, high phosphate, low calcium, high uric acid.","IV normal saline (hydration)."],"requires":[],"terms":["Sepsis and DIC","SIADH","Hypercalcemia"],"termDefinitions":{"Sepsis and DIC":"Sepsis: fever (or none in neutropenia), tachycardia, low BP, confusion. DIC: bleeding from IV sites, gums and wounds, petechiae, plus clots. Neutropenia lets infection spread; sepsis and some cancers (leukemia) trigger widespread clotting that uses up platelets and clotting factors. Cultures then antibiotics within 1 hour. DIC labs: low platelets and fibrinogen, prolonged PT/aPTT, high D-dimer. Treat the cause; replace platelets, plasma, cryoprecipitate; bleeding precautions.","SIADH":"Weight gain without edema, low urine output, concentrated urine, hyponatremia (headache, confusion, seizures). Tumors (especially small cell lung cancer) or drugs make excess ADH, so the kidneys keep water. Fluid restriction, daily weight, sodium checks, seizure precautions; hypertonic saline or ADH antagonists (tolvaptan, conivaptan) for severe cases; correct sodium slowly.","Hypercalcemia":"Fatigue, weakness, constipation, nausea, confusion, polyuria and thirst, dehydration, ECG changes. Bone metastases break down bone, or tumors release a PTH-like hormone. IV normal saline hydration, then loop diuretics; calcitonin, bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid) or denosumab; walking helps keep calcium in bone."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-oe-2","sourceId":"q1-oe-2","q":"A patient with small cell lung cancer has a sodium of 121 mEq/L, concentrated urine and weight gain. Which intervention does the nurse expect?","o":["Fluid restriction and daily weights","Rapid infusion of hypotonic fluid","Potassium supplements","Increased water intake"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. SIADH retains water. Restrict fluids, weigh daily, and use seizure precautions.","Not supported for this question. SIADH retains water. Restrict fluids, weigh daily, and use seizure precautions.","Not supported for this question. SIADH retains water. Restrict fluids, weigh daily, and use seizure precautions.","Not supported for this question. SIADH retains water. Restrict fluids, weigh daily, and use seizure precautions."],"phys":"SIADH retains water. Restrict fluids, weigh daily, and use seizure precautions.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-oe-3","sourceId":"q1-oe-3","q":"A patient with bone metastases has fatigue, constipation, confusion and polyuria. Which initial treatment does the nurse anticipate?","o":["Fluid restriction","IV normal saline hydration","Calcium supplements","Bed rest"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Hypercalcemia causes dehydration; IV saline restores volume and helps the kidneys excrete calcium. Walking also helps.","Supported. Hypercalcemia causes dehydration; IV saline restores volume and helps the kidneys excrete calcium. Walking also helps.","Not supported for this question. Hypercalcemia causes dehydration; IV saline restores volume and helps the kidneys excrete calcium. Walking also helps.","Not supported for this question. Hypercalcemia causes dehydration; IV saline restores volume and helps the kidneys excrete calcium. Walking also helps."],"phys":"Hypercalcemia causes dehydration; IV saline restores volume and helps the kidneys excrete calcium. Walking also helps.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-oe-4","sourceId":"q1-oe-4","q":"Which findings support disseminated intravascular coagulation? Select all that apply.","o":["Oozing from IV sites","Low platelet count","Low fibrinogen","Elevated D-dimer","Shortened PT"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. DIC uses up platelets and clotting factors, so clotting times are prolonged, not shortened.","Supported. DIC uses up platelets and clotting factors, so clotting times are prolonged, not shortened.","Supported. DIC uses up platelets and clotting factors, so clotting times are prolonged, not shortened.","Supported. DIC uses up platelets and clotting factors, so clotting times are prolonged, not shortened.","Not supported for this question. DIC uses up platelets and clotting factors, so clotting times are prolonged, not shortened."],"phys":"DIC uses up platelets and clotting factors, so clotting times are prolonged, not shortened.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-oncemerg"},{"id":"t-infect","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"How infection happens and how it is found","notes":["t-infect"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 19; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["An infection needs every link of the chain of infection: an agent (bacteria, virus, fungus, parasite), a reservoir, a portal of exit, a mode of transmission, a portal of entry and a susceptible host. Breaking any link prevents it, and hand hygiene breaks the most links. Health care–associated infections (HAIs) such as CLABSI, CAUTI, surgical site infections and C. diff are largely preventable.","Labs and imaging: WBC (normal about 5,000–10,000) with differential; a left shift (more bands) signals acute bacterial infection.; ESR and CRP (nonspecific markers of inflammation); procalcitonin (rises in bacterial infection).; Imaging (x-ray, CT, ultrasound) to find abscesses or pneumonia.","Fever: A fever helps fight infection; mild fever may not need treatment.; Treat high or uncomfortable fevers: antipyretics (acetaminophen, NSAIDs), fluids (fever raises fluid loss), light clothing, cooling blankets; avoid shivering (it raises temperature and oxygen use).; Older adults and immunosuppressed people may have little or no fever."],"checks":["What is the single best way to break the chain of infection?","When are cultures collected?","Name the four classic airborne diseases."],"recallAnswers":["Hand hygiene.","Before the first antibiotic dose.","TB, measles (rubeola), chickenpox (varicella), disseminated herpes zoster."],"requires":[],"terms":["Modes of transmission","Culture and sensitivity"],"termDefinitions":{"Modes of transmission":"Contact (direct touch, or indirect via objects and hands), droplet (large particles from coughing, sneezing and talking that travel only a short distance), airborne (tiny particles that stay in the air and travel far). The route decides which precautions and PPE stop it. Airborne examples: TB, measles, chickenpox, disseminated shingles. Droplet: influenza, pertussis, meningococcal meningitis, mumps. Contact: MRSA, VRE, C. diff, scabies, draining wounds.","Culture and sensitivity":"A sample (blood, urine, sputum, wound) is grown in the lab to identify the organism and test which antibiotics work. Antibiotics given first can kill organisms in the sample and give a false negative. Collect cultures before the first antibiotic dose, but don’t delay antibiotics in sepsis. Results take 24–72 hours; then the antibiotic may be changed."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-ic-7","sourceId":"q1-ic-7","q":"Which is the most effective way to prevent the spread of infection in the hospital?","o":["Wearing gloves for all care","Hand hygiene","Giving antibiotics prophylactically","Keeping all patients in private rooms"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Hand hygiene breaks the most links in the chain of infection.","Supported. Hand hygiene breaks the most links in the chain of infection.","Not supported for this question. Hand hygiene breaks the most links in the chain of infection.","Not supported for this question. Hand hygiene breaks the most links in the chain of infection."],"phys":"Hand hygiene breaks the most links in the chain of infection.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ic-8","sourceId":"q1-ic-8","q":"Which statement shows understanding of antibiotic teaching?","o":["“I’ll stop when I feel better.”","“I’ll take all of it exactly as prescribed, even after I feel better.”","“I’ll save the leftovers for next time.”","“I’ll share them with my sick spouse.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Finishing the course as prescribed reduces relapse and resistance.","Supported. Finishing the course as prescribed reduces relapse and resistance.","Not supported for this question. Finishing the course as prescribed reduces relapse and resistance.","Not supported for this question. Finishing the course as prescribed reduces relapse and resistance."],"phys":"Finishing the course as prescribed reduces relapse and resistance.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-infect"},{"id":"t-precautions","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"Standard and transmission-based precautions","notes":["t-precautions"],"source":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 19; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Standard Precautions are used for every patient, every time, because anyone may carry an infection: hand hygiene, PPE for expected contact with blood or body fluids, respiratory hygiene, safe injections and sharps safety. Transmission-based precautions are added for known or suspected infections spread by contact, droplets or air.","Hand hygiene: Alcohol-based hand rub is preferred for routine care (faster and kills more germs), except: hands visibly soiled, after caring for C. diff or norovirus (spores and some viruses), and before eating or after the restroom: then soap and water for at least 15–20 seconds.; Keep nails short; no artificial nails for direct care.","PPE order (CDC): Donning (on): gown → mask or respirator → goggles or face shield → gloves (gloves over the gown cuffs).; Doffing (off): gloves → goggles or face shield → gown → mask or respirator, then hand hygiene. (Another accepted way: remove gown and gloves together.); Remove PPE at the doorway or in the anteroom; take off an N95 only after leaving the room and closing the door.; The most contaminated items (gloves, the front of the gown and face shield) come off first; don’t touch the front."],"checks":["Which precautions need a negative-pressure room and an N95?","What PPE goes on first, and what comes off first?","When is alcohol hand rub not enough?","Meningococcal meningitis needs which precautions?"],"recallAnswers":["Airborne (TB, measles, chickenpox, disseminated zoster).","On first: gown. Off first: gloves.","Visibly soiled hands, C. diff or norovirus, before eating or after the restroom.","Droplet."],"requires":[],"terms":["Contact Precautions","Droplet Precautions","Airborne Precautions"],"termDefinitions":{"Contact Precautions":"MRSA, VRE, CRE, C. diff, scabies, lice, draining wounds, RSV (in some settings). These spread by touch, directly or from contaminated surfaces and equipment. Gown and gloves on entering the room; private room or cohort with the same organism; dedicated equipment (stethoscope, BP cuff); clean surfaces often. For C. diff: soap and water handwashing and bleach cleaning.","Droplet Precautions":"Influenza, pertussis, meningococcal meningitis, mumps, rubella, Mycoplasma pneumonia, adenovirus. Large droplets travel only a short distance (about 3–6 feet) and fall. A surgical mask when working near the patient (put it on as you enter); private room (door may stay open); the patient wears a surgical mask when leaving the room.","Airborne Precautions":"TB, measles, chickenpox, disseminated zoster (and some emerging diseases). Tiny particles float on air currents for long periods and distances. An airborne infection isolation room (AIIR) with negative pressure, the door closed; a fit-tested N95 respirator or a PAPR; the patient wears a surgical mask for transport; staff immune to measles or chickenpox care for those patients."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-ic-1","sourceId":"q1-ic-1","q":"A patient is admitted with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.","o":["Place in a negative-pressure airborne infection isolation room","Wear a fit-tested N95 respirator","Keep the door closed","Have the patient wear a surgical mask during transport","Wear a surgical mask only within 3 feet"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. Airborne Precautions. A surgical mask is not enough for staff caring for a patient with TB.","Supported. Airborne Precautions. A surgical mask is not enough for staff caring for a patient with TB.","Supported. Airborne Precautions. A surgical mask is not enough for staff caring for a patient with TB.","Supported. Airborne Precautions. A surgical mask is not enough for staff caring for a patient with TB.","Not supported for this question. Airborne Precautions. A surgical mask is not enough for staff caring for a patient with TB."],"phys":"Airborne Precautions. A surgical mask is not enough for staff caring for a patient with TB.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ic-3","sourceId":"q1-ic-3","q":"A patient with influenza needs which precautions in addition to Standard Precautions?","o":["Airborne","Droplet","Contact","None"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Influenza spreads by large droplets: a surgical mask near the patient.","Supported. Influenza spreads by large droplets: a surgical mask near the patient.","Not supported for this question. Influenza spreads by large droplets: a surgical mask near the patient.","Not supported for this question. Influenza spreads by large droplets: a surgical mask near the patient."],"phys":"Influenza spreads by large droplets: a surgical mask near the patient.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ic-4","sourceId":"q1-ic-4","q":"Which is the correct order for putting on PPE?","o":["Gloves, gown, mask, goggles","Gown, mask or respirator, goggles or face shield, gloves","Mask, gloves, gown, goggles","Goggles, mask, gloves, gown"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Gown, mask, eye protection, then gloves (over the gown cuffs).","Supported. Gown, mask, eye protection, then gloves (over the gown cuffs).","Not supported for this question. Gown, mask, eye protection, then gloves (over the gown cuffs).","Not supported for this question. Gown, mask, eye protection, then gloves (over the gown cuffs)."],"phys":"Gown, mask, eye protection, then gloves (over the gown cuffs).","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ic-5","sourceId":"q1-ic-5","q":"Which patients can be placed on Droplet Precautions? Select all that apply.","o":["Pertussis","Meningococcal meningitis","Measles","Mumps","Influenza"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Supported. Measles is airborne.","Supported. Measles is airborne.","Not supported for this question. Measles is airborne.","Supported. Measles is airborne.","Supported. Measles is airborne."],"phys":"Measles is airborne.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-precautions"},{"id":"c-neutro","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Neutropenic fever after chemotherapy","patient":"52-year-old woman 9 days after her second cycle of chemotherapy for breast cancer. She calls the clinic: her temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), she had a chill, and she feels “a bit tired but otherwise fine.” In the ED: HR 108, BP 112/68, RR 20, no cough, no redness at her port. ANC 300/mm³, platelets 62,000/mm³, hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL.","notes":["c-neutro"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Fever with chills, tachycardia and an ANC of 300 at the nadir = febrile neutropenia, a medical emergency that can become sepsis within hours, even though she “feels fine.”","Priority: Priority: infection and risk for sepsis (get cultures and antibiotics in fast). Then risk for bleeding (platelets 62,000), fatigue, and anxiety.","Act: Draw cultures (blood from each line and a vein, urine, others as ordered) before the first antibiotic dose, then give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour; don’t delay antibiotics waiting for results. Monitor VS often for sepsis. Neutropenic precautions: hand hygiene (the most important), a private room, no one ill visiting, no fresh flowers, plants or standing water, dedicated equipment, a clean room. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories, enemas and unnecessary invasive procedures. Inspect the mouth, skin, perineum and lines every shift. Give growth factors as ordered (filgrastim, pegfilgrastim: bone pain is common).","Reassess: Recheck temperature, VS, mental status and lactate; review culture results to narrow antibiotics; follow the daily ANC. She is ready for discharge when she is afebrile and her counts are recovering."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Chemotherapy kills fast-dividing cells, including the bone marrow’s, so blood counts fall to their lowest point (the nadir), usually about 7–14 days after a dose. With neutropenia (ANC below 1,500; severe below 500), the usual signs of infection (pus, redness, swelling, infiltrates on x-ray) may be missing, because there are too few neutrophils to make them. A fever of 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher may be the only sign of a life-threatening infection. Also watch for chills, new tachycardia, low BP, confusion, mouth sores, and changes at IV or port sites. Fever with chills, tachycardia and an ANC of 300 at the nadir = febrile neutropenia, a medical emergency that can become sepsis within hours, even though she “feels fine.”","Priority: infection and risk for sepsis (get cultures and antibiotics in fast). Then risk for bleeding (platelets 62,000), fatigue, and anxiety. Draw cultures (blood from each line and a vein, urine, others as ordered) before the first antibiotic dose, then give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour; don’t delay antibiotics waiting for results. Monitor VS often for sepsis. Neutropenic precautions: hand hygiene (the most important), a private room, no one ill visiting, no fresh flowers, plants or standing water, dedicated equipment, a clean room. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories, enemas and unnecessary invasive procedures. Inspect the mouth, skin, perineum and lines every shift. Give growth factors as ordered (filgrastim, pegfilgrastim: bone pain is common).","Recheck temperature, VS, mental status and lactate; review culture results to narrow antibiotics; follow the daily ANC. She is ready for discharge when she is afebrile and her counts are recovering."],"requires":[],"terms":["Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)","Nadir"],"termDefinitions":{"Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)":"WBC × (% segs + % bands) ÷ 100. Example: WBC 1,000 with 25% segs and 5% bands = ANC 300. Neutrophils are the first line against bacteria and fungi. Below 500: severe risk; any fever is an emergency.","Nadir":"The lowest count after a chemotherapy dose. Timing depends on the drug; marrow recovers over the next 1–2 weeks. Teach patients the days of highest risk; doses may be delayed until counts recover."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-ne-2","sourceId":"q1-ne-2","q":"A WBC is 2,000/mm³ with 20% segs and 5% bands. What is the ANC?","o":["400","500","1,000","2,000"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. ANC = 2,000 × (20 + 5)/100 = 500: severe neutropenia.","Supported. ANC = 2,000 × (20 + 5)/100 = 500: severe neutropenia.","Not supported for this question. ANC = 2,000 × (20 + 5)/100 = 500: severe neutropenia.","Not supported for this question. ANC = 2,000 × (20 + 5)/100 = 500: severe neutropenia."],"phys":"ANC = 2,000 × (20 + 5)/100 = 500: severe neutropenia.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ne-3","sourceId":"q1-ne-3","q":"Which actions are appropriate for a patient with severe neutropenia? Select all that apply.","o":["Strict hand hygiene by everyone entering the room","Take rectal temperatures for accuracy","Remove fresh flowers and standing water","Assess the mouth, skin and IV sites every shift","Avoid visitors who are ill"],"a":[0,2,3,4],"r":["Supported. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories and enemas: they can injure the mucosa and let bacteria in.","Not supported for this question. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories and enemas: they can injure the mucosa and let bacteria in.","Supported. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories and enemas: they can injure the mucosa and let bacteria in.","Supported. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories and enemas: they can injure the mucosa and let bacteria in.","Supported. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories and enemas: they can injure the mucosa and let bacteria in."],"phys":"Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories and enemas: they can injure the mucosa and let bacteria in.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ne-4","sourceId":"q1-ne-4","q":"A patient receiving pegfilgrastim reports aching in her hips and back. The nurse explains:","o":["This is a sign of metastasis","Bone pain is a common effect as the marrow makes new white cells","She is allergic to the drug","She should stop the drug"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Growth factors stimulate the marrow, which causes bone pain (treated with mild analgesics).","Supported. Growth factors stimulate the marrow, which causes bone pain (treated with mild analgesics).","Not supported for this question. Growth factors stimulate the marrow, which causes bone pain (treated with mild analgesics).","Not supported for this question. Growth factors stimulate the marrow, which causes bone pain (treated with mild analgesics)."],"phys":"Growth factors stimulate the marrow, which causes bone pain (treated with mild analgesics).","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-neutro"},{"id":"c-tls","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Tumor lysis syndrome","patient":"19-year-old man with acute lymphoblastic leukemia (very high WBC), on day 2 of induction chemotherapy. He has muscle cramps, tingling around his mouth, nausea, and only 120 mL of dark urine in the last 8 hours. Labs: K⁺ 6.4 mEq/L, phosphorus 7.2 mg/dL, calcium 6.9 mg/dL, uric acid 13 mg/dL, creatinine 2.1 mg/dL (baseline 0.8). His monitor shows tall, peaked T waves.","notes":["c-tls"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Day 2 of induction for leukemia with K⁺ 6.4 and peaked T waves, high phosphate, low calcium, high uric acid and oliguria with rising creatinine = tumor lysis syndrome with life-threatening hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury.","Priority: Priority: risk for dysrhythmia and cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia (decreased cardiac output). Then acute kidney injury (fluid and electrolyte imbalance), and injury from hypocalcemia (seizures, tetany).","Act: Continuous cardiac monitoring. Treat hyperkalemia as ordered: IV calcium (protects the heart), insulin with glucose (moves K⁺ into cells), a potassium binder, loop diuretic, and dialysis if severe. Aggressive IV hydration to flush the kidneys (watch for fluid overload). Rasburicase or allopurinol for uric acid; phosphate binders. Strict I&O and daily weights; labs every few hours; seizure precautions.","Reassess: Recheck K⁺, phosphorus, calcium, uric acid and creatinine every 4–6 hours; check the ECG, hourly urine output, weight and lung sounds (fluid overload), and neuromuscular signs."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["When treatment kills a large number of cancer cells quickly, their contents flood the blood: potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (which become uric acid). Highest risk: leukemias and lymphomas, bulky or fast-growing tumors, high WBC, existing kidney problems, dehydration. It usually starts within 1–5 days of starting treatment. Cues: hyperkalemia (weakness, cramps, dysrhythmias, peaked T waves), hyperphosphatemia with hypocalcemia (tingling, muscle spasms, tetany, seizures), hyperuricemia, and acute kidney injury (falling urine output, rising creatinine). Day 2 of induction for leukemia with K⁺ 6.4 and peaked T waves, high phosphate, low calcium, high uric acid and oliguria with rising creatinine = tumor lysis syndrome with life-threatening hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury.","Priority: risk for dysrhythmia and cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia (decreased cardiac output). Then acute kidney injury (fluid and electrolyte imbalance), and injury from hypocalcemia (seizures, tetany). Continuous cardiac monitoring. Treat hyperkalemia as ordered: IV calcium (protects the heart), insulin with glucose (moves K⁺ into cells), a potassium binder, loop diuretic, and dialysis if severe. Aggressive IV hydration to flush the kidneys (watch for fluid overload). Rasburicase or allopurinol for uric acid; phosphate binders. Strict I&O and daily weights; labs every few hours; seizure precautions.","Recheck K⁺, phosphorus, calcium, uric acid and creatinine every 4–6 hours; check the ECG, hourly urine output, weight and lung sounds (fluid overload), and neuromuscular signs."],"requires":[],"terms":["Why calcium falls","Uric acid kidney injury"],"termDefinitions":{"Why calcium falls":"Tingling lips, cramps, positive Chvostek or Trousseau signs. Excess phosphate binds calcium, and the calcium-phosphate crystals also damage the kidneys. Seizure precautions; replace calcium only if symptomatic (as ordered).","Uric acid kidney injury":"Dark, scant urine; rising creatinine. Uric acid crystallizes in the renal tubules and blocks them. Prevent with hydration, allopurinol (stops uric acid forming) or rasburicase (breaks it down)."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-tls-2","sourceId":"q1-tls-2","q":"Which patient is at highest risk for tumor lysis syndrome?","o":["A patient with early-stage breast cancer after lumpectomy","A patient with leukemia and a very high WBC starting induction chemotherapy","A patient receiving hormone therapy for prostate cancer","A patient on radiation for a small skin cancer"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Fast-growing blood cancers with a high tumor burden release the most cell contents when treatment starts.","Supported. Fast-growing blood cancers with a high tumor burden release the most cell contents when treatment starts.","Not supported for this question. Fast-growing blood cancers with a high tumor burden release the most cell contents when treatment starts.","Not supported for this question. Fast-growing blood cancers with a high tumor burden release the most cell contents when treatment starts."],"phys":"Fast-growing blood cancers with a high tumor burden release the most cell contents when treatment starts.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-tls-3","sourceId":"q1-tls-3","q":"Which measures help prevent tumor lysis syndrome? Select all that apply.","o":["IV hydration before and during chemotherapy","Allopurinol or rasburicase","Fluid restriction","Monitoring electrolytes and urine output","Potassium supplements"],"a":[0,1,3],"r":["Supported. Hydration flushes the kidneys; allopurinol or rasburicase lowers uric acid. Potassium is already high.","Supported. Hydration flushes the kidneys; allopurinol or rasburicase lowers uric acid. Potassium is already high.","Not supported for this question. Hydration flushes the kidneys; allopurinol or rasburicase lowers uric acid. Potassium is already high.","Supported. Hydration flushes the kidneys; allopurinol or rasburicase lowers uric acid. Potassium is already high.","Not supported for this question. Hydration flushes the kidneys; allopurinol or rasburicase lowers uric acid. Potassium is already high."],"phys":"Hydration flushes the kidneys; allopurinol or rasburicase lowers uric acid. Potassium is already high.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-tls"},{"id":"c-scc","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Spinal cord compression","patient":"70-year-old man with prostate cancer that has spread to his bones. He has had steadily worsening mid-back pain for 2 weeks that is worse lying down and when he coughs. Today his legs “feel heavy,” he stumbled twice, and he has numbness around his waist. He hasn’t urinated since this morning.","notes":["c-scc"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Worsening back pain, new leg weakness, a sensory level and urinary retention in a man with spinal metastases = spinal cord compression, a neurological emergency: the outcome depends on how much function he has when treatment starts.","Priority: Priority: risk for permanent paralysis (protect neurological function: treatment within hours). Then urinary retention, acute pain, risk for falls, and skin breakdown from immobility.","Act: Report immediately; prepare for an urgent MRI. Give high-dose corticosteroids (dexamethasone) as ordered to reduce swelling around the cord, then radiation and/or surgery (decompression). Neuro checks (strength, sensation, bowel and bladder) often; fall precautions; catheterize for retention; pain control; bowel program; turn and protect the skin; keep the spine aligned as ordered.","Reassess: Compare leg strength, sensation, bladder and bowel function and pain with the last check every few hours. Any decline goes to the provider immediately."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["A tumor (usually a metastasis to the spine: from breast, lung, prostate, kidney cancer, myeloma or lymphoma) presses on the spinal cord. The first symptom in most patients is back pain, often worse lying down, with movement, coughing or straining. Later: weakness of the legs, sensory loss (numbness, tingling, a “level” of lost feeling), and bowel and bladder dysfunction (retention, incontinence, constipation). Worsening back pain, new leg weakness, a sensory level and urinary retention in a man with spinal metastases = spinal cord compression, a neurological emergency: the outcome depends on how much function he has when treatment starts.","Priority: risk for permanent paralysis (protect neurological function: treatment within hours). Then urinary retention, acute pain, risk for falls, and skin breakdown from immobility. Report immediately; prepare for an urgent MRI. Give high-dose corticosteroids (dexamethasone) as ordered to reduce swelling around the cord, then radiation and/or surgery (decompression). Neuro checks (strength, sensation, bowel and bladder) often; fall precautions; catheterize for retention; pain control; bowel program; turn and protect the skin; keep the spine aligned as ordered.","Compare leg strength, sensation, bladder and bowel function and pain with the last check every few hours. Any decline goes to the provider immediately."],"requires":[],"terms":["New back pain in a patient with cancer"],"termDefinitions":{"New back pain in a patient with cancer":"Back pain that keeps getting worse, or wakes the person at night. It may be the only early sign of a tumor growing into the spinal canal. Report it promptly. Waiting until weakness appears risks permanent paralysis."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-scc-1","sourceId":"q1-scc-1","q":"A patient with lung cancer and bone metastases reports new, worsening back pain. What should the nurse do?","o":["Apply heat and reassess tomorrow","Report it promptly as a possible sign of spinal cord compression","Encourage more walking","Document it as expected arthritis"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Back pain is the earliest sign of spinal cord compression. Early treatment prevents paralysis.","Supported. Back pain is the earliest sign of spinal cord compression. Early treatment prevents paralysis.","Not supported for this question. Back pain is the earliest sign of spinal cord compression. Early treatment prevents paralysis.","Not supported for this question. Back pain is the earliest sign of spinal cord compression. Early treatment prevents paralysis."],"phys":"Back pain is the earliest sign of spinal cord compression. Early treatment prevents paralysis.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-scc-2","sourceId":"q1-scc-2","q":"Which drug does the nurse expect to give first for suspected spinal cord compression?","o":["Dexamethasone","Furosemide","Allopurinol","Heparin"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. High-dose corticosteroids reduce swelling around the cord while radiation or surgery is arranged.","Not supported for this question. High-dose corticosteroids reduce swelling around the cord while radiation or surgery is arranged.","Not supported for this question. High-dose corticosteroids reduce swelling around the cord while radiation or surgery is arranged.","Not supported for this question. High-dose corticosteroids reduce swelling around the cord while radiation or surgery is arranged."],"phys":"High-dose corticosteroids reduce swelling around the cord while radiation or surgery is arranged.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-scc"},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Anaphylaxis","patient":"45-year-old woman admitted with pneumonia. Ten minutes after IV ceftriaxone is started she says her skin is itchy and “my throat feels tight.” She has hives on her chest, a hoarse voice, audible wheezing and swollen lips. HR 128, BP 82/50, RR 28, SpO₂ 89%. She takes metoprolol for hypertension. She is anxious and says, “Something terrible is happening.”","notes":["c-anaphylaxis"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Hives, lip swelling, hoarseness, wheezing, hypotension and hypoxemia within minutes of a new IV antibiotic = anaphylaxis with airway compromise and shock. It can kill within minutes.","Priority: Priority: airway (ineffective airway clearance from laryngeal edema and bronchospasm), then breathing (impaired gas exchange), then circulation (decreased cardiac output from shock). Anxiety comes later.","Act: Stop the trigger (stop the infusion but keep the IV access). Call the rapid response team. Assess the airway. Give epinephrine IM first: 0.3–0.5 mg of 1 mg/mL in the anterolateral thigh; repeat every 5–15 minutes as needed. High-flow oxygen (100% by non-rebreather); be ready for intubation or an emergency airway. Position: lying flat with legs raised for shock, or sitting up if she struggles to breathe. IV normal saline bolus. Then the add-on drugs: diphenhydramine (H1 blocker), an H2 blocker, inhaled albuterol for wheezing, and corticosteroids (to prevent a late reaction).","Reassess: Reassess airway sounds, SpO₂, BP and HR every few minutes after each dose. Watch her for several hours (and up to 24) for a biphasic reaction. Document the allergy and update her allergy band.","Suspected anaphylaxis needs immediate emergency help and prompt IM epinephrine under protocol. Airway and breathing support proceed together; antihistamines do not replace epinephrine. Avoid having the patient stand or walk during the reaction."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Anaphylaxis is the most severe type I (IgE-mediated) hypersensitivity reaction: mast cells and basophils release histamine and other mediators all over the body within minutes of exposure (drugs such as antibiotics, contrast dye, latex, insect stings, foods such as peanuts and shellfish). Early cues: a feeling of “impending doom,” anxiety, itching, flushing, hives, a lump in the throat. Then airway signs (hoarseness, stridor, wheezing, swelling of lips, tongue, throat) and circulatory signs (hypotension, tachycardia, weak pulse, dizziness), plus GI cramps, vomiting and diarrhea. Hives, lip swelling, hoarseness, wheezing, hypotension and hypoxemia within minutes of a new IV antibiotic = anaphylaxis with airway compromise and shock. It can kill within minutes.","Priority: airway (ineffective airway clearance from laryngeal edema and bronchospasm), then breathing (impaired gas exchange), then circulation (decreased cardiac output from shock). Anxiety comes later. Stop the trigger (stop the infusion but keep the IV access). Call the rapid response team. Assess the airway. Give epinephrine IM first: 0.3–0.5 mg of 1 mg/mL in the anterolateral thigh; repeat every 5–15 minutes as needed. High-flow oxygen (100% by non-rebreather); be ready for intubation or an emergency airway. Position: lying flat with legs raised for shock, or sitting up if she struggles to breathe. IV normal saline bolus. Then the add-on drugs: diphenhydramine (H1 blocker), an H2 blocker, inhaled albuterol for wheezing, and corticosteroids (to prevent a late reaction).","Reassess airway sounds, SpO₂, BP and HR every few minutes after each dose. Watch her for several hours (and up to 24) for a biphasic reaction. Document the allergy and update her allergy band."],"requires":[],"terms":["Stridor and hoarseness","Hypotension and tachycardia"],"termDefinitions":{"Stridor and hoarseness":"A high-pitched sound on breathing in; her voice changes. Laryngeal edema: histamine makes capillaries leak, and the upper airway swells. The airway is closing. It is the most urgent finding; act now.","Hypotension and tachycardia":"BP falls; HR climbs; skin may be flushed, then pale and cool. Massive vasodilation and fluid leaking out of vessels = distributive shock. Epinephrine, a fluid bolus, and lie her flat with legs raised if breathing allows."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-ana-2","sourceId":"q1-ana-2","q":"Where should the nurse give IM epinephrine for anaphylaxis?","o":["Deltoid","Anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis)","Gluteus","Subcutaneous abdomen"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. The thigh gives the fastest absorption.","Supported. The thigh gives the fastest absorption.","Not supported for this question. The thigh gives the fastest absorption.","Not supported for this question. The thigh gives the fastest absorption."],"phys":"The thigh gives the fastest absorption.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ana-3","sourceId":"q1-ana-3","q":"Which statement shows the patient understands teaching about his epinephrine auto-injector? Select all that apply.","o":["“I’ll carry two auto-injectors with me at all times.”","“I’ll inject it into my outer thigh, even through my pants.”","“After I use it, I’ll call 911.”","“I’ll take an antihistamine first and use the epinephrine only if that doesn’t work.”","“I’ll wear a medical alert bracelet.”"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Use epinephrine at the first sign; antihistamines don’t stop anaphylaxis. Always seek emergency care afterward.","Supported. Use epinephrine at the first sign; antihistamines don’t stop anaphylaxis. Always seek emergency care afterward.","Supported. Use epinephrine at the first sign; antihistamines don’t stop anaphylaxis. Always seek emergency care afterward.","Not supported for this question. Use epinephrine at the first sign; antihistamines don’t stop anaphylaxis. Always seek emergency care afterward.","Supported. Use epinephrine at the first sign; antihistamines don’t stop anaphylaxis. Always seek emergency care afterward."],"phys":"Use epinephrine at the first sign; antihistamines don’t stop anaphylaxis. Always seek emergency care afterward.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ana-4","sourceId":"q1-ana-4","q":"A patient in anaphylaxis is not responding to repeated epinephrine. Which home medication may explain this?","o":["Metoprolol","Acetaminophen","Omeprazole","Levothyroxine"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Beta blockers blunt epinephrine’s effects; glucagon may be needed.","Not supported for this question. Beta blockers blunt epinephrine’s effects; glucagon may be needed.","Not supported for this question. Beta blockers blunt epinephrine’s effects; glucagon may be needed.","Not supported for this question. Beta blockers blunt epinephrine’s effects; glucagon may be needed."],"phys":"Beta blockers blunt epinephrine’s effects; glucagon may be needed.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ana-5","sourceId":"q1-ana-5","q":"Why should a patient be observed for several hours after anaphylaxis has resolved?","o":["To complete paperwork","A biphasic (second-wave) reaction can occur without new exposure","Epinephrine causes delayed hypotension","To give a skin test"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. A biphasic reaction may return hours later.","Supported. A biphasic reaction may return hours later.","Not supported for this question. A biphasic reaction may return hours later.","Not supported for this question. A biphasic reaction may return hours later."],"phys":"A biphasic reaction may return hours later.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Suspected anaphylaxis needs immediate emergency help and prompt IM epinephrine under protocol. Airway and breathing support proceed together; antihistamines do not replace epinephrine. Avoid having the patient stand or walk during the reaction.","studio":"c-anaphylaxis"},{"id":"c-angio","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Angioedema from an ACE inhibitor","patient":"58-year-old Black man who has taken lisinopril for 3 years. He woke up with a swollen lower lip and tongue that has grown over 2 hours. He is drooling a little and his voice is muffled. No hives, no itching, no new foods or drugs. RR 22, SpO₂ 95%, BP 150/92.","notes":["c-angio"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Growing lip and tongue swelling with drooling and a muffled voice in a man on lisinopril, with no hives = ACE-inhibitor angioedema with risk of airway obstruction.","Priority: Priority: risk for airway obstruction (ineffective airway clearance). Then anxiety, and teaching to prevent recurrence.","Act: Assess the airway continuously and keep him sitting upright; give oxygen and keep suction and intubation (or emergency airway) equipment ready, and call the provider and anesthesia early (intubation gets harder as swelling grows). Stop the ACE inhibitor. Give drugs as ordered: diphenhydramine, corticosteroids and epinephrine are often tried; specific options for bradykinin swelling (such as icatibant or C1 esterase inhibitor) may be used. Stay with him.","Reassess: Recheck voice, swallowing, drooling, stridor and SpO₂ often until the swelling is clearly going down. Confirm the allergy is charted and his new BP drug is not an ACE inhibitor."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Angioedema is deep swelling of the skin and mucous membranes, usually the lips, tongue, face, eyelids, throat, and sometimes the bowel. ACE inhibitors (the “-prils”) are a leading cause: they block the breakdown of bradykinin, which builds up and makes vessels leak. It can start any time, even after years on the drug, and is more common in Black patients. ACE-inhibitor angioedema usually has no hives or itching (not histamine). Watch for airway signs: drooling, trouble swallowing, a muffled or hoarse voice, stridor. Growing lip and tongue swelling with drooling and a muffled voice in a man on lisinopril, with no hives = ACE-inhibitor angioedema with risk of airway obstruction.","Priority: risk for airway obstruction (ineffective airway clearance). Then anxiety, and teaching to prevent recurrence. Assess the airway continuously and keep him sitting upright; give oxygen and keep suction and intubation (or emergency airway) equipment ready, and call the provider and anesthesia early (intubation gets harder as swelling grows). Stop the ACE inhibitor. Give drugs as ordered: diphenhydramine, corticosteroids and epinephrine are often tried; specific options for bradykinin swelling (such as icatibant or C1 esterase inhibitor) may be used. Stay with him.","Recheck voice, swallowing, drooling, stridor and SpO₂ often until the swelling is clearly going down. Confirm the allergy is charted and his new BP drug is not an ACE inhibitor."],"requires":[],"terms":["Bradykinin vs histamine swelling"],"termDefinitions":{"Bradykinin vs histamine swelling":"Swelling without hives or itching, slower to build and slower to go away. Bradykinin, not histamine, causes it, so antihistamines and epinephrine help less. The airway is the priority; stop the drug; some patients need intubation (often with fiberoptic tools)."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-ang-1","sourceId":"q1-ang-1","q":"A patient who has taken lisinopril for 4 years has sudden tongue and lip swelling with a muffled voice. What is the priority?","o":["Teach about switching drugs","Assess and protect the airway","Apply ice to the lips","Give an oral antihistamine and discharge"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. ACE-inhibitor angioedema can close the airway. It can occur even after years on the drug.","Supported. ACE-inhibitor angioedema can close the airway. It can occur even after years on the drug.","Not supported for this question. ACE-inhibitor angioedema can close the airway. It can occur even after years on the drug.","Not supported for this question. ACE-inhibitor angioedema can close the airway. It can occur even after years on the drug."],"phys":"ACE-inhibitor angioedema can close the airway. It can occur even after years on the drug.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ang-2","sourceId":"q1-ang-2","q":"Which statement about ACE-inhibitor angioedema is correct?","o":["It only occurs in the first week of therapy","It is caused by histamine, so antihistamines reliably reverse it","It is caused by bradykinin buildup and can occur after years on the drug","It is always accompanied by hives"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown. There are often no hives, and antihistamines and epinephrine help less.","Not supported for this question. ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown. There are often no hives, and antihistamines and epinephrine help less.","Supported. ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown. There are often no hives, and antihistamines and epinephrine help less.","Not supported for this question. ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown. There are often no hives, and antihistamines and epinephrine help less."],"phys":"ACE inhibitors block bradykinin breakdown. There are often no hives, and antihistamines and epinephrine help less.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-angio"},{"id":"c-sle","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","patient":"29-year-old woman diagnosed with SLE 2 years ago, on hydroxychloroquine. After a beach vacation she has a red rash across her cheeks and nose, painful swollen hand joints, extreme fatigue and a low-grade fever. Today her ankles are puffy, her urine is foamy, BP is 148/94 and she has gained 2 kg in a week. She asks whether she can get pregnant.","notes":["c-sle"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Malar rash, arthritis, fever and fatigue after sun exposure = an SLE flare; new edema, proteinuria, hypertension and rising creatinine = probable lupus nephritis.","Priority: Priority: risk for kidney injury (and fluid excess). Then acute pain and fatigue, risk for infection (the disease and its drugs suppress immunity), impaired skin integrity, and body image.","Act: Give drugs as ordered and teach them: hydroxychloroquine (an antimalarial and the backbone of treatment; needs regular eye exams for retinal damage), NSAIDs for joint pain, corticosteroids for flares, and immunosuppressants (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, belimumab) for organ involvement. Balance rest and activity; protect the skin; prevent infection.","Reassess: Track weight, BP, edema, urine protein and creatinine, joint pain and fatigue scores, skin, temperature and signs of infection. Check that she knows sun protection and the warning signs to report."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["SLE is a chronic autoimmune disease: the body makes autoantibodies against its own cells, and immune complexes deposit in tissues (a type III reaction), causing inflammation in the skin, joints, kidneys, heart, lungs, blood and brain. It is most common in women of childbearing age and more common and severe in Black, Hispanic, Asian and Indigenous women. It flares and remits. Look for the butterfly (malar) rash, photosensitivity, joint pain, fatigue, fever, mouth ulcers, hair loss, Raynaud’s, and signs of organ damage. Malar rash, arthritis, fever and fatigue after sun exposure = an SLE flare; new edema, proteinuria, hypertension and rising creatinine = probable lupus nephritis.","Priority: risk for kidney injury (and fluid excess). Then acute pain and fatigue, risk for infection (the disease and its drugs suppress immunity), impaired skin integrity, and body image. Give drugs as ordered and teach them: hydroxychloroquine (an antimalarial and the backbone of treatment; needs regular eye exams for retinal damage), NSAIDs for joint pain, corticosteroids for flares, and immunosuppressants (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, belimumab) for organ involvement. Balance rest and activity; protect the skin; prevent infection.","Track weight, BP, edema, urine protein and creatinine, joint pain and fatigue scores, skin, temperature and signs of infection. Check that she knows sun protection and the warning signs to report."],"requires":[],"terms":["Lupus nephritis","Serositis"],"termDefinitions":{"Lupus nephritis":"Swelling, foamy urine (protein), blood in urine, rising BP and creatinine, weight gain. Immune complexes inflame the glomeruli. A leading cause of death in SLE (kidney failure). Report it; track daily weight, I&O, BP and labs.","Serositis":"Sharp chest pain worse when lying down or breathing in; a friction rub. Inflammation of the pericardium (pericarditis) or pleura. Report new chest pain; heart disease is the other major cause of death."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-sle-2","sourceId":"q1-sle-2","q":"Which findings in a patient with SLE suggest lupus nephritis? Select all that apply.","o":["Foamy urine","Rising serum creatinine","Ankle edema and weight gain","New hypertension","Butterfly rash"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. Proteinuria (foamy urine), rising creatinine, edema and hypertension point to kidney involvement. The rash is a skin sign.","Supported. Proteinuria (foamy urine), rising creatinine, edema and hypertension point to kidney involvement. The rash is a skin sign.","Supported. Proteinuria (foamy urine), rising creatinine, edema and hypertension point to kidney involvement. The rash is a skin sign.","Supported. Proteinuria (foamy urine), rising creatinine, edema and hypertension point to kidney involvement. The rash is a skin sign.","Not supported for this question. Proteinuria (foamy urine), rising creatinine, edema and hypertension point to kidney involvement. The rash is a skin sign."],"phys":"Proteinuria (foamy urine), rising creatinine, edema and hypertension point to kidney involvement. The rash is a skin sign.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-sle-3","sourceId":"q1-sle-3","q":"A patient taking hydroxychloroquine for SLE needs which regular follow-up?","o":["Hearing tests","Eye examinations","Bone density scans every month","Daily blood glucose"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Hydroxychloroquine can damage the retina.","Supported. Hydroxychloroquine can damage the retina.","Not supported for this question. Hydroxychloroquine can damage the retina.","Not supported for this question. Hydroxychloroquine can damage the retina."],"phys":"Hydroxychloroquine can damage the retina.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-sle-4","sourceId":"q1-sle-4","q":"A patient with SLE on immunosuppressants reports a temperature of 38.2 °C. What should the nurse advise?","o":["Take acetaminophen and wait a week","Report it to the provider promptly","Increase sun exposure","Skip the next dose of all medications"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Immunosuppression raises infection risk; a fever must be reported right away.","Supported. Immunosuppression raises infection risk; a fever must be reported right away.","Not supported for this question. Immunosuppression raises infection risk; a fever must be reported right away.","Not supported for this question. Immunosuppression raises infection risk; a fever must be reported right away."],"phys":"Immunosuppression raises infection risk; a fever must be reported right away.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-sle"},{"id":"c-hiv","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"HIV and AIDS","patient":"36-year-old man with HIV who stopped his antiretroviral therapy 8 months ago after losing his job and insurance. For 3 weeks he has had a dry cough, fever and shortness of breath that gets worse on exertion. He has white patches in his mouth and has lost 7 kg. SpO₂ 88% on room air after walking, RR 26. His CD4 count is 140 cells/mm³ and his viral load is high.","notes":["c-hiv"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: CD4 140, off treatment, with dry cough, fever, exertional hypoxemia, diffuse infiltrates and thrush = AIDS with probable Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP).","Priority: Priority: impaired gas exchange (PJP). Then risk for infection (other opportunistic infections), imbalanced nutrition, activity intolerance, and the barriers that stopped his treatment (cost, stigma, depression).","Act: Oxygen, rest periods and position for breathing; treat PJP (TMP-SMX, with corticosteroids for severe hypoxemia). Use Standard Precautions (HIV needs no special isolation). Restart combination antiretroviral therapy (cART): several drugs from different classes, taken every day on schedule to prevent resistance. Prevent infection: hand hygiene, food safety (no raw eggs, meat or seafood; wash produce), avoid cat litter and bird cages, avoid sick contacts. Offer nutrition support, mouth care, and nonjudgmental support for stigma and depression.","Reassess: Track SpO₂, RR and temperature, weight, viral load (should become undetectable) and CD4 (should rise), side effects, adherence, and whether cost and support barriers were solved before discharge."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["HIV is a retrovirus that infects and destroys CD4+ T cells (helper T cells), the coordinators of immunity. It spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk (sex, shared needles, birth or breastfeeding, rarely needlesticks), not saliva, tears, sweat, hugging or sharing dishes. Stages: acute HIV infection (flu-like illness 2–4 weeks after infection; very high viral load and very infectious), chronic HIV (few symptoms for years), and AIDS: CD4 below 200 cells/mm³ or an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection or cancer. Look for weight loss, thrush, fever, diarrhea, night sweats, cough and changes in thinking. CD4 140, off treatment, with dry cough, fever, exertional hypoxemia, diffuse infiltrates and thrush = AIDS with probable Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP).","Priority: impaired gas exchange (PJP). Then risk for infection (other opportunistic infections), imbalanced nutrition, activity intolerance, and the barriers that stopped his treatment (cost, stigma, depression). Oxygen, rest periods and position for breathing; treat PJP (TMP-SMX, with corticosteroids for severe hypoxemia). Use Standard Precautions (HIV needs no special isolation). Restart combination antiretroviral therapy (cART): several drugs from different classes, taken every day on schedule to prevent resistance. Prevent infection: hand hygiene, food safety (no raw eggs, meat or seafood; wash produce), avoid cat litter and bird cages, avoid sick contacts. Offer nutrition support, mouth care, and nonjudgmental support for stigma and depression.","Track SpO₂, RR and temperature, weight, viral load (should become undetectable) and CD4 (should rise), side effects, adherence, and whether cost and support barriers were solved before discharge."],"requires":[],"terms":["CD4 count and viral load","Opportunistic infection"],"termDefinitions":{"CD4 count and viral load":"CD4 is normally about 500–1,500 cells/mm³; viral load is the amount of HIV RNA in the blood. The CD4 count shows how much immunity is left; viral load shows how fast HIV is multiplying and how well treatment works. Treatment aims for an undetectable viral load; falling CD4 means rising risk of opportunistic infections.","Opportunistic infection":"PJP pneumonia, thrush, CMV retinitis (blurred vision, floaters), toxoplasmosis (headache, confusion, seizures), MAC, cryptococcal meningitis, TB. Organisms a healthy immune system controls cause disease when CD4 cells are few. Prophylaxis by CD4 level: TMP-SMX below 200 for PJP (it also covers toxoplasmosis below about 100)."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-hiv-1","sourceId":"q1-hiv-1","q":"Which body fluids can transmit HIV? Select all that apply.","o":["Blood","Semen","Vaginal fluid","Breast milk","Saliva","Tears"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. HIV spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, not saliva, tears or sweat.","Supported. HIV spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, not saliva, tears or sweat.","Supported. HIV spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, not saliva, tears or sweat.","Supported. HIV spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, not saliva, tears or sweat.","Not supported for this question. HIV spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, not saliva, tears or sweat.","Not supported for this question. HIV spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, not saliva, tears or sweat."],"phys":"HIV spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk, not saliva, tears or sweat.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-hiv-3","sourceId":"q1-hiv-3","q":"A nurse sustains a needlestick from a patient with HIV. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Finish the shift and report it the next day","Wash the site with soap and water and report immediately","Squeeze the wound and apply bleach","Wait for the patient’s viral load result"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Wash, report immediately, and start PEP as soon as possible (within 72 hours, ideally within hours).","Supported. Wash, report immediately, and start PEP as soon as possible (within 72 hours, ideally within hours).","Not supported for this question. Wash, report immediately, and start PEP as soon as possible (within 72 hours, ideally within hours).","Not supported for this question. Wash, report immediately, and start PEP as soon as possible (within 72 hours, ideally within hours)."],"phys":"Wash, report immediately, and start PEP as soon as possible (within 72 hours, ideally within hours).","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-hiv-4","sourceId":"q1-hiv-4","q":"Which statement about HIV prevention is correct?","o":["PEP must be started within 72 hours and taken for 28 days","PrEP is taken only after exposure","An undetectable viral load still transmits HIV sexually","Condoms are unnecessary for people on PrEP for other infections"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. PEP: within 72 hours, for 28 days. PrEP is before exposure. Undetectable = untransmittable.","Not supported for this question. PEP: within 72 hours, for 28 days. PrEP is before exposure. Undetectable = untransmittable.","Not supported for this question. PEP: within 72 hours, for 28 days. PrEP is before exposure. Undetectable = untransmittable.","Not supported for this question. PEP: within 72 hours, for 28 days. PrEP is before exposure. Undetectable = untransmittable."],"phys":"PEP: within 72 hours, for 28 days. PrEP is before exposure. Undetectable = untransmittable.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-hiv-5","sourceId":"q1-hiv-5","q":"A patient with a CD4 count of 150 cells/mm³ has a new dry cough and dyspnea on exertion. Which opportunistic infection does the nurse suspect, and which drug prevents it?","o":["Tuberculosis; isoniazid","Pneumocystis pneumonia; trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole","Candidiasis; nystatin","CMV retinitis; ganciclovir"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. PJP is the classic infection below CD4 200; TMP-SMX is the prophylaxis.","Supported. PJP is the classic infection below CD4 200; TMP-SMX is the prophylaxis.","Not supported for this question. PJP is the classic infection below CD4 200; TMP-SMX is the prophylaxis.","Not supported for this question. PJP is the classic infection below CD4 200; TMP-SMX is the prophylaxis."],"phys":"PJP is the classic infection below CD4 200; TMP-SMX is the prophylaxis.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-hiv-6","sourceId":"q1-hiv-6","q":"Why must antiretroviral therapy be taken every day on schedule?","o":["Missed doses cause hypoglycemia","Missed doses let HIV develop drug resistance","The drugs cure HIV if taken for a year","Skipping doses improves the CD4 count"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Low drug levels let the virus mutate and become resistant.","Supported. Low drug levels let the virus mutate and become resistant.","Not supported for this question. Low drug levels let the virus mutate and become resistant.","Not supported for this question. Low drug levels let the virus mutate and become resistant."],"phys":"Low drug levels let the virus mutate and become resistant.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-hiv-7","sourceId":"q1-hiv-7","q":"Which precautions does the nurse use for a hospitalized patient with HIV and no other infection?","o":["Airborne","Droplet","Contact","Standard"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. HIV is blood-borne: Standard Precautions only.","Not supported for this question. HIV is blood-borne: Standard Precautions only.","Not supported for this question. HIV is blood-borne: Standard Precautions only.","Supported. HIV is blood-borne: Standard Precautions only."],"phys":"HIV is blood-borne: Standard Precautions only.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-hiv"},{"id":"c-cdiff","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"Clostridioides difficile infection","patient":"78-year-old woman in a long-term care facility, admitted with a hip wound infection, now on day 6 of clindamycin. She also takes omeprazole. Today she has had 8 watery, foul-smelling stools, crampy lower abdominal pain and a temperature of 38.3 °C. HR 106, BP 98/60; her mouth is dry. WBC 18,000/mm³; creatinine up to 1.6 mg/dL.","notes":["c-cdiff"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 19; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Eight watery stools, cramping, fever and a WBC of 18,000 on day 6 of clindamycin in an older woman on a PPI, with low BP and rising creatinine = C. difficile infection with dehydration and acute kidney injury.","Priority: Priority: deficient fluid volume (diarrhea, low BP, rising creatinine) and infection (treat it and stop it spreading). Then risk for impaired skin integrity (perineal skin) and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium).","Act: Contact Precautions (gown and gloves on entering the room; private room or cohorting; dedicated equipment such as a stethoscope and BP cuff), soap-and-water hand hygiene, and bleach cleaning. Stop the triggering antibiotic if possible, as ordered. Give oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole is no longer first choice). Avoid antidiarrheals (loperamide): they keep toxins in the colon. Replace fluids and electrolytes (potassium). Perineal care and barrier cream. Watch for toxic megacolon. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) is used for recurrent infection.","Reassess: Count and describe stools, check I&O, daily weight, BP and HR, creatinine and potassium, temperature and WBC, abdominal girth and tenderness, and perineal skin.","Use contact precautions, appropriate gown/glove use, dedicated equipment and sporicidal environmental cleaning. Soap and water is important for visibly soiled hands and during C. difficile outbreaks. CDC still prefers alcohol-based hand rub in most routine clinical situations; follow the facility’s infection-prevention policy."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["C. difficile is a spore-forming bacterium. Antibiotics (especially clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins and broad-spectrum drugs) wipe out normal gut flora so C. diff can overgrow and release toxins that inflame the colon. Other risks: older age, hospital or long-term care stays, PPIs, a weak immune system. Cues: 3 or more watery stools in 24 hours, foul odor, abdominal cramps, fever, high WBC, dehydration. Severe disease: pseudomembranous colitis, toxic megacolon (a distended, tender abdomen with fewer stools), perforation, sepsis. Eight watery stools, cramping, fever and a WBC of 18,000 on day 6 of clindamycin in an older woman on a PPI, with low BP and rising creatinine = C. difficile infection with dehydration and acute kidney injury.","Priority: deficient fluid volume (diarrhea, low BP, rising creatinine) and infection (treat it and stop it spreading). Then risk for impaired skin integrity (perineal skin) and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium). Contact Precautions (gown and gloves on entering the room; private room or cohorting; dedicated equipment such as a stethoscope and BP cuff), soap-and-water hand hygiene, and bleach cleaning. Stop the triggering antibiotic if possible, as ordered. Give oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole is no longer first choice). Avoid antidiarrheals (loperamide): they keep toxins in the colon. Replace fluids and electrolytes (potassium). Perineal care and barrier cream. Watch for toxic megacolon. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) is used for recurrent infection.","Count and describe stools, check I&O, daily weight, BP and HR, creatinine and potassium, temperature and WBC, abdominal girth and tenderness, and perineal skin."],"requires":[],"terms":["Spores","Stool testing"],"termDefinitions":{"Spores":"They survive for months on bed rails, commodes, call lights and hands. Spores resist drying and alcohol. Wash hands with soap and water (alcohol hand rub doesn’t kill spores); clean with a sporicidal (bleach) disinfectant; use dedicated equipment.","Stool testing":"Only unformed (liquid) stool is tested. Many people carry C. diff without disease; testing formed stool finds carriers, not infection. Send the sample promptly; don’t test for cure after treatment."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-cd-2","sourceId":"q1-cd-2","q":"Which factors increase the risk for C. difficile infection? Select all that apply.","o":["Recent clindamycin","Proton pump inhibitor use","Age over 65","Long-term care residence","Young adult with no antibiotics"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. Antibiotics, PPIs, older age and health care settings raise the risk.","Supported. Antibiotics, PPIs, older age and health care settings raise the risk.","Supported. Antibiotics, PPIs, older age and health care settings raise the risk.","Supported. Antibiotics, PPIs, older age and health care settings raise the risk.","Not supported for this question. Antibiotics, PPIs, older age and health care settings raise the risk."],"phys":"Antibiotics, PPIs, older age and health care settings raise the risk.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-cd-3","sourceId":"q1-cd-3","q":"A patient with C. difficile asks for loperamide to stop the diarrhea. The nurse’s best response:","o":["“I’ll get it right away.”","“Antidiarrheals can keep the toxin in your bowel and make the infection worse; let’s talk to your provider.”","“You can take it after each loose stool.”","“Drink less fluid so you have fewer stools.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Antidiarrheals are avoided: they can trap toxins and raise the risk of toxic megacolon.","Supported. Antidiarrheals are avoided: they can trap toxins and raise the risk of toxic megacolon.","Not supported for this question. Antidiarrheals are avoided: they can trap toxins and raise the risk of toxic megacolon.","Not supported for this question. Antidiarrheals are avoided: they can trap toxins and raise the risk of toxic megacolon."],"phys":"Antidiarrheals are avoided: they can trap toxins and raise the risk of toxic megacolon.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-cd-4","sourceId":"q1-cd-4","q":"Which treatment does the nurse anticipate for a first episode of C. difficile infection?","o":["IV vancomycin","Oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin","Clindamycin","Fecal microbiota transplantation right away"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Oral vancomycin (IV doesn’t reach the colon) or fidaxomicin. FMT is for recurrent infection.","Supported. Oral vancomycin (IV doesn’t reach the colon) or fidaxomicin. FMT is for recurrent infection.","Not supported for this question. Oral vancomycin (IV doesn’t reach the colon) or fidaxomicin. FMT is for recurrent infection.","Not supported for this question. Oral vancomycin (IV doesn’t reach the colon) or fidaxomicin. FMT is for recurrent infection."],"phys":"Oral vancomycin (IV doesn’t reach the colon) or fidaxomicin. FMT is for recurrent infection.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Use contact precautions, appropriate gown/glove use, dedicated equipment and sporicidal environmental cleaning. Soap and water is important for visibly soiled hands and during C. difficile outbreaks. CDC still prefers alcohol-based hand rub in most routine clinical situations; follow the facility’s infection-prevention policy.","studio":"c-cdiff"},{"id":"c-mrsa","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"MRSA wound infection progressing to sepsis","patient":"64-year-old man, post-op day 4 after a colectomy, with diabetes. His incision is red, hot and swollen, with thick yellow drainage; a wound culture grew MRSA. This evening his temperature is 39.1 °C, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, and he is newly confused. WBC 19,500 with 14% bands; lactate 3.2 mmol/L.","notes":["c-mrsa"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 19; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: An MRSA-infected wound plus fever, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, new confusion, a left shift and lactate 3.2 = sepsis from the wound, at risk of septic shock.","Priority: Priority: infection and ineffective tissue perfusion (sepsis). Then risk for injury (confusion), impaired tissue integrity at the wound, and preventing spread to others.","Act: Sepsis bundle: blood cultures before antibiotics, then IV antibiotics within 1 hour (vancomycin for MRSA), 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate of 4 or more, repeat lactate, vasopressors if BP doesn’t respond. Monitor VS and urine output often. Contact Precautions (gown and gloves, dedicated equipment). Wound care as ordered (the surgeon may open and drain it). Vancomycin: monitor trough levels and kidney function; infuse slowly (at least 60 minutes) to prevent infusion reaction (flushing, itching, low BP).","Reassess: Track MAP, HR, RR, temperature, mental status, urine output and repeat lactate; review culture and sensitivity results; trend WBC and the wound’s appearance and drainage; check vancomycin levels and creatinine."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Know the signs of a local infection (redness, warmth, swelling, pain, purulent drainage, loss of function) and the signs it has become systemic (fever or hypothermia, chills, tachycardia, tachypnea, low BP, new confusion, low urine output, mottled skin, high lactate). MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) resists most beta-lactam antibiotics. Older adults may have no fever; confusion or a fall can be the first sign. An MRSA-infected wound plus fever, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, new confusion, a left shift and lactate 3.2 = sepsis from the wound, at risk of septic shock.","Priority: infection and ineffective tissue perfusion (sepsis). Then risk for injury (confusion), impaired tissue integrity at the wound, and preventing spread to others. Sepsis bundle: blood cultures before antibiotics, then IV antibiotics within 1 hour (vancomycin for MRSA), 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate of 4 or more, repeat lactate, vasopressors if BP doesn’t respond. Monitor VS and urine output often. Contact Precautions (gown and gloves, dedicated equipment). Wound care as ordered (the surgeon may open and drain it). Vancomycin: monitor trough levels and kidney function; infuse slowly (at least 60 minutes) to prevent infusion reaction (flushing, itching, low BP).","Track MAP, HR, RR, temperature, mental status, urine output and repeat lactate; review culture and sensitivity results; trend WBC and the wound’s appearance and drainage; check vancomycin levels and creatinine."],"requires":[],"terms":["Left shift","Lactate"],"termDefinitions":{"Left shift":"More bands (immature neutrophils) than normal on the differential (normal bands about 0–5%). The marrow releases young neutrophils early when the body is fighting a serious bacterial infection. Supports acute bacterial infection; trend it with the WBC.","Lactate":"Above 2 mmol/L is abnormal; 4 or more means severe hypoperfusion. Poorly perfused tissues switch to anaerobic metabolism. Give fluids; repeat the lactate to see if perfusion improves."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-mr-2","sourceId":"q1-mr-2","q":"Which precautions are used for a patient with MRSA in a draining wound?","o":["Airborne","Droplet","Contact","Neutropenic"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. MRSA spreads by contact: gown, gloves and dedicated equipment.","Not supported for this question. MRSA spreads by contact: gown, gloves and dedicated equipment.","Supported. MRSA spreads by contact: gown, gloves and dedicated equipment.","Not supported for this question. MRSA spreads by contact: gown, gloves and dedicated equipment."],"phys":"MRSA spreads by contact: gown, gloves and dedicated equipment.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-mr-3","sourceId":"q1-mr-3","q":"IV vancomycin is ordered for MRSA. Which nursing actions are correct? Select all that apply.","o":["Infuse over at least 60 minutes","Monitor trough levels as ordered","Monitor kidney function","Give it as a rapid IV push","Watch for flushing and itching during the infusion"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Infusing vancomycin too fast causes flushing, itching and low BP; it can harm the kidneys, so levels and creatinine are monitored.","Supported. Infusing vancomycin too fast causes flushing, itching and low BP; it can harm the kidneys, so levels and creatinine are monitored.","Supported. Infusing vancomycin too fast causes flushing, itching and low BP; it can harm the kidneys, so levels and creatinine are monitored.","Not supported for this question. Infusing vancomycin too fast causes flushing, itching and low BP; it can harm the kidneys, so levels and creatinine are monitored.","Supported. Infusing vancomycin too fast causes flushing, itching and low BP; it can harm the kidneys, so levels and creatinine are monitored."],"phys":"Infusing vancomycin too fast causes flushing, itching and low BP; it can harm the kidneys, so levels and creatinine are monitored.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-mrsa"},{"id":"c-postresp","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Post-op opioid-induced respiratory depression","patient":"68-year-old man, 2 hours after transfer from PACU following a total knee replacement. He has obstructive sleep apnea (doesn’t use his CPAP at home) and a hydromorphone PCA, and his wife has been pressing the button “so he doesn’t hurt.” He is snoring loudly and only rouses when you shake his shoulder and call his name, then drifts off mid-sentence. RR 8, shallow; SpO₂ 86% on 2 L; HR 58; BP 104/62.","notes":["c-postresp"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 9; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Deep sedation (POSS 4), RR 8, SpO₂ 86% and a slow heart rate in an older man with untreated sleep apnea on a PCA pushed by someone else = opioid-induced respiratory depression with hypoventilation and hypoxemia. This can progress to respiratory arrest.","Priority: Priority: ineffective breathing pattern and impaired gas exchange (airway and breathing come before pain). Then risk for injury from opioid excess, and later acute pain once he is safe.","Act: Stop the opioid (pause the PCA). Stimulate him (call his name loudly, sternal rub), tell him to take deep breaths, raise the head of the bed or turn him on his side, open the airway and increase oxygen. Call the rapid response team. Give naloxone per protocol (dilute and give slowly, titrated to breathing, not to full wakefulness, so you don’t cause severe pain or withdrawal). Stay with him and monitor continuously (SpO₂ and capnography).","Reassess: Recheck his sedation, RR and depth, SpO₂ and end-tidal CO₂ every few minutes until stable, then closely for the next hours (naloxone wears off). Confirm the new pain plan keeps him at POSS 1–2 with tolerable pain.","Opioid-related shallow or slow breathing with reduced alertness needs immediate assessment, emergency escalation, breathing support and naloxone as indicated by protocol. Supplemental oxygen alone does not correct inadequate ventilation."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["In the first 24 hours after surgery the biggest danger is the airway and breathing. Watch sedation level first: excess sleepiness comes before the respiratory rate drops. Look for snoring or noisy breathing, slow or shallow breaths, falling SpO₂ or rising end-tidal CO₂, pinpoint pupils, and who is pressing the PCA button. Know who is at risk: older adults, obstructive sleep apnea, obesity, opioid-naive patients, lung or kidney disease, and anyone also on sedatives. Deep sedation (POSS 4), RR 8, SpO₂ 86% and a slow heart rate in an older man with untreated sleep apnea on a PCA pushed by someone else = opioid-induced respiratory depression with hypoventilation and hypoxemia. This can progress to respiratory arrest.","Priority: ineffective breathing pattern and impaired gas exchange (airway and breathing come before pain). Then risk for injury from opioid excess, and later acute pain once he is safe. Stop the opioid (pause the PCA). Stimulate him (call his name loudly, sternal rub), tell him to take deep breaths, raise the head of the bed or turn him on his side, open the airway and increase oxygen. Call the rapid response team. Give naloxone per protocol (dilute and give slowly, titrated to breathing, not to full wakefulness, so you don’t cause severe pain or withdrawal). Stay with him and monitor continuously (SpO₂ and capnography).","Recheck his sedation, RR and depth, SpO₂ and end-tidal CO₂ every few minutes until stable, then closely for the next hours (naloxone wears off). Confirm the new pain plan keeps him at POSS 1–2 with tolerable pain."],"requires":[],"terms":["Sedation scale (POSS)","PCA by proxy","Snoring after surgery","naloxone"],"termDefinitions":{"Sedation scale (POSS)":"1 awake and alert; 2 slightly drowsy, easily roused; 3 frequently drowsy, drifts off mid-conversation; 4 somnolent, minimal or no response to touch and voice; S = normal sleep, easy to wake. Opioids blunt the brain’s drive to breathe. Sleepiness shows up first; respiratory depression follows. At 3: hold the opioid, cut the dose and watch closely. At 4: stop the opioid, stimulate, call for help and give naloxone.","PCA by proxy":"A family member or a nurse not following protocol presses the button for the patient. A PCA is safe because a sedated patient can’t press the button, so he gets no more drug. Anyone else pressing it removes that safety. Teach: only the patient presses the button.","Snoring after surgery":"Loud snoring, pauses, obstructive breathing. Relaxed throat muscles from anesthesia and opioids, plus sleep apnea, block the upper airway. Do not assume he is resting comfortably. Assess his arousal and SpO₂; use his CPAP; position him on his side or with the head up."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-post-2","sourceId":"q1-post-2","q":"The nurse gave naloxone, and the patient is now awake with RR 14. Why must the nurse keep monitoring closely?","o":["Naloxone causes hypertension for 24 hours","Naloxone may wear off before the opioid, so sedation can return","The patient may become allergic","Naloxone causes urinary retention"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Naloxone lasts about 30–90 minutes; many opioids last longer, so respiratory depression can return.","Supported. Naloxone lasts about 30–90 minutes; many opioids last longer, so respiratory depression can return.","Not supported for this question. Naloxone lasts about 30–90 minutes; many opioids last longer, so respiratory depression can return.","Not supported for this question. Naloxone lasts about 30–90 minutes; many opioids last longer, so respiratory depression can return."],"phys":"Naloxone lasts about 30–90 minutes; many opioids last longer, so respiratory depression can return.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-post-3","sourceId":"q1-post-3","q":"A spouse is pressing the PCA button for a sleeping patient “so he won’t wake up in pain.” What is the nurse’s best response?","o":["“That’s thoughtful; keep it up.”","“Only he should press the button. If he is too sleepy to press it, he doesn’t need more medicine right now.”","“Press it only every hour.”","“I’ll turn the dose up so you don’t have to.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. The PCA’s safety relies on a sedated patient not pressing the button. PCA by proxy causes overdose.","Supported. The PCA’s safety relies on a sedated patient not pressing the button. PCA by proxy causes overdose.","Not supported for this question. The PCA’s safety relies on a sedated patient not pressing the button. PCA by proxy causes overdose.","Not supported for this question. The PCA’s safety relies on a sedated patient not pressing the button. PCA by proxy causes overdose."],"phys":"The PCA’s safety relies on a sedated patient not pressing the button. PCA by proxy causes overdose.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Opioid-related shallow or slow breathing with reduced alertness needs immediate assessment, emergency escalation, breathing support and naloxone as indicated by protocol. Supplemental oxygen alone does not correct inadequate ventilation.","studio":"c-postresp"},{"id":"c-malhyp","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Malignant hyperthermia","patient":"24-year-old man having an ACL repair under general anesthesia with sevoflurane; he received succinylcholine for intubation. Twenty minutes in, his end-tidal CO₂ rises from 38 to 62 mm Hg despite more ventilation, HR is 142 and irregular, his jaw and chest are rigid, and his skin is mottled. Temperature is 37.9 °C and climbing. His mother mentioned “a cousin who almost died during surgery.”","notes":["c-malhyp"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 9; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Rising end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia with ectopy, rigidity and climbing temperature after sevoflurane + succinylcholine, with a family history = malignant hyperthermia crisis. It is life-threatening within minutes.","Priority: Priority: hyperthermia and the hypermetabolic state (stop the trigger and give dantrolene), together with impaired gas exchange and risk for decreased cardiac output (hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias). Then risk for acute kidney injury.","Act: Call for help and the MH cart. Stop the triggering agent; hyperventilate with 100% oxygen. Give dantrolene IV (it stops the calcium release; repeat until signs resolve). Cool the patient (ice packs to the groin and armpits, cold IV fluids, cooling blanket). Treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias (insulin and glucose, calcium; avoid calcium channel blockers with dantrolene). Monitor arterial blood gases, potassium, CK, urine output and core temperature. Transfer to the ICU: MH can recur in the next 24–72 hours.","Reassess: Watch end-tidal CO₂, HR and rhythm, temperature, potassium and blood gases, CK and myoglobin, and the color and amount of urine. Watch for recurrence in the ICU for at least 24 hours.","Suspected malignant hyperthermia requires immediate help, discontinuing triggering agents, 100% oxygen and rapid IV dantrolene under the crisis protocol. Do not wait for fever to begin the response."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Malignant hyperthermia (MH) is a rare, inherited muscle disorder: certain inhaled (volatile) anesthetics and succinylcholine make muscle cells release calcium uncontrollably, so the muscles contract and burn energy. Earliest cues: an unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia and dysrhythmias, muscle rigidity (the jaw, the masseter, first), tachypnea, and mottling. High fever is a late sign. It can start in the OR or hours later in PACU. Ask every patient about personal or family problems with anesthesia. Rising end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia with ectopy, rigidity and climbing temperature after sevoflurane + succinylcholine, with a family history = malignant hyperthermia crisis. It is life-threatening within minutes.","Priority: hyperthermia and the hypermetabolic state (stop the trigger and give dantrolene), together with impaired gas exchange and risk for decreased cardiac output (hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias). Then risk for acute kidney injury. Call for help and the MH cart. Stop the triggering agent; hyperventilate with 100% oxygen. Give dantrolene IV (it stops the calcium release; repeat until signs resolve). Cool the patient (ice packs to the groin and armpits, cold IV fluids, cooling blanket). Treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias (insulin and glucose, calcium; avoid calcium channel blockers with dantrolene). Monitor arterial blood gases, potassium, CK, urine output and core temperature. Transfer to the ICU: MH can recur in the next 24–72 hours.","Watch end-tidal CO₂, HR and rhythm, temperature, potassium and blood gases, CK and myoglobin, and the color and amount of urine. Watch for recurrence in the ICU for at least 24 hours."],"requires":[],"terms":["Rising end-tidal CO₂","Masseter (jaw) rigidity","dantrolene"],"termDefinitions":{"Rising end-tidal CO₂":"The capnography number climbs even though the patient is being ventilated faster. Hypermetabolic muscle produces huge amounts of CO₂. Often the first sign. Don’t wait for the temperature.","Masseter (jaw) rigidity":"The jaw won’t open after succinylcholine. Sustained muscle contraction from uncontrolled calcium release. A warning; general muscle rigidity follows."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-in-3","sourceId":"q1-in-3","q":"Malignant hyperthermia is suspected. Which drug should the nurse prepare?","o":["Naloxone","Dantrolene","Succinylcholine","Flumazenil"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Dantrolene stops the calcium release in muscle. Succinylcholine is a trigger.","Supported. Dantrolene stops the calcium release in muscle. Succinylcholine is a trigger.","Not supported for this question. Dantrolene stops the calcium release in muscle. Succinylcholine is a trigger.","Not supported for this question. Dantrolene stops the calcium release in muscle. Succinylcholine is a trigger."],"phys":"Dantrolene stops the calcium release in muscle. Succinylcholine is a trigger.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-in-4","sourceId":"q1-in-4","q":"Which actions are part of treating a malignant hyperthermia crisis? Select all that apply.","o":["Stop the volatile anesthetic","Give 100% oxygen","Give dantrolene IV","Apply ice packs and give cold IV fluids","Give succinylcholine to relax the jaw","Monitor potassium and the cardiac rhythm"],"a":[0,1,2,3,5],"r":["Supported. Stop the trigger, oxygenate, dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias. Succinylcholine makes it worse.","Supported. Stop the trigger, oxygenate, dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias. Succinylcholine makes it worse.","Supported. Stop the trigger, oxygenate, dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias. Succinylcholine makes it worse.","Supported. Stop the trigger, oxygenate, dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias. Succinylcholine makes it worse.","Not supported for this question. Stop the trigger, oxygenate, dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias. Succinylcholine makes it worse.","Supported. Stop the trigger, oxygenate, dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias. Succinylcholine makes it worse."],"phys":"Stop the trigger, oxygenate, dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias. Succinylcholine makes it worse.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Suspected malignant hyperthermia requires immediate help, discontinuing triggering agents, 100% oxygen and rapid IV dantrolene under the crisis protocol. Do not wait for fever to begin the response.","studio":"c-malhyp"},{"id":"c-evisc","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Wound dehiscence and evisceration","patient":"61-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes, post-op day 6 after an open bowel resection. His incision had pink drainage yesterday. During a coughing fit he says, “Something just popped.” The dressing is soaked with serosanguineous fluid, the edges of the incision have separated, and loops of bowel are visible. He is pale and anxious; HR 112, BP 108/70.","notes":["c-evisc"],"source":"Ignatavicius chapter 9; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: An open abdominal incision with bowel exposed after coughing on post-op day 6, in a man with obesity and diabetes, plus tachycardia = evisceration, a surgical emergency (risk of infection, bowel injury and shock).","Priority: Priority: protect the exposed organs (impaired tissue integrity and risk for infection), and watch for hypovolemic shock. Then anxiety (stay with him).","Act: Stay with him and call for help (the surgeon or rapid response). Cover the wound with sterile dressings moistened with warm sterile normal saline (keep them moist). Do not try to push organs back in. Position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent (or hips flexed) to relax the abdominal muscles. Keep him NPO, take vital signs often, watch for shock, and prepare for surgery. Stay calm and reassure him.","Reassess: Check that the dressing stays moist, watch vital signs and urine output for shock, and assess his anxiety until he goes to surgery. Afterward, watch the wound for infection and teach prevention."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Dehiscence = the wound edges separate; evisceration = organs (usually bowel) push out through the open wound. It usually happens about 5–10 days after surgery, often after straining, coughing, sneezing or vomiting. Cues: a sudden increase in serosanguineous (pink, watery) drainage, the patient feeling something “pop” or “give way,” separated edges, visible tissue. Risk factors: obesity, diabetes, poor nutrition (low protein), smoking, steroids, wound infection, older age, abdominal distension. An open abdominal incision with bowel exposed after coughing on post-op day 6, in a man with obesity and diabetes, plus tachycardia = evisceration, a surgical emergency (risk of infection, bowel injury and shock).","Priority: protect the exposed organs (impaired tissue integrity and risk for infection), and watch for hypovolemic shock. Then anxiety (stay with him). Stay with him and call for help (the surgeon or rapid response). Cover the wound with sterile dressings moistened with warm sterile normal saline (keep them moist). Do not try to push organs back in. Position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent (or hips flexed) to relax the abdominal muscles. Keep him NPO, take vital signs often, watch for shock, and prepare for surgery. Stay calm and reassure him.","Check that the dressing stays moist, watch vital signs and urine output for shock, and assess his anxiety until he goes to surgery. Afterward, watch the wound for infection and teach prevention."],"requires":[],"terms":["Serosanguineous drainage"],"termDefinitions":{"Serosanguineous drainage":"Pink, watery fluid on the dressing. Plasma mixed with a little blood; a sudden gush can mean the deep layers are separating. Look at the incision and tell the surgeon about any sudden increase."},"questions":[{"id":"q1-ev-2","sourceId":"q1-ev-2","q":"Which patient is at greatest risk for wound dehiscence?","o":["A 30-year-old after laparoscopic appendectomy","A 66-year-old with obesity, diabetes and low albumin after open abdominal surgery","A 40-year-old after a hand laceration repair","A 22-year-old athlete after knee arthroscopy"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Obesity, diabetes and poor nutrition (low protein) slow healing and strain the incision.","Supported. Obesity, diabetes and poor nutrition (low protein) slow healing and strain the incision.","Not supported for this question. Obesity, diabetes and poor nutrition (low protein) slow healing and strain the incision.","Not supported for this question. Obesity, diabetes and poor nutrition (low protein) slow healing and strain the incision."],"phys":"Obesity, diabetes and poor nutrition (low protein) slow healing and strain the incision.","ex":[]},{"id":"q1-ev-3","sourceId":"q1-ev-3","q":"Which teaching helps prevent dehiscence?","o":["Splint the incision with a pillow when coughing","Lift heavy objects to build strength","Avoid protein to reduce swelling","Take deep breaths only once a day"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Splinting supports the incision against the pressure of coughing; protein supports healing.","Not supported for this question. Splinting supports the incision against the pressure of coughing; protein supports healing.","Not supported for this question. Splinting supports the incision against the pressure of coughing; protein supports healing.","Not supported for this question. Splinting supports the incision against the pressure of coughing; protein supports healing."],"phys":"Splinting supports the incision against the pressure of coughing; protein supports healing.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-evisc"}],"studios":{"version":1,"mapPage":"Exam 1 Map.html","learnPage":"Exam 1 Learn.html","labs":[{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Before surgery (preoperative)","cards":[{"title":"Assessment: who is at higher risk","text":"Older adults (less reserve; delirium, falls, skin injury), obesity, obstructive sleep apnea (screen with STOP-BANG), smoking (stop at least 4–8 weeks before if possible), diabetes, heart, lung, kidney or liver disease, malnutrition, alcohol or substance use (withdrawal). · Personal or family history of problems with anesthesia (malignant hyperthermia). · Allergies: drugs, latex, tape, iodine or chlorhexidine."},{"title":"Medications and supplements","text":"Ask about all drugs, OTC products and herbs. Garlic, ginkgo, ginseng, fish oil, vitamin E and St. John’s wort can raise bleeding risk or interact with anesthesia; usually stopped about 1–2 weeks before. · Anticoagulants and antiplatelets (warfarin, DOACs, clopidogrel, aspirin) are often held days before, exactly as the surgeon or prescriber directs. · Diabetes drugs are adjusted (insulin dose often reduced; some oral drugs held); beta blockers are usually continued."},{"title":"Preparing the patient","text":"Chlorhexidine bathing or wipes as ordered; remove hair only if needed, with clippers (never razors: nicks let bacteria in). · Bowel prep for some bowel surgery; IV access; preop antibiotics usually within 60 minutes before the incision. · Remove jewelry, dentures, glasses, hearing aids (often kept until the OR), and makeup or nail polish as policy requires; patient voids before going."}],"caption":"Assessment, consent, NPO, medications, skin prep and the teaching that prevents complications. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Who explains the surgery and obtains consent?","cue":"Assessment, consent, NPO, medications, skin prep and the teaching that prevents complications.","rule":"The surgeon. The nurse witnesses the signature and notifies the surgeon if the patient doesn’t understand.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-preop","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Before surgery (preoperative)","notes":["t-preop"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The preoperative phase runs from the decision to operate until the patient enters the OR. The nurse’s jobs: find the risks that could cause complications, make sure the patient is informed and safely prepared, and teach the exercises that prevent post-op problems. Many surgeries are now same-day, so teaching often happens in a short visit.","Assessment: who is at higher risk: Older adults (less reserve; delirium, falls, skin injury), obesity, obstructive sleep apnea (screen with STOP-BANG), smoking (stop at least 4–8 weeks before if possible), diabetes, heart, lung, kidney or liver disease, malnutrition, alcohol or substance use (withdrawal).; Personal or family history of problems with anesthesia (malignant hyperthermia).; Allergies: drugs, latex, tape, iodine or chlorhexidine.; Labs: CBC, electrolytes, glucose, coagulation (PT/INR, aPTT), kidney function, type and screen, pregnancy test for those who could be pregnant; ECG and chest x-ray as needed.","Medications and supplements: Ask about all drugs, OTC products and herbs. Garlic, ginkgo, ginseng, fish oil, vitamin E and St. John’s wort can raise bleeding risk or interact with anesthesia; usually stopped about 1–2 weeks before.; Anticoagulants and antiplatelets (warfarin, DOACs, clopidogrel, aspirin) are often held days before, exactly as the surgeon or prescriber directs.; Diabetes drugs are adjusted (insulin dose often reduced; some oral drugs held); beta blockers are usually continued.; Long-term corticosteroids may need a stress dose (the adrenal glands can’t respond to surgery).","Fasting intervals such as clear liquids up to 2 hours before anesthesia apply to selected healthy patients having elective procedures. Confirm the individual anesthesia instructions and aspiration risk; a general interval is not permission to give intake against the patient’s orders."],"terms":["Informed consent","NPO","Advance directive"],"termDefinitions":{"Informed consent":"The patient signs a form after the surgeon explains the procedure, risks, benefits and alternatives. Explaining and getting consent is the surgeon’s legal responsibility; the patient must be an adult (or emancipated), competent, and not sedated. The nurse witnesses the signature, checks that the patient understands, and if he doesn’t, stops and notifies the surgeon (don’t explain the surgery yourself). Give no preop sedatives until the form is signed.","NPO":"Nothing by mouth for a set time before anesthesia. Anesthesia relaxes the protective reflexes, so stomach contents can be aspirated into the lungs. Usual guidance: clear liquids up to 2 hours before, a light meal 6 hours, fatty or fried foods 8 hours. Some protocols give a carbohydrate drink 2 hours before (enhanced recovery).","Advance directive":"A living will or durable power of attorney for health care. Surgery carries risks; the patient’s wishes must be known. Ask whether one exists and place a copy in the chart."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-preop","title":"Before surgery (preoperative)","blocks":[{"title":"Assessment: who is at higher risk","lines":["Older adults (less reserve; delirium, falls, skin injury), obesity, obstructive sleep apnea (screen with STOP-BANG), smoking (stop at least 4–8 weeks before if possible), diabetes, heart, lung, kidney or liver disease, malnutrition, alcohol or substance use (withdrawal).","Personal or family history of problems with anesthesia (malignant hyperthermia).","Allergies: drugs, latex, tape, iodine or chlorhexidine.","Labs: CBC, electrolytes, glucose, coagulation (PT/INR, aPTT), kidney function, type and screen, pregnancy test for those who could be pregnant; ECG and chest x-ray as needed."]},{"title":"Medications and supplements","lines":["Ask about all drugs, OTC products and herbs. Garlic, ginkgo, ginseng, fish oil, vitamin E and St. John’s wort can raise bleeding risk or interact with anesthesia; usually stopped about 1–2 weeks before.","Anticoagulants and antiplatelets (warfarin, DOACs, clopidogrel, aspirin) are often held days before, exactly as the surgeon or prescriber directs.","Diabetes drugs are adjusted (insulin dose often reduced; some oral drugs held); beta blockers are usually continued.","Long-term corticosteroids may need a stress dose (the adrenal glands can’t respond to surgery)."]},{"title":"Preparing the patient","lines":["Chlorhexidine bathing or wipes as ordered; remove hair only if needed, with clippers (never razors: nicks let bacteria in).","Bowel prep for some bowel surgery; IV access; preop antibiotics usually within 60 minutes before the incision.","Remove jewelry, dentures, glasses, hearing aids (often kept until the OR), and makeup or nail polish as policy requires; patient voids before going.","Preop checklist: ID band, allergy band, consent, site marking, H&P, labs, NPO status, meds given, VS."]},{"title":"Teaching that prevents complications","lines":["Incentive spirometry: sit up, breathe out, seal the lips around the mouthpiece, inhale slowly and deeply, hold 3–5 seconds; about 10 times an hour while awake.","Deep breathing and coughing with the incision splinted by a pillow.","Leg exercises, early walking, sequential compression devices or stockings to prevent VTE.","What pain control to expect (pain scale, PCA, multimodal plan), drains and tubes they will wake up with, and when they may eat."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Fasting intervals such as clear liquids up to 2 hours before anesthesia apply to selected healthy patients having elective procedures. Confirm the individual anesthesia instructions and aspiration risk; a general interval is not permission to give intake against the patient’s orders.","sources":[{"title":"ASA: fasting guidance for healthy patients having elective procedures","url":"https://doi.org/10.1097/ALN.0000000000004381"}],"steps":[{"id":"t-preop:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient about to sign a surgical consent says, “I’m not really sure what they’re going to remove.” What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Explain the procedure using the surgeon’s notes","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Explaining the surgery and getting consent is the surgeon’s job. The nurse witnesses the signature and stops to call the surgeon when the patient doesn’t understand."},{"id":"o1","text":"Have the patient sign, since the surgeon already talked with him","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Explaining the surgery and getting consent is the surgeon’s job. The nurse witnesses the signature and stops to call the surgeon when the patient doesn’t understand."},{"id":"o2","text":"Notify the surgeon that the patient needs more information before signing","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Explaining the surgery and getting consent is the surgeon’s job. The nurse witnesses the signature and stops to call the surgeon when the patient doesn’t understand."},{"id":"o3","text":"Ask a family member to explain it","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Explaining the surgery and getting consent is the surgeon’s job. The nurse witnesses the signature and stops to call the surgeon when the patient doesn’t understand."}]},{"id":"t-preop:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Who explains the surgery and obtains consent?","answer":"The surgeon. The nurse witnesses the signature and notifies the surgeon if the patient doesn’t understand.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-preop:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"How long before surgery may an adult drink clear liquids?","answer":"Usually up to 2 hours before.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"During surgery (intraoperative)","cards":[{"title":"Types of anesthesia","text":"General: unconsciousness, amnesia, pain relief and muscle relaxation (inhaled and IV drugs); airway is managed with a tube or supraglottic device. · Moderate (procedural) sedation: the patient breathes on his own and responds to voice; needs continuous monitoring and a provider trained in airway rescue. · Regional: a spinal or epidural block, or a peripheral nerve block. Watch for hypotension and bradycardia (sympathetic block), urinary retention, and a post-dural puncture headache (worse sitting up)."},{"title":"Keeping the patient safe","text":"Positioning: pad bony prominences and protect nerves and skin; long cases risk pressure injuries and nerve damage. · Unplanned hypothermia (cold room, open body cavity, anesthesia): use warming devices and warmed fluids. Hypothermia raises infection, bleeding and cardiac risk. · Counts of sponges, sharps and instruments to prevent retained surgical items."},{"title":"Malignant hyperthermia (see the walk-through)","text":"A genetic reaction to volatile inhaled anesthetics and succinylcholine. · First signs: rising end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia, muscle (jaw) rigidity; fever comes late. · Treatment: stop the trigger, dantrolene, 100% oxygen, cooling, treat hyperkalemia."}],"caption":"The surgical team, the time-out, types of anesthesia, positioning and keeping the patient safe."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Who marks the surgical site?","cue":"The surgical team, the time-out, types of anesthesia, positioning and keeping the patient safe.","rule":"The surgeon (or the person doing the procedure), ideally with the patient awake and taking part.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-intraop","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"During surgery (intraoperative)","notes":["t-intraop"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The intraoperative phase runs from entering the OR to arriving in the PACU. The patient can’t protect himself, so the team’s focus is safety: the right patient, procedure and site, sterile technique, safe positioning, preventing retained items and hypothermia, and catching anesthesia complications such as malignant hyperthermia early.","Types of anesthesia: General: unconsciousness, amnesia, pain relief and muscle relaxation (inhaled and IV drugs); airway is managed with a tube or supraglottic device.; Moderate (procedural) sedation: the patient breathes on his own and responds to voice; needs continuous monitoring and a provider trained in airway rescue.; Regional: a spinal or epidural block, or a peripheral nerve block. Watch for hypotension and bradycardia (sympathetic block), urinary retention, and a post-dural puncture headache (worse sitting up).; Local: numbs a small area; watch for local anesthetic toxicity (ringing ears, metallic taste, perioral numbness, seizures, dysrhythmias).","Keeping the patient safe: Positioning: pad bony prominences and protect nerves and skin; long cases risk pressure injuries and nerve damage.; Unplanned hypothermia (cold room, open body cavity, anesthesia): use warming devices and warmed fluids. Hypothermia raises infection, bleeding and cardiac risk.; Counts of sponges, sharps and instruments to prevent retained surgical items.; Fire safety (oxygen + an ignition source + alcohol prep), electrosurgical grounding pads, and fall prevention on the narrow table."],"terms":["Time-out (Universal Protocol)","Circulating nurse vs scrub nurse","dantrolene"],"termDefinitions":{"Time-out (Universal Protocol)":"Right before the incision, the whole team stops and confirms the patient’s identity, the procedure, the site and side, consent, allergies and that needed equipment is present. Prevents wrong-site, wrong-procedure and wrong-patient surgery. Part of a 3-step protocol: preprocedure verification, site marking (by the surgeon, with the patient awake when possible), and the time-out. Anyone can stop the process if something doesn’t match.","Circulating nurse vs scrub nurse":"Circulator: an RN, not scrubbed, who manages the room. Scrub: sterile, hands instruments to the surgeon. The circulator can move freely between sterile and non-sterile areas. The circulator coordinates, advocates, documents, does counts with the scrub person, and monitors the sterile field."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-intraop","title":"During surgery (intraoperative)","blocks":[{"title":"Types of anesthesia","lines":["General: unconsciousness, amnesia, pain relief and muscle relaxation (inhaled and IV drugs); airway is managed with a tube or supraglottic device.","Moderate (procedural) sedation: the patient breathes on his own and responds to voice; needs continuous monitoring and a provider trained in airway rescue.","Regional: a spinal or epidural block, or a peripheral nerve block. Watch for hypotension and bradycardia (sympathetic block), urinary retention, and a post-dural puncture headache (worse sitting up).","Local: numbs a small area; watch for local anesthetic toxicity (ringing ears, metallic taste, perioral numbness, seizures, dysrhythmias)."]},{"title":"Keeping the patient safe","lines":["Positioning: pad bony prominences and protect nerves and skin; long cases risk pressure injuries and nerve damage.","Unplanned hypothermia (cold room, open body cavity, anesthesia): use warming devices and warmed fluids. Hypothermia raises infection, bleeding and cardiac risk.","Counts of sponges, sharps and instruments to prevent retained surgical items.","Fire safety (oxygen + an ignition source + alcohol prep), electrosurgical grounding pads, and fall prevention on the narrow table."]},{"title":"Malignant hyperthermia (see the walk-through)","lines":["A genetic reaction to volatile inhaled anesthetics and succinylcholine.","First signs: rising end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia, muscle (jaw) rigidity; fever comes late.","Treatment: stop the trigger, dantrolene, 100% oxygen, cooling, treat hyperkalemia."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-intraop:case","kind":"choice","scene":"During the time-out, the circulating nurse notices the consent says the left knee but the site marking is on the right. What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Let the surgeon decide after the incision","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Any team member can and must stop the process. The time-out exists to prevent wrong-site surgery."},{"id":"o1","text":"Speak up and stop the procedure until the discrepancy is resolved","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Any team member can and must stop the process. The time-out exists to prevent wrong-site surgery."},{"id":"o2","text":"Re-mark the left knee","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Any team member can and must stop the process. The time-out exists to prevent wrong-site surgery."},{"id":"o3","text":"Document the discrepancy after surgery","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Any team member can and must stop the process. The time-out exists to prevent wrong-site surgery."}]},{"id":"t-intraop:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Who marks the surgical site?","answer":"The surgeon (or the person doing the procedure), ideally with the patient awake and taking part.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-intraop:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What is the earliest sign of malignant hyperthermia?","answer":"An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂ (with tachycardia and rigidity).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"After surgery (postoperative)","cards":[{"title":"Complications by system","text":"Respiratory: airway obstruction, hypoxemia (restlessness or confusion may be the first sign), atelectasis, pneumonia, opioid respiratory depression, PE. · Cardiovascular: hypotension and hemorrhage (tachycardia, falling BP, a soaked dressing or high drain output), dysrhythmias, DVT (calf swelling, warmth, pain; prevent with SCDs, anticoagulants, early walking). · GI: post-op nausea and vomiting; paralytic ileus (absent bowel sounds, distension, no flatus; NG tube and NPO if severe; walking helps)."},{"title":"Pain control","text":"Assess with a scale; reassess after each dose (about 30 minutes after IV, 60 after oral). · Multimodal: acetaminophen, NSAIDs (if allowed), nerve blocks, plus opioids at the lowest effective dose. · PCA: only the patient presses the button; check sedation and RR regularly."},{"title":"Drains and dressings","text":"The surgeon usually does the first dressing change; reinforce a damp dressing and report. · Jackson-Pratt (bulb) and Hemovac (spring): empty when half full, measure, compress to restore suction. · Drainage changes from sanguineous → serosanguineous → serous. A sudden rise, bright red blood or purulent drainage is reported."}],"caption":"PACU priorities, assessment by system, the complications to catch early, pain, drains and discharge."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"What is the first priority in the PACU?","cue":"PACU priorities, assessment by system, the complications to catch early, pain, drains and discharge.","rule":"The airway (then breathing and circulation).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-postop","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"After surgery (postoperative)","notes":["t-postop"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 9; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The postoperative phase starts in the PACU and continues on the unit and at home. The order of assessment: airway, breathing, circulation, then level of consciousness, pain, the surgical site and drains, fluids and urine output. Most complications have a typical time window, so know what to look for and when.","Complications by system: Respiratory: airway obstruction, hypoxemia (restlessness or confusion may be the first sign), atelectasis, pneumonia, opioid respiratory depression, PE.; Cardiovascular: hypotension and hemorrhage (tachycardia, falling BP, a soaked dressing or high drain output), dysrhythmias, DVT (calf swelling, warmth, pain; prevent with SCDs, anticoagulants, early walking).; GI: post-op nausea and vomiting; paralytic ileus (absent bowel sounds, distension, no flatus; NG tube and NPO if severe; walking helps).; Neuro: post-op delirium in older adults (acute, fluctuating confusion).; Skin and wound: infection (usually 3–6 days), dehiscence and evisceration (about 5–10 days).","Pain control: Assess with a scale; reassess after each dose (about 30 minutes after IV, 60 after oral).; Multimodal: acetaminophen, NSAIDs (if allowed), nerve blocks, plus opioids at the lowest effective dose.; PCA: only the patient presses the button; check sedation and RR regularly.; Treat pain so the patient can breathe deeply, cough and walk."],"terms":["Hand-off from PACU","Urine output"],"termDefinitions":{"Hand-off from PACU":"The PACU nurse reports the procedure, anesthesia, fluids and blood loss, drains, meds given, VS trends and problems. Information lost at hand-off causes errors. Do a full assessment on arrival, then VS often (for example every 15 minutes ×4, every 30 ×2, hourly ×4, then every 4 hours, per policy).","Urine output":"At least 30 mL/hour (about 0.5 mL/kg/hour). Low output is an early sign of hypovolemia or kidney injury; anesthesia and opioids can also cause retention. The patient should void within 6–8 hours; use a bladder scan if he can’t."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-postop","title":"After surgery (postoperative)","blocks":[{"title":"Complications by system","lines":["Respiratory: airway obstruction, hypoxemia (restlessness or confusion may be the first sign), atelectasis, pneumonia, opioid respiratory depression, PE.","Cardiovascular: hypotension and hemorrhage (tachycardia, falling BP, a soaked dressing or high drain output), dysrhythmias, DVT (calf swelling, warmth, pain; prevent with SCDs, anticoagulants, early walking).","GI: post-op nausea and vomiting; paralytic ileus (absent bowel sounds, distension, no flatus; NG tube and NPO if severe; walking helps).","Neuro: post-op delirium in older adults (acute, fluctuating confusion).","Skin and wound: infection (usually 3–6 days), dehiscence and evisceration (about 5–10 days)."]},{"title":"Pain control","lines":["Assess with a scale; reassess after each dose (about 30 minutes after IV, 60 after oral).","Multimodal: acetaminophen, NSAIDs (if allowed), nerve blocks, plus opioids at the lowest effective dose.","PCA: only the patient presses the button; check sedation and RR regularly.","Treat pain so the patient can breathe deeply, cough and walk."]},{"title":"Drains and dressings","lines":["The surgeon usually does the first dressing change; reinforce a damp dressing and report.","Jackson-Pratt (bulb) and Hemovac (spring): empty when half full, measure, compress to restore suction.","Drainage changes from sanguineous → serosanguineous → serous. A sudden rise, bright red blood or purulent drainage is reported.","NG tube: check placement and patency; record output."]},{"title":"Discharge to home (or PACU discharge)","lines":["PACU discharge uses a score (Aldrete): activity, breathing, circulation, consciousness and color or SpO₂.","Teach wound care, signs of infection, pain control, activity limits (no heavy lifting), when to resume diet and driving, and when to call the surgeon (fever, increasing redness, drainage, calf pain, shortness of breath).","Same-day patients need a responsible adult to drive them home."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-postop:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which finding should the nurse report immediately for a patient 4 hours after abdominal surgery?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Pain 5/10 at the incision","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Tachycardia, falling BP and restlessness suggest hemorrhage or hypovolemic shock. The others are expected early after surgery."},{"id":"o1","text":"HR 122, BP 88/54 and restlessness","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Tachycardia, falling BP and restlessness suggest hemorrhage or hypovolemic shock. The others are expected early after surgery."},{"id":"o2","text":"Hypoactive bowel sounds","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Tachycardia, falling BP and restlessness suggest hemorrhage or hypovolemic shock. The others are expected early after surgery."},{"id":"o3","text":"Serosanguineous drainage in the JP drain","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Tachycardia, falling BP and restlessness suggest hemorrhage or hypovolemic shock. The others are expected early after surgery."}]},{"id":"t-postop:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What is the first priority in the PACU?","answer":"The airway (then breathing and circulation).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-postop:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Minimum hourly urine output after surgery?","answer":"About 30 mL/hour (0.5 mL/kg/hour).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Inflammation and the white blood cells","cards":[{"title":"The five signs of inflammation","text":"Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, loss of function. · Caused by vasodilation and leaky capillaries (histamine, kinins, prostaglandins). · Inflammation happens with or without infection (a sprained ankle, a burn, an insect bite)."},{"title":"The three stages","text":"Stage I, vascular: vessels dilate and leak; redness, warmth and swelling (exudate). · Stage II, cellular exudate: neutrophils rush in (the count rises within hours); pus forms in bacterial infection. · Stage III, tissue repair and replacement: tissue is rebuilt or replaced by scar; this phase needs protein and nutrients."},{"title":"Older adults","text":"Immunity weakens with age (immunosenescence): more infections, cancer and autoimmunity; vaccines work less well. · They often have no fever or a lower one and fewer classic signs; confusion, falls or appetite loss may be the first sign."}],"caption":"Self vs non-self, the five WBC types and what each does, the stages and signs of inflammation."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which WBC is the first to arrive at an infection?","cue":"Self vs non-self, the five WBC types and what each does, the stages and signs of inflammation.","rule":"The neutrophil.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-inflam","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Inflammation and the white blood cells","notes":["t-inflam"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["The immune system’s first job is to tell self from non-self (cells carry self markers, the HLAs, on their surfaces). Inflammation is the fast, nonspecific (innate) response to any injury or invader: it acts the same way every time and doesn’t create memory. The white blood cells (leukocytes), made in the bone marrow, carry out both inflammation and immunity. A normal WBC count is about 5,000–10,000/mm³.","The five signs of inflammation: Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, loss of function.; Caused by vasodilation and leaky capillaries (histamine, kinins, prostaglandins).; Inflammation happens with or without infection (a sprained ankle, a burn, an insect bite).","The three stages: Stage I, vascular: vessels dilate and leak; redness, warmth and swelling (exudate).; Stage II, cellular exudate: neutrophils rush in (the count rises within hours); pus forms in bacterial infection.; Stage III, tissue repair and replacement: tissue is rebuilt or replaced by scar; this phase needs protein and nutrients."],"terms":["Neutrophils","Macrophages (monocytes)","Eosinophils and basophils","Lymphocytes"],"termDefinitions":{"Neutrophils":"The largest share of WBCs (about 55–70%). Mature ones are segs; immature ones are bands. First responders: they swallow and destroy bacteria (phagocytosis) but live only hours. High count = acute bacterial infection; more bands (left shift) = a serious infection; a low count (neutropenia) = high infection risk.","Macrophages (monocytes)":"Monocytes in the blood become macrophages in tissue. They live longer, eat invaders and debris, and present antigens to lymphocytes, linking inflammation to immunity. Important in chronic infection and tissue repair.","Eosinophils and basophils":"Eosinophils about 1–4%; basophils about 0.5–1%. Eosinophils fight parasites and rise in allergies; basophils (and tissue mast cells) release histamine, heparin and other mediators. Raised eosinophils point to allergy or parasites; histamine drives allergic reactions.","Lymphocytes":"About 20–40% of WBCs: B cells, T cells and natural killer (NK) cells. B cells make antibodies; T cells direct and carry out cell-mediated immunity; NK cells kill virus-infected and cancer cells. These give specific immunity with memory (see the next topic)."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-inflam","title":"Inflammation and the white blood cells","blocks":[{"title":"The five signs of inflammation","lines":["Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, loss of function.","Caused by vasodilation and leaky capillaries (histamine, kinins, prostaglandins).","Inflammation happens with or without infection (a sprained ankle, a burn, an insect bite)."]},{"title":"The three stages","lines":["Stage I, vascular: vessels dilate and leak; redness, warmth and swelling (exudate).","Stage II, cellular exudate: neutrophils rush in (the count rises within hours); pus forms in bacterial infection.","Stage III, tissue repair and replacement: tissue is rebuilt or replaced by scar; this phase needs protein and nutrients."]},{"title":"Older adults","lines":["Immunity weakens with age (immunosenescence): more infections, cancer and autoimmunity; vaccines work less well.","They often have no fever or a lower one and fewer classic signs; confusion, falls or appetite loss may be the first sign."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-inflam:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A WBC differential shows a rise in bands. The nurse interprets this as:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A viral infection","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Bands are immature neutrophils released early during serious bacterial infection: a left shift."},{"id":"o1","text":"An allergic reaction","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Bands are immature neutrophils released early during serious bacterial infection: a left shift."},{"id":"o2","text":"An acute bacterial infection (left shift)","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Bands are immature neutrophils released early during serious bacterial infection: a left shift."},{"id":"o3","text":"A normal finding","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Bands are immature neutrophils released early during serious bacterial infection: a left shift."}]},{"id":"t-inflam:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which WBC is the first to arrive at an infection?","answer":"The neutrophil.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-inflam:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What is a “left shift”?","answer":"More bands (immature neutrophils) than normal: a serious bacterial infection.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Antibody-mediated and cell-mediated immunity","cards":[{"title":"Antibody classes","text":"IgG: the most common; the main memory antibody; crosses the placenta. · IgA: in secretions (tears, saliva, mucous membranes, breast milk). · IgM: the first antibody made in a new infection."},{"title":"Active vs passive immunity","text":"Natural active: you get the disease and make your own antibodies (long-lasting). · Artificial active: a vaccine makes you produce antibodies (long-lasting; may need boosters). · Natural passive: antibodies from mother to baby via placenta or breast milk (temporary)."},{"title":"Why it matters for nursing","text":"Live vaccines are avoided in people with weak immunity. · Corticosteroids, chemotherapy and transplant drugs suppress immunity: infection risk rises, and fever and inflammation signs may be hidden."}],"caption":"How B cells and antibodies work, the antibody classes, T cells, and active vs passive immunity."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which antibody crosses the placenta?","cue":"How B cells and antibodies work, the antibody classes, T cells, and active vs passive immunity.","rule":"IgG.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-immunity","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Antibody-mediated and cell-mediated immunity","notes":["t-immunity"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Specific (adaptive) immunity recognizes one particular invader (antigen) and remembers it, so the next exposure gets a faster, stronger response. Antibody-mediated immunity (AMI, humoral) uses B cells and antibodies (immunoglobulins). Cell-mediated immunity (CMI) uses T cells, which also coordinate the whole immune response. Together with inflammation they give full protection.","Antibody classes: IgG: the most common; the main memory antibody; crosses the placenta.; IgA: in secretions (tears, saliva, mucous membranes, breast milk).; IgM: the first antibody made in a new infection.; IgE: binds mast cells; drives allergic (type I) reactions and fights parasites.; IgD: on B-cell surfaces, helps activate B cells.","Active vs passive immunity: Natural active: you get the disease and make your own antibodies (long-lasting).; Artificial active: a vaccine makes you produce antibodies (long-lasting; may need boosters).; Natural passive: antibodies from mother to baby via placenta or breast milk (temporary).; Artificial passive: someone else’s ready-made antibodies by injection: immune globulin, antivenom, rabies or tetanus immune globulin. Works immediately but lasts only weeks to months."],"terms":["How antibodies form","CD4 (helper) and CD8 (cytotoxic) T cells"],"termDefinitions":{"How antibodies form":"Antigen recognition → sensitization (B cells become plasma cells and memory cells) → antibody production → the antibody binds the antigen. Once bound, the antibody marks the invader for destruction (neutralizing it, clumping it, or activating complement). The first response takes days; the memory response is fast. That is how vaccines protect.","CD4 (helper) and CD8 (cytotoxic) T cells":"Helper T cells direct both arms of immunity; cytotoxic T cells kill infected and cancer cells; regulatory (suppressor) T cells calm the response. Losing helper T cells (as in HIV) weakens all of immunity. CMI also drives transplant rejection and type IV (delayed) reactions such as a positive TB skin test."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-immunity","title":"Antibody-mediated and cell-mediated immunity","blocks":[{"title":"Antibody classes","lines":["IgG: the most common; the main memory antibody; crosses the placenta.","IgA: in secretions (tears, saliva, mucous membranes, breast milk).","IgM: the first antibody made in a new infection.","IgE: binds mast cells; drives allergic (type I) reactions and fights parasites.","IgD: on B-cell surfaces, helps activate B cells."]},{"title":"Active vs passive immunity","lines":["Natural active: you get the disease and make your own antibodies (long-lasting).","Artificial active: a vaccine makes you produce antibodies (long-lasting; may need boosters).","Natural passive: antibodies from mother to baby via placenta or breast milk (temporary).","Artificial passive: someone else’s ready-made antibodies by injection: immune globulin, antivenom, rabies or tetanus immune globulin. Works immediately but lasts only weeks to months."]},{"title":"Why it matters for nursing","lines":["Live vaccines are avoided in people with weak immunity.","Corticosteroids, chemotherapy and transplant drugs suppress immunity: infection risk rises, and fever and inflammation signs may be hidden."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-immunity:case","kind":"choice","scene":"After a needlestick, a nurse receives hepatitis B immune globulin. This provides:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Natural active immunity","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Ready-made antibodies by injection = artificial passive: immediate but short-lived."},{"id":"o1","text":"Artificial active immunity","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Ready-made antibodies by injection = artificial passive: immediate but short-lived."},{"id":"o2","text":"Artificial passive immunity","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Ready-made antibodies by injection = artificial passive: immediate but short-lived."},{"id":"o3","text":"Natural passive immunity","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Ready-made antibodies by injection = artificial passive: immediate but short-lived."}]},{"id":"t-immunity:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which antibody crosses the placenta?","answer":"IgG.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-immunity:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which antibody causes allergic reactions?","answer":"IgE.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Hypersensitivity (allergy) reactions","cards":[{"title":"Type V (stimulated)","text":"Antibodies stimulate a cell receptor instead of destroying the cell: Graves’ disease (antibodies switch on the thyroid)."},{"title":"Latex allergy","text":"Can be type I (hives, anaphylaxis) or type IV (contact dermatitis). · Higher risk: health care workers, people with many surgeries (e.g., spina bifida), and food allergies to banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut (cross-reactions). · Use a latex-free room, equipment and supplies; schedule surgery first in the day if the OR isn’t latex-free."},{"title":"Angioedema and anaphylaxis (see the walk-throughs)","text":"Angioedema: deep swelling of lips, tongue and throat; ACE inhibitors are a common cause (bradykinin); the airway is the priority. · Anaphylaxis: give IM epinephrine into the thigh first; oxygen; fluids; then antihistamines, albuterol, steroids."}],"caption":"Types I–IV (and V) with examples, latex allergy, angioedema and anaphylaxis in brief."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which hypersensitivity type is a hemolytic transfusion reaction?","cue":"Types I–IV (and V) with examples, latex allergy, angioedema and anaphylaxis in brief.","rule":"Type II (cytotoxic).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-hyper","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Hypersensitivity (allergy) reactions","notes":["t-hyper"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 17; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["A hypersensitivity reaction is an immune response that is too strong or aimed at something harmless, and it damages the body. The reactions are grouped by how they happen and how fast. Types I–III involve antibodies; type IV involves T cells and is delayed.","Type V (stimulated): Antibodies stimulate a cell receptor instead of destroying the cell: Graves’ disease (antibodies switch on the thyroid).","Latex allergy: Can be type I (hives, anaphylaxis) or type IV (contact dermatitis).; Higher risk: health care workers, people with many surgeries (e.g., spina bifida), and food allergies to banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut (cross-reactions).; Use a latex-free room, equipment and supplies; schedule surgery first in the day if the OR isn’t latex-free."],"terms":["Type I: rapid, IgE-mediated","Type II: cytotoxic","Type III: immune complex","Type IV: delayed (cell-mediated)"],"termDefinitions":{"Type I: rapid, IgE-mediated":"Minutes after exposure: allergic rhinitis (hay fever), hives, allergic asthma, food, drug, latex and sting allergies, anaphylaxis. IgE on mast cells binds the allergen → histamine release → vasodilation, capillary leak, bronchospasm. Mild: antihistamines, avoiding the allergen. Severe: epinephrine first.","Type II: cytotoxic":"Hemolytic transfusion reaction (ABO mismatch), autoimmune hemolytic anemia, Goodpasture syndrome, some drug reactions. IgG or IgM antibodies attack antigens on the surface of cells, and complement destroys the cells. Stop the trigger (stop a transfusion right away) and support the patient.","Type III: immune complex":"SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, serum sickness, vasculitis, glomerulonephritis. Antigen-antibody complexes circulate and deposit in tissues (kidneys, joints, skin, vessels), triggering inflammation. Treat with anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressant drugs; watch the kidneys.","Type IV: delayed (cell-mediated)":"Positive TB skin test, contact dermatitis (poison ivy, nickel, latex gloves’ chemicals), transplant rejection. Sensitized T cells, not antibodies, cause the reaction, so it takes 24–72 hours. Read a TB skin test at 48–72 hours; topical steroids for contact dermatitis."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-hyper","title":"Hypersensitivity (allergy) reactions","blocks":[{"title":"Type V (stimulated)","lines":["Antibodies stimulate a cell receptor instead of destroying the cell: Graves’ disease (antibodies switch on the thyroid)."]},{"title":"Latex allergy","lines":["Can be type I (hives, anaphylaxis) or type IV (contact dermatitis).","Higher risk: health care workers, people with many surgeries (e.g., spina bifida), and food allergies to banana, avocado, kiwi, chestnut (cross-reactions).","Use a latex-free room, equipment and supplies; schedule surgery first in the day if the OR isn’t latex-free."]},{"title":"Angioedema and anaphylaxis (see the walk-throughs)","lines":["Angioedema: deep swelling of lips, tongue and throat; ACE inhibitors are a common cause (bradykinin); the airway is the priority.","Anaphylaxis: give IM epinephrine into the thigh first; oxygen; fluids; then antihistamines, albuterol, steroids."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-hyper:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A transfusion of ABO-incompatible blood causes which type of hypersensitivity reaction?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Type I","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Antibodies attack antigens on the donor red cells: type II (cytotoxic)."},{"id":"o1","text":"Type II","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Antibodies attack antigens on the donor red cells: type II (cytotoxic)."},{"id":"o2","text":"Type III","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Antibodies attack antigens on the donor red cells: type II (cytotoxic)."},{"id":"o3","text":"Type IV","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Antibodies attack antigens on the donor red cells: type II (cytotoxic)."}]},{"id":"t-hyper:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which hypersensitivity type is a hemolytic transfusion reaction?","answer":"Type II (cytotoxic).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-hyper:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which type is SLE?","answer":"Type III (immune complex).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Autoimmunity, Lyme disease and transplant rejection","cards":[{"title":"Lyme disease stages and prevention","text":"Stage 2 (early disseminated): weeks later: more rashes, facial (Bell’s) palsy, meningitis, heart block and palpitations. · Stage 3 (late): months later: arthritis (especially knees), memory and nerve problems. · Prevent: long sleeves and pants tucked into socks, insect repellent (DEET), stay on trails, check skin daily for ticks; remove a tick with fine tweezers pulling straight up steadily, then wash the area."},{"title":"Immunosuppressants after transplant","text":"Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine): check blood levels; can harm the kidneys and raise BP and glucose. · Mycophenolate (bone marrow suppression, GI upset), corticosteroids, and others. · Taken for life, exactly on schedule; never stopped. Big risks: infection and cancer (especially skin: use sunscreen)."},{"title":"Graft-versus-host disease","text":"After stem cell transplant, the donor’s T cells attack the recipient’s tissues: skin rash, diarrhea, liver problems."}],"caption":"When the immune system attacks self, Lyme disease stages, the three kinds of rejection and immunosuppressants."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which rejection happens within minutes and requires removing the organ?","cue":"When the immune system attacks self, Lyme disease stages, the three kinds of rejection and immunosuppressants.","rule":"Hyperacute rejection.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-autoimm","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Autoimmunity, Lyme disease and transplant rejection","notes":["t-autoimm"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 17; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Autoimmunity happens when the body loses tolerance and attacks its own tissue (examples: SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diabetes, MS, Graves’ disease). Lyme disease is a tick-borne infection whose later stages cause immune-driven joint, heart and nerve problems. Transplant rejection is the immune system doing its normal job against foreign (non-self) tissue.","Lyme disease stages and prevention: Stage 2 (early disseminated): weeks later: more rashes, facial (Bell’s) palsy, meningitis, heart block and palpitations.; Stage 3 (late): months later: arthritis (especially knees), memory and nerve problems.; Prevent: long sleeves and pants tucked into socks, insect repellent (DEET), stay on trails, check skin daily for ticks; remove a tick with fine tweezers pulling straight up steadily, then wash the area.","Immunosuppressants after transplant: Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine): check blood levels; can harm the kidneys and raise BP and glucose.; Mycophenolate (bone marrow suppression, GI upset), corticosteroids, and others.; Taken for life, exactly on schedule; never stopped. Big risks: infection and cancer (especially skin: use sunscreen).; Avoid grapefruit and St. John’s wort with some drugs (they change drug levels); no live vaccines."],"terms":["Erythema migrans","Hyperacute, acute and chronic rejection"],"termDefinitions":{"Erythema migrans":"An expanding red rash, often with a clear center (“bull’s-eye”), at the tick bite, 3–30 days later, with flu-like symptoms. Borrelia burgdorferi spreading in the skin after a bite from an infected deer (blacklegged) tick. Stage 1 (early localized). Treated with doxycycline (or amoxicillin); early treatment prevents later stages.","Hyperacute, acute and chronic rejection":"Hyperacute: minutes to hours. Acute: usually in the first months. Chronic: months to years, gradual loss of organ function. Hyperacute: pre-existing antibodies (the organ must be removed). Acute: mostly T cells (often reversible with more immunosuppression). Chronic: slow inflammation and fibrosis of vessels (no cure; may need re-transplant). Teach the signs of rejection for that organ: fever, tenderness over the graft, falling function (e.g., rising creatinine and less urine for a kidney)."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-autoimm","title":"Autoimmunity, Lyme disease and transplant rejection","blocks":[{"title":"Lyme disease stages and prevention","lines":["Stage 2 (early disseminated): weeks later: more rashes, facial (Bell’s) palsy, meningitis, heart block and palpitations.","Stage 3 (late): months later: arthritis (especially knees), memory and nerve problems.","Prevent: long sleeves and pants tucked into socks, insect repellent (DEET), stay on trails, check skin daily for ticks; remove a tick with fine tweezers pulling straight up steadily, then wash the area."]},{"title":"Immunosuppressants after transplant","lines":["Calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, cyclosporine): check blood levels; can harm the kidneys and raise BP and glucose.","Mycophenolate (bone marrow suppression, GI upset), corticosteroids, and others.","Taken for life, exactly on schedule; never stopped. Big risks: infection and cancer (especially skin: use sunscreen).","Avoid grapefruit and St. John’s wort with some drugs (they change drug levels); no live vaccines."]},{"title":"Graft-versus-host disease","lines":["After stem cell transplant, the donor’s T cells attack the recipient’s tissues: skin rash, diarrhea, liver problems."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-autoimm:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A kidney recipient’s creatinine rises 3 weeks after transplant, with fever and tenderness over the graft. Which type of rejection is most likely?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Hyperacute","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Acute rejection usually occurs in the first months; it is T-cell mediated and often reversible with more immunosuppression."},{"id":"o1","text":"Acute","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Acute rejection usually occurs in the first months; it is T-cell mediated and often reversible with more immunosuppression."},{"id":"o2","text":"Chronic","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Acute rejection usually occurs in the first months; it is T-cell mediated and often reversible with more immunosuppression."},{"id":"o3","text":"Graft-versus-host disease","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Acute rejection usually occurs in the first months; it is T-cell mediated and often reversible with more immunosuppression."}]},{"id":"t-autoimm:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which rejection happens within minutes and requires removing the organ?","answer":"Hyperacute rejection.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-autoimm:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What is the first-line drug for early Lyme disease?","answer":"Doxycycline.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Cancer biology, staging and prevention","cards":[{"title":"How cancer spreads","text":"Local invasion → angiogenesis (the tumor grows its own blood supply) → cells break off and travel in blood or lymph → they lodge and grow elsewhere. · Common metastasis sites: bone, liver, lung, brain (and lymph nodes). · Cancers are named by tissue: carcinoma (epithelial), sarcoma (connective tissue), leukemia and lymphoma (blood and lymph)."},{"title":"Causes and risk factors","text":"Chemical (tobacco is the biggest preventable cause; alcohol, asbestos, benzene). · Physical: UV light, ionizing radiation, chronic irritation. · Viral: HPV (cervical, throat, anal), hepatitis B and C (liver), Epstein-Barr (lymphoma), HIV."},{"title":"Prevention and early detection","text":"Primary (stop cancer from starting): no tobacco, limit alcohol, healthy weight, activity, a plant-rich diet, sun protection, HPV and hepatitis B vaccines, chemoprevention (e.g., tamoxifen for some high-risk women), prophylactic surgery for some inherited risks. · Secondary (find it early: screening): colorectal from age 45 (colonoscopy or stool tests), mammography for breast cancer, cervical (Pap and HPV tests), low-dose CT for lung cancer in heavy smokers aged 50–80 (20 pack-years, current or quit within 15 years), and shared decision-making for PSA (prostate). · Warning signs (CAUTION): change in bowel or bladder habits, a sore that doesn’t heal, unusual bleeding or discharge, a thickening or lump, indigestion or trouble swallowing, an obvious change in a wart or mole, a nagging cough or hoarseness."}],"caption":"Normal vs cancer cells, how cancer develops and spreads, grading and TNM staging, prevention and screening."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"What does the “M” in TNM mean?","cue":"Normal vs cancer cells, how cancer develops and spreads, grading and TNM staging, prevention and screening.","rule":"Distant metastasis (M0 none, M1 present).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-cancerbio","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Cancer biology, staging and prevention","notes":["t-cancerbio"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Normal cells have contact inhibition (stop dividing when they touch), a specific job (differentiation), and orderly growth and death (apoptosis). Cancer cells have lost these controls: they divide continuously, look and act less like their tissue of origin (anaplasia), ignore contact inhibition, avoid apoptosis, and can invade and spread (metastasize). Benign tumors grow by expansion and stay put; malignant tumors invade and spread.","How cancer spreads: Local invasion → angiogenesis (the tumor grows its own blood supply) → cells break off and travel in blood or lymph → they lodge and grow elsewhere.; Common metastasis sites: bone, liver, lung, brain (and lymph nodes).; Cancers are named by tissue: carcinoma (epithelial), sarcoma (connective tissue), leukemia and lymphoma (blood and lymph).","Causes and risk factors: Chemical (tobacco is the biggest preventable cause; alcohol, asbestos, benzene).; Physical: UV light, ionizing radiation, chronic irritation.; Viral: HPV (cervical, throat, anal), hepatitis B and C (liver), Epstein-Barr (lymphoma), HIV.; Genetic: inherited mutations in tumor suppressor genes (BRCA1/2), oncogene activation; also age, obesity, inactivity, and a weak immune system.; Health equity: income, insurance, neighborhood and racism affect exposure, screening and outcomes."],"terms":["Carcinogenesis","Grading vs staging"],"termDefinitions":{"Carcinogenesis":"Initiation (a carcinogen damages DNA; irreversible) → promotion (promoters such as hormones, obesity or alcohol help initiated cells grow; the latency period) → progression → metastasis. Each step needs more genetic changes, which is why cancer risk rises with age and exposure. Removing promoters (stopping smoking, losing weight, limiting alcohol) can still lower risk after initiation.","Grading vs staging":"Grading: how abnormal the cells look (G1 well differentiated → G4 undifferentiated). Staging: how far the cancer has spread (TNM). Higher grade = more aggressive cells; higher stage = more extensive disease. TNM: T = size of the primary tumor, N = lymph node involvement, M = distant metastasis (M0 none, M1 present). Stages run 0 (in situ) to IV (distant spread)."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-cancerbio","title":"Cancer biology, staging and prevention","blocks":[{"title":"How cancer spreads","lines":["Local invasion → angiogenesis (the tumor grows its own blood supply) → cells break off and travel in blood or lymph → they lodge and grow elsewhere.","Common metastasis sites: bone, liver, lung, brain (and lymph nodes).","Cancers are named by tissue: carcinoma (epithelial), sarcoma (connective tissue), leukemia and lymphoma (blood and lymph)."]},{"title":"Causes and risk factors","lines":["Chemical (tobacco is the biggest preventable cause; alcohol, asbestos, benzene).","Physical: UV light, ionizing radiation, chronic irritation.","Viral: HPV (cervical, throat, anal), hepatitis B and C (liver), Epstein-Barr (lymphoma), HIV.","Genetic: inherited mutations in tumor suppressor genes (BRCA1/2), oncogene activation; also age, obesity, inactivity, and a weak immune system.","Health equity: income, insurance, neighborhood and racism affect exposure, screening and outcomes."]},{"title":"Prevention and early detection","lines":["Primary (stop cancer from starting): no tobacco, limit alcohol, healthy weight, activity, a plant-rich diet, sun protection, HPV and hepatitis B vaccines, chemoprevention (e.g., tamoxifen for some high-risk women), prophylactic surgery for some inherited risks.","Secondary (find it early: screening): colorectal from age 45 (colonoscopy or stool tests), mammography for breast cancer, cervical (Pap and HPV tests), low-dose CT for lung cancer in heavy smokers aged 50–80 (20 pack-years, current or quit within 15 years), and shared decision-making for PSA (prostate).","Warning signs (CAUTION): change in bowel or bladder habits, a sore that doesn’t heal, unusual bleeding or discharge, a thickening or lump, indigestion or trouble swallowing, an obvious change in a wart or mole, a nagging cough or hoarseness."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-cancerbio:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A tumor is staged T2 N1 M0. The nurse interprets this as:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A tumor with distant metastasis","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. N1 = regional nodes involved; M0 = no distant spread."},{"id":"o1","text":"A tumor with regional lymph node involvement and no distant metastasis","correct":true,"why":"Supported. N1 = regional nodes involved; M0 = no distant spread."},{"id":"o2","text":"A benign tumor","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. N1 = regional nodes involved; M0 = no distant spread."},{"id":"o3","text":"Cancer in situ","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. N1 = regional nodes involved; M0 = no distant spread."}]},{"id":"t-cancerbio:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What does the “M” in TNM mean?","answer":"Distant metastasis (M0 none, M1 present).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-cancerbio:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Grade 4 tumor cells are…","answer":"Undifferentiated (look least like normal cells; most aggressive).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Cancer treatment: surgery, radiation, chemotherapy and beyond","cards":[{"title":"Kinds of cancer surgery","text":"Prophylactic (remove at-risk tissue, e.g., mastectomy for a BRCA mutation), diagnostic (biopsy), curative (remove all the tumor with clear margins), debulking (cytoreductive), palliative (relieve symptoms), reconstructive."},{"title":"Chemo safety: extravasation","text":"Vesicants can destroy tissue if they leak out of the vein. · Signs: pain, burning, swelling, redness, or no blood return. · Stop the infusion immediately and follow the facility’s extravasation protocol (antidote, warm or cold compresses depending on the drug, notify the provider, document)."},{"title":"Immunotherapy and targeted therapy","text":"Checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab, ipilimumab) release the brakes on T cells so they attack the cancer, but they can also attack normal organs: immune-related adverse events (irAEs) such as colitis (diarrhea), pneumonitis (cough, dyspnea), hepatitis, rash, and endocrine problems (thyroid, adrenal, pituitary, type 1 diabetes). Patients must report new symptoms early; treatment is usually corticosteroids. · CAR-T cell therapy: watch for cytokine release syndrome (fever, low BP, hypoxia) and neurotoxicity (confusion). · Targeted therapy blocks a specific feature of cancer cells: tyrosine kinase inhibitors (“-tinibs”), monoclonal antibodies (“-mabs”; infusion reactions), angiogenesis inhibitors (bevacizumab: bleeding, high BP, poor wound healing). EGFR inhibitors cause an acne-like rash."}],"caption":"Kinds of cancer surgery, radiation and brachytherapy safety, chemo safety, immunotherapy, targeted and hormone therapy."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Is a patient getting external beam radiation radioactive?","cue":"Kinds of cancer surgery, radiation and brachytherapy safety, chemo safety, immunotherapy, targeted and hormone therapy.","rule":"No. Only patients with internal (brachytherapy) sources emit radiation.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-cancertx","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Cancer treatment: surgery, radiation, chemotherapy and beyond","notes":["t-cancertx"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Treatment depends on the cancer type, stage and the person’s goals. Most people get a combination. The goal may be cure, control (living with cancer as a chronic disease) or palliation (comfort). Every treatment also harms some normal cells, so the nurse’s work is safety, side-effect management and teaching.","Kinds of cancer surgery: Prophylactic (remove at-risk tissue, e.g., mastectomy for a BRCA mutation), diagnostic (biopsy), curative (remove all the tumor with clear margins), debulking (cytoreductive), palliative (relieve symptoms), reconstructive.","Chemo safety: extravasation: Vesicants can destroy tissue if they leak out of the vein.; Signs: pain, burning, swelling, redness, or no blood return.; Stop the infusion immediately and follow the facility’s extravasation protocol (antidote, warm or cold compresses depending on the drug, notify the provider, document)."],"terms":["External beam radiation (teletherapy)","Brachytherapy (internal radiation)","Chemotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{"External beam radiation (teletherapy)":"A machine aims radiation at marked skin areas, usually daily on weekdays for several weeks. Radiation damages DNA, mostly in fast-dividing cells in the treatment field. The patient is not radioactive. Skin care: wash gently with lukewarm water and mild soap, pat dry, don’t remove the markings, no lotions, powders or deodorant on the area unless approved, avoid sun, heat and tight clothing. Fatigue is common.","Brachytherapy (internal radiation)":"A radioactive source is placed inside or next to the tumor: sealed (implants, seeds) or unsealed (oral or IV radioactive drugs). Delivers a high dose to the tumor while the source is in place, so the patient emits radiation. Private room, a radiation sign, a dosimeter badge for staff, time (limit each visit), distance (stay at least about 6 feet away when not giving care), shielding. No pregnant visitors or children; limited visit time. A dislodged sealed source: never touch it: use long forceps and a lead container. With unsealed sources, body fluids are radioactive.","Chemotherapy":"Drugs that kill dividing cells, given in cycles (rest periods let normal cells recover), often several drugs at once. Some drugs work in a specific phase of the cell cycle; combining them kills more cancer cells and limits resistance. They are hazardous drugs: prepare them in a special hood, wear chemo-rated gloves (two pairs) and a gown, use closed-system devices, and handle the patient’s body fluids as hazardous for about 48 hours (flush the toilet twice with the lid down)."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-cancertx","title":"Cancer treatment: surgery, radiation, chemotherapy and beyond","blocks":[{"title":"Kinds of cancer surgery","lines":["Prophylactic (remove at-risk tissue, e.g., mastectomy for a BRCA mutation), diagnostic (biopsy), curative (remove all the tumor with clear margins), debulking (cytoreductive), palliative (relieve symptoms), reconstructive."]},{"title":"Chemo safety: extravasation","lines":["Vesicants can destroy tissue if they leak out of the vein.","Signs: pain, burning, swelling, redness, or no blood return.","Stop the infusion immediately and follow the facility’s extravasation protocol (antidote, warm or cold compresses depending on the drug, notify the provider, document)."]},{"title":"Immunotherapy and targeted therapy","lines":["Checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab, ipilimumab) release the brakes on T cells so they attack the cancer, but they can also attack normal organs: immune-related adverse events (irAEs) such as colitis (diarrhea), pneumonitis (cough, dyspnea), hepatitis, rash, and endocrine problems (thyroid, adrenal, pituitary, type 1 diabetes). Patients must report new symptoms early; treatment is usually corticosteroids.","CAR-T cell therapy: watch for cytokine release syndrome (fever, low BP, hypoxia) and neurotoxicity (confusion).","Targeted therapy blocks a specific feature of cancer cells: tyrosine kinase inhibitors (“-tinibs”), monoclonal antibodies (“-mabs”; infusion reactions), angiogenesis inhibitors (bevacizumab: bleeding, high BP, poor wound healing). EGFR inhibitors cause an acne-like rash."]},{"title":"Hormone (endocrine) therapy","lines":["For hormone-sensitive breast and prostate cancers.","Tamoxifen: hot flashes, blood clots, endometrial cancer risk. Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole): bone loss, joint pain.","Androgen deprivation for prostate cancer: hot flashes, sexual dysfunction, bone loss, weight gain."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-cancertx:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient getting external beam radiation asks if she is radioactive and should stay away from her grandchildren. The nurse’s best response:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Yes, for 48 hours after each treatment.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Only internal sources make the patient emit radiation."},{"id":"o1","text":"“No. External radiation does not make you radioactive.”","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Only internal sources make the patient emit radiation."},{"id":"o2","text":"“Only your body fluids are radioactive.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Only internal sources make the patient emit radiation."},{"id":"o3","text":"“Avoid them for the whole course of treatment.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Only internal sources make the patient emit radiation."}]},{"id":"t-cancertx:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Is a patient getting external beam radiation radioactive?","answer":"No. Only patients with internal (brachytherapy) sources emit radiation.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-cancertx:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you do if a sealed radiation implant falls out?","answer":"Don’t touch it; pick it up with long-handled forceps and put it in the lead container; notify radiation safety.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Side effects of cancer treatment","cards":[{"title":"Mucositis (sores along the GI tract)","text":"Inspect the mouth daily; frequent oral care with a soft toothbrush and rinses of saline or baking soda solution. · Avoid alcohol-based mouthwash, very hot, spicy or acidic foods, and tobacco. · Topical anesthetic or “magic mouthwash” as ordered; watch for thrush and herpes infections; ensure fluids and nutrition."},{"title":"Alopecia and skin","text":"Hair loss usually starts 1–3 weeks after treatment and is temporary (it may grow back a different color or texture); radiation hair loss in the field may be permanent at high doses. · Suggest a wig, scarf or hat before it starts, a gentle shampoo, and sun protection for the scalp; scalp cooling may reduce loss for some drugs. · Acknowledge body-image grief."},{"title":"Chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy (CIPN)","text":"Numbness, tingling and pain in hands and feet, loss of fine motor skill and balance (e.g., platinum drugs, taxanes, vincristine). · Safety: check water temperature with a thermometer or an unaffected body part, wear well-fitting shoes, inspect the feet, remove trip hazards; report it (doses may be changed)."}],"caption":"Bone marrow suppression, CINV, mucositis, alopecia, neuropathy, fatigue, and how the nurse helps."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which side effect of cancer therapy is the most common?","cue":"Bone marrow suppression, CINV, mucositis, alopecia, neuropathy, fatigue, and how the nurse helps.","rule":"Fatigue.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-sidefx","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Side effects of cancer treatment","notes":["t-sidefx"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Chemotherapy and radiation hit the body’s fast-dividing normal cells: bone marrow, the GI lining (mouth to rectum), hair follicles and skin. Knowing which cells are hit predicts the side effects. Bone marrow suppression is the most dangerous; fatigue is the most common.","Mucositis (sores along the GI tract): Inspect the mouth daily; frequent oral care with a soft toothbrush and rinses of saline or baking soda solution.; Avoid alcohol-based mouthwash, very hot, spicy or acidic foods, and tobacco.; Topical anesthetic or “magic mouthwash” as ordered; watch for thrush and herpes infections; ensure fluids and nutrition.","Alopecia and skin: Hair loss usually starts 1–3 weeks after treatment and is temporary (it may grow back a different color or texture); radiation hair loss in the field may be permanent at high doses.; Suggest a wig, scarf or hat before it starts, a gentle shampoo, and sun protection for the scalp; scalp cooling may reduce loss for some drugs.; Acknowledge body-image grief."],"terms":["Thrombocytopenia","Anemia","Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV)","olanzapine"],"termDefinitions":{"Thrombocytopenia":"Platelets below 150,000/mm³; bleeding risk rises below 50,000, spontaneous bleeding below about 20,000. The marrow makes fewer platelets. Bleeding precautions: soft toothbrush, electric razor, no IM injections or rectal procedures if possible, pressure on puncture sites for at least 5–10 minutes, no aspirin or NSAIDs, prevent constipation and straining, watch for petechiae, bruising, nosebleeds, blood in urine or stool. Platelet transfusion if very low.","Anemia":"Fatigue, pallor, dyspnea on exertion, tachycardia. Fewer red cells carry oxygen. Rest periods; transfusion for low Hgb or symptoms; erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (epoetin) raise the risk of clots and high BP.","Chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (CINV)":"Acute (first 24 hours), delayed (days 2–5) or anticipatory (before treatment, a learned response). The drugs stimulate the brain’s vomiting center and the gut (serotonin, substance P). Give antiemetics before chemo and on a schedule, not only when needed: 5-HT3 blockers (ondansetron), NK1 blockers (aprepitant), dexamethasone, olanzapine. Small, bland meals; relaxation for anticipatory nausea."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-sidefx","title":"Side effects of cancer treatment","blocks":[{"title":"Mucositis (sores along the GI tract)","lines":["Inspect the mouth daily; frequent oral care with a soft toothbrush and rinses of saline or baking soda solution.","Avoid alcohol-based mouthwash, very hot, spicy or acidic foods, and tobacco.","Topical anesthetic or “magic mouthwash” as ordered; watch for thrush and herpes infections; ensure fluids and nutrition."]},{"title":"Alopecia and skin","lines":["Hair loss usually starts 1–3 weeks after treatment and is temporary (it may grow back a different color or texture); radiation hair loss in the field may be permanent at high doses.","Suggest a wig, scarf or hat before it starts, a gentle shampoo, and sun protection for the scalp; scalp cooling may reduce loss for some drugs.","Acknowledge body-image grief."]},{"title":"Chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy (CIPN)","lines":["Numbness, tingling and pain in hands and feet, loss of fine motor skill and balance (e.g., platinum drugs, taxanes, vincristine).","Safety: check water temperature with a thermometer or an unaffected body part, wear well-fitting shoes, inspect the feet, remove trip hazards; report it (doses may be changed)."]},{"title":"Other effects","lines":["Fatigue (most common): plan rest and light exercise, which helps.","“Chemo brain” (cognitive changes): lists, routines.","Organ toxicity (heart, lungs, kidneys), fertility loss (discuss sperm or egg banking before treatment), sexual changes, taste changes and weight loss."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-sidefx:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which mouth care is best for a patient with chemotherapy-induced mucositis?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Alcohol-based mouthwash after meals","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Gentle care. Alcohol and lemon-glycerin dry and irritate the mucosa."},{"id":"o1","text":"Saline or baking soda rinses and a soft toothbrush","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Gentle care. Alcohol and lemon-glycerin dry and irritate the mucosa."},{"id":"o2","text":"Lemon-glycerin swabs","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Gentle care. Alcohol and lemon-glycerin dry and irritate the mucosa."},{"id":"o3","text":"Brush with a firm brush to remove debris","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Gentle care. Alcohol and lemon-glycerin dry and irritate the mucosa."}]},{"id":"t-sidefx:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which side effect of cancer therapy is the most common?","answer":"Fatigue.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-sidefx:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Platelet count where spontaneous bleeding becomes likely?","answer":"Below about 20,000/mm³.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Oncologic emergencies","cards":[{"title":"Spinal cord compression","text":"Early cue: back pain (worse lying down or coughing). Late: leg weakness, sensory loss, bowel and bladder dysfunction. · Act: report at once; dexamethasone, MRI, radiation or surgery."},{"title":"Superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome","text":"A tumor (lung cancer, lymphoma) compresses the SVC: facial, neck and arm swelling, periorbital edema, a tight collar, distended chest veins; late: stridor, cyanosis, confusion. · Act: head up, oxygen, report; radiation, chemo or a stent."},{"title":"Tumor lysis syndrome","text":"Rapid cancer cell death (leukemia, lymphoma) in the first days of treatment: high K⁺, high phosphate, low Ca²⁺, high uric acid, acute kidney injury. · Act: hydration, allopurinol or rasburicase, cardiac monitoring, treat hyperkalemia, strict I&O."}],"caption":"Sepsis and DIC, SIADH, hypercalcemia, spinal cord compression, SVC syndrome and tumor lysis syndrome."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Earliest symptom of spinal cord compression?","cue":"Sepsis and DIC, SIADH, hypercalcemia, spinal cord compression, SVC syndrome and tumor lysis syndrome.","rule":"Back pain.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-oncemerg","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Oncologic emergencies","notes":["t-oncemerg"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["An oncologic emergency is a complication of cancer or its treatment that can cause death or permanent harm within hours to days unless it is recognized and treated fast. Nurses often see the first signs, so learn the early cue and the first action for each.","Spinal cord compression: Early cue: back pain (worse lying down or coughing). Late: leg weakness, sensory loss, bowel and bladder dysfunction.; Act: report at once; dexamethasone, MRI, radiation or surgery.","Superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome: A tumor (lung cancer, lymphoma) compresses the SVC: facial, neck and arm swelling, periorbital edema, a tight collar, distended chest veins; late: stridor, cyanosis, confusion.; Act: head up, oxygen, report; radiation, chemo or a stent."],"terms":["Sepsis and DIC","SIADH","Hypercalcemia"],"termDefinitions":{"Sepsis and DIC":"Sepsis: fever (or none in neutropenia), tachycardia, low BP, confusion. DIC: bleeding from IV sites, gums and wounds, petechiae, plus clots. Neutropenia lets infection spread; sepsis and some cancers (leukemia) trigger widespread clotting that uses up platelets and clotting factors. Cultures then antibiotics within 1 hour. DIC labs: low platelets and fibrinogen, prolonged PT/aPTT, high D-dimer. Treat the cause; replace platelets, plasma, cryoprecipitate; bleeding precautions.","SIADH":"Weight gain without edema, low urine output, concentrated urine, hyponatremia (headache, confusion, seizures). Tumors (especially small cell lung cancer) or drugs make excess ADH, so the kidneys keep water. Fluid restriction, daily weight, sodium checks, seizure precautions; hypertonic saline or ADH antagonists (tolvaptan, conivaptan) for severe cases; correct sodium slowly.","Hypercalcemia":"Fatigue, weakness, constipation, nausea, confusion, polyuria and thirst, dehydration, ECG changes. Bone metastases break down bone, or tumors release a PTH-like hormone. IV normal saline hydration, then loop diuretics; calcitonin, bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid) or denosumab; walking helps keep calcium in bone."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-oncemerg","title":"Oncologic emergencies","blocks":[{"title":"Spinal cord compression","lines":["Early cue: back pain (worse lying down or coughing). Late: leg weakness, sensory loss, bowel and bladder dysfunction.","Act: report at once; dexamethasone, MRI, radiation or surgery."]},{"title":"Superior vena cava (SVC) syndrome","lines":["A tumor (lung cancer, lymphoma) compresses the SVC: facial, neck and arm swelling, periorbital edema, a tight collar, distended chest veins; late: stridor, cyanosis, confusion.","Act: head up, oxygen, report; radiation, chemo or a stent."]},{"title":"Tumor lysis syndrome","lines":["Rapid cancer cell death (leukemia, lymphoma) in the first days of treatment: high K⁺, high phosphate, low Ca²⁺, high uric acid, acute kidney injury.","Act: hydration, allopurinol or rasburicase, cardiac monitoring, treat hyperkalemia, strict I&O."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-oncemerg:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with lung cancer has facial swelling in the morning, distended neck veins and says her collar feels tight. The nurse suspects:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"SIADH","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A tumor blocks the SVC, so blood backs up in the head, neck and arms."},{"id":"o1","text":"Superior vena cava syndrome","correct":true,"why":"Supported. A tumor blocks the SVC, so blood backs up in the head, neck and arms."},{"id":"o2","text":"Hypercalcemia","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A tumor blocks the SVC, so blood backs up in the head, neck and arms."},{"id":"o3","text":"Tumor lysis syndrome","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A tumor blocks the SVC, so blood backs up in the head, neck and arms."}]},{"id":"t-oncemerg:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Earliest symptom of spinal cord compression?","answer":"Back pain.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-oncemerg:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which emergency causes a tight shirt collar and facial swelling?","answer":"Superior vena cava syndrome.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"How infection happens and how it is found","cards":[{"title":"Labs and imaging","text":"WBC (normal about 5,000–10,000) with differential; a left shift (more bands) signals acute bacterial infection. · ESR and CRP (nonspecific markers of inflammation); procalcitonin (rises in bacterial infection). · Imaging (x-ray, CT, ultrasound) to find abscesses or pneumonia."},{"title":"Fever","text":"A fever helps fight infection; mild fever may not need treatment. · Treat high or uncomfortable fevers: antipyretics (acetaminophen, NSAIDs), fluids (fever raises fluid loss), light clothing, cooling blankets; avoid shivering (it raises temperature and oxygen use). · Older adults and immunosuppressed people may have little or no fever."},{"title":"Antibiotic therapy and stewardship","text":"Take the full course exactly as prescribed; don’t share or save antibiotics. · Watch for allergy, C. diff diarrhea, and organ toxicity; some drugs (vancomycin, aminoglycosides) need peak and trough levels. · Stewardship: use the narrowest effective antibiotic for the shortest time, to slow resistance."}],"caption":"The chain of infection, modes of transmission, cultures and labs, fever, and antibiotic stewardship."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"What is the single best way to break the chain of infection?","cue":"The chain of infection, modes of transmission, cultures and labs, fever, and antibiotic stewardship.","rule":"Hand hygiene.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-infect","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"How infection happens and how it is found","notes":["t-infect"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 19; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["An infection needs every link of the chain of infection: an agent (bacteria, virus, fungus, parasite), a reservoir, a portal of exit, a mode of transmission, a portal of entry and a susceptible host. Breaking any link prevents it, and hand hygiene breaks the most links. Health care–associated infections (HAIs) such as CLABSI, CAUTI, surgical site infections and C. diff are largely preventable.","Labs and imaging: WBC (normal about 5,000–10,000) with differential; a left shift (more bands) signals acute bacterial infection.; ESR and CRP (nonspecific markers of inflammation); procalcitonin (rises in bacterial infection).; Imaging (x-ray, CT, ultrasound) to find abscesses or pneumonia.","Fever: A fever helps fight infection; mild fever may not need treatment.; Treat high or uncomfortable fevers: antipyretics (acetaminophen, NSAIDs), fluids (fever raises fluid loss), light clothing, cooling blankets; avoid shivering (it raises temperature and oxygen use).; Older adults and immunosuppressed people may have little or no fever."],"terms":["Modes of transmission","Culture and sensitivity"],"termDefinitions":{"Modes of transmission":"Contact (direct touch, or indirect via objects and hands), droplet (large particles from coughing, sneezing and talking that travel only a short distance), airborne (tiny particles that stay in the air and travel far). The route decides which precautions and PPE stop it. Airborne examples: TB, measles, chickenpox, disseminated shingles. Droplet: influenza, pertussis, meningococcal meningitis, mumps. Contact: MRSA, VRE, C. diff, scabies, draining wounds.","Culture and sensitivity":"A sample (blood, urine, sputum, wound) is grown in the lab to identify the organism and test which antibiotics work. Antibiotics given first can kill organisms in the sample and give a false negative. Collect cultures before the first antibiotic dose, but don’t delay antibiotics in sepsis. Results take 24–72 hours; then the antibiotic may be changed."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-infect","title":"How infection happens and how it is found","blocks":[{"title":"Labs and imaging","lines":["WBC (normal about 5,000–10,000) with differential; a left shift (more bands) signals acute bacterial infection.","ESR and CRP (nonspecific markers of inflammation); procalcitonin (rises in bacterial infection).","Imaging (x-ray, CT, ultrasound) to find abscesses or pneumonia."]},{"title":"Fever","lines":["A fever helps fight infection; mild fever may not need treatment.","Treat high or uncomfortable fevers: antipyretics (acetaminophen, NSAIDs), fluids (fever raises fluid loss), light clothing, cooling blankets; avoid shivering (it raises temperature and oxygen use).","Older adults and immunosuppressed people may have little or no fever."]},{"title":"Antibiotic therapy and stewardship","lines":["Take the full course exactly as prescribed; don’t share or save antibiotics.","Watch for allergy, C. diff diarrhea, and organ toxicity; some drugs (vancomycin, aminoglycosides) need peak and trough levels.","Stewardship: use the narrowest effective antibiotic for the shortest time, to slow resistance."]},{"title":"Health equity (the COVID-19 lesson)","lines":["Black, Hispanic and Indigenous communities had higher COVID-19 infection, hospitalization and death rates, driven by social determinants: crowded housing, essential jobs that couldn’t be done from home, less access to care and testing, and chronic diseases linked to inequity.","Nurses address these through culturally appropriate teaching, access to testing and vaccines, and advocacy."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-infect:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient is ordered blood cultures and the first dose of IV ceftriaxone. What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Give the antibiotic, then draw cultures","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Cultures before antibiotics so the organism can grow; then start antibiotics without delay."},{"id":"o1","text":"Draw the cultures, then give the antibiotic promptly","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Cultures before antibiotics so the organism can grow; then start antibiotics without delay."},{"id":"o2","text":"Wait for culture results before starting antibiotics","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Cultures before antibiotics so the organism can grow; then start antibiotics without delay."},{"id":"o3","text":"Draw cultures 24 hours after antibiotics","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Cultures before antibiotics so the organism can grow; then start antibiotics without delay."}]},{"id":"t-infect:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What is the single best way to break the chain of infection?","answer":"Hand hygiene.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-infect:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"When are cultures collected?","answer":"Before the first antibiotic dose.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Standard and transmission-based precautions","cards":[{"title":"Hand hygiene","text":"Alcohol-based hand rub is preferred for routine care (faster and kills more germs), except: hands visibly soiled, after caring for C. diff or norovirus (spores and some viruses), and before eating or after the restroom: then soap and water for at least 15–20 seconds. · Keep nails short; no artificial nails for direct care."},{"title":"PPE order (CDC)","text":"Donning (on): gown → mask or respirator → goggles or face shield → gloves (gloves over the gown cuffs). · Doffing (off): gloves → goggles or face shield → gown → mask or respirator, then hand hygiene. (Another accepted way: remove gown and gloves together.) · Remove PPE at the doorway or in the anteroom; take off an N95 only after leaving the room and closing the door."},{"title":"Resistant organisms (MDROs)","text":"MRSA, VRE, CRE, and ESBL-producing bacteria resist many antibiotics, mainly because of overuse and misuse of antibiotics. · Prevention: hand hygiene, Contact Precautions, cleaning, removing catheters and lines as soon as possible, and antibiotic stewardship. · C. diff recurrences may be treated with fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)."}],"caption":"Standard, Contact, Droplet and Airborne Precautions; PPE order; hand hygiene; resistant organisms."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which precautions need a negative-pressure room and an N95?","cue":"Standard, Contact, Droplet and Airborne Precautions; PPE order; hand hygiene; resistant organisms.","rule":"Airborne (TB, measles, chickenpox, disseminated zoster).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-precautions","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"Standard and transmission-based precautions","notes":["t-precautions"],"book":"Ignatavicius · Chapter 19; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Standard Precautions are used for every patient, every time, because anyone may carry an infection: hand hygiene, PPE for expected contact with blood or body fluids, respiratory hygiene, safe injections and sharps safety. Transmission-based precautions are added for known or suspected infections spread by contact, droplets or air.","Hand hygiene: Alcohol-based hand rub is preferred for routine care (faster and kills more germs), except: hands visibly soiled, after caring for C. diff or norovirus (spores and some viruses), and before eating or after the restroom: then soap and water for at least 15–20 seconds.; Keep nails short; no artificial nails for direct care.","PPE order (CDC): Donning (on): gown → mask or respirator → goggles or face shield → gloves (gloves over the gown cuffs).; Doffing (off): gloves → goggles or face shield → gown → mask or respirator, then hand hygiene. (Another accepted way: remove gown and gloves together.); Remove PPE at the doorway or in the anteroom; take off an N95 only after leaving the room and closing the door.; The most contaminated items (gloves, the front of the gown and face shield) come off first; don’t touch the front."],"terms":["Contact Precautions","Droplet Precautions","Airborne Precautions"],"termDefinitions":{"Contact Precautions":"MRSA, VRE, CRE, C. diff, scabies, lice, draining wounds, RSV (in some settings). These spread by touch, directly or from contaminated surfaces and equipment. Gown and gloves on entering the room; private room or cohort with the same organism; dedicated equipment (stethoscope, BP cuff); clean surfaces often. For C. diff: soap and water handwashing and bleach cleaning.","Droplet Precautions":"Influenza, pertussis, meningococcal meningitis, mumps, rubella, Mycoplasma pneumonia, adenovirus. Large droplets travel only a short distance (about 3–6 feet) and fall. A surgical mask when working near the patient (put it on as you enter); private room (door may stay open); the patient wears a surgical mask when leaving the room.","Airborne Precautions":"TB, measles, chickenpox, disseminated zoster (and some emerging diseases). Tiny particles float on air currents for long periods and distances. An airborne infection isolation room (AIIR) with negative pressure, the door closed; a fit-tested N95 respirator or a PAPR; the patient wears a surgical mask for transport; staff immune to measles or chickenpox care for those patients."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-precautions","title":"Standard and transmission-based precautions","blocks":[{"title":"Hand hygiene","lines":["Alcohol-based hand rub is preferred for routine care (faster and kills more germs), except: hands visibly soiled, after caring for C. diff or norovirus (spores and some viruses), and before eating or after the restroom: then soap and water for at least 15–20 seconds.","Keep nails short; no artificial nails for direct care."]},{"title":"PPE order (CDC)","lines":["Donning (on): gown → mask or respirator → goggles or face shield → gloves (gloves over the gown cuffs).","Doffing (off): gloves → goggles or face shield → gown → mask or respirator, then hand hygiene. (Another accepted way: remove gown and gloves together.)","Remove PPE at the doorway or in the anteroom; take off an N95 only after leaving the room and closing the door.","The most contaminated items (gloves, the front of the gown and face shield) come off first; don’t touch the front."]},{"title":"Resistant organisms (MDROs)","lines":["MRSA, VRE, CRE, and ESBL-producing bacteria resist many antibiotics, mainly because of overuse and misuse of antibiotics.","Prevention: hand hygiene, Contact Precautions, cleaning, removing catheters and lines as soon as possible, and antibiotic stewardship.","C. diff recurrences may be treated with fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT)."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-precautions:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which item of PPE is removed first when leaving an isolation room?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Gown","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Gloves are the most contaminated (or they come off together with the gown)."},{"id":"o1","text":"Mask","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Gloves are the most contaminated (or they come off together with the gown)."},{"id":"o2","text":"Gloves","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Gloves are the most contaminated (or they come off together with the gown)."},{"id":"o3","text":"Goggles","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Gloves are the most contaminated (or they come off together with the gown)."}]},{"id":"t-precautions:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which precautions need a negative-pressure room and an N95?","answer":"Airborne (TB, measles, chickenpox, disseminated zoster).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-precautions:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What PPE goes on first, and what comes off first?","answer":"On first: gown. Off first: gloves.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Chemotherapy kills fast-dividing cells, including the bone marrow’s, so blood counts fall to their lowest point (the nadir), usually about 7–14 days after a dose. With neutropenia (ANC below 1,500; severe below 500), the usual signs of infection (pus, redness, swelling, infiltrates on x-ray) may be missing, because there are too few neutrophils to make them. A fever of 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher may be the only sign of a life-threatening infection. Also watch for chills, new tachycardia, low BP, confusion, mouth sores, and changes at IV or port sites."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: infection and risk for sepsis (get cultures and antibiotics in fast). Then risk for bleeding (platelets 62,000), fatigue, and anxiety."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Recheck temperature, VS, mental status and lactate; review culture results to narrow antibiotics; follow the daily ANC. She is ready for discharge when she is afebrile and her counts are recovering."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Day 9 after chemo for breast cancer: 38.4 °C, a little chilled, “otherwise fine.” ANC 300.","rule":"Priority: infection and risk for sepsis (get cultures and antibiotics in fast). Then risk for bleeding (platelets 62,000), fatigue, and anxiety.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-neutro","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Neutropenic fever after chemotherapy","notes":["c-neutro"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Fever with chills, tachycardia and an ANC of 300 at the nadir = febrile neutropenia, a medical emergency that can become sepsis within hours, even though she “feels fine.”","Priority: Priority: infection and risk for sepsis (get cultures and antibiotics in fast). Then risk for bleeding (platelets 62,000), fatigue, and anxiety.","Act: Draw cultures (blood from each line and a vein, urine, others as ordered) before the first antibiotic dose, then give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour; don’t delay antibiotics waiting for results. Monitor VS often for sepsis. Neutropenic precautions: hand hygiene (the most important), a private room, no one ill visiting, no fresh flowers, plants or standing water, dedicated equipment, a clean room. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories, enemas and unnecessary invasive procedures. Inspect the mouth, skin, perineum and lines every shift. Give growth factors as ordered (filgrastim, pegfilgrastim: bone pain is common).","Reassess: Recheck temperature, VS, mental status and lactate; review culture results to narrow antibiotics; follow the daily ANC. She is ready for discharge when she is afebrile and her counts are recovering."],"terms":["Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)","Nadir"],"termDefinitions":{"Absolute neutrophil count (ANC)":"WBC × (% segs + % bands) ÷ 100. Example: WBC 1,000 with 25% segs and 5% bands = ANC 300. Neutrophils are the first line against bacteria and fungi. Below 500: severe risk; any fever is an emergency.","Nadir":"The lowest count after a chemotherapy dose. Timing depends on the drug; marrow recovers over the next 1–2 weeks. Teach patients the days of highest risk; doses may be delayed until counts recover."},"patient":"52-year-old woman 9 days after her second cycle of chemotherapy for breast cancer. She calls the clinic: her temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), she had a chill, and she feels “a bit tired but otherwise fine.” In the ED: HR 108, BP 112/68, RR 20, no cough, no redness at her port. ANC 300/mm³, platelets 62,000/mm³, hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-neutro","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Chemotherapy kills fast-dividing cells, including the bone marrow’s, so blood counts fall to their lowest point (the nadir), usually about 7–14 days after a dose. With neutropenia (ANC below 1,500; severe below 500), the usual signs of infection (pus, redness, swelling, infiltrates on x-ray) may be missing, because there are too few neutrophils to make them. A fever of 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher may be the only sign of a life-threatening infection. Also watch for chills, new tachycardia, low BP, confusion, mouth sores, and changes at IV or port sites."]}]},{"id":"c-neutro","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Fever with chills, tachycardia and an ANC of 300 at the nadir = febrile neutropenia, a medical emergency that can become sepsis within hours, even though she “feels fine.”"]}]},{"id":"c-neutro","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: infection and risk for sepsis (get cultures and antibiotics in fast). Then risk for bleeding (platelets 62,000), fatigue, and anxiety."]}]},{"id":"c-neutro","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Antibiotics start within 60 minutes of arrival; she stays out of septic shock (BP stable, lactate normal, mental status clear); fever resolves; ANC recovers above 500 and then 1,500; she can describe neutropenic precautions at home."]}]},{"id":"c-neutro","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Draw cultures (blood from each line and a vein, urine, others as ordered) before the first antibiotic dose, then give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour; don’t delay antibiotics waiting for results. Monitor VS often for sepsis. Neutropenic precautions: hand hygiene (the most important), a private room, no one ill visiting, no fresh flowers, plants or standing water, dedicated equipment, a clean room. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories, enemas and unnecessary invasive procedures. Inspect the mouth, skin, perineum and lines every shift. Give growth factors as ordered (filgrastim, pegfilgrastim: bone pain is common)."]},{"title":"Teaching at home","lines":["Check temperature if you feel unwell and call right away for 38 °C (100.4 °F) or higher, chills or other signs of infection. Don’t take acetaminophen to hide a fever before calling.","Wash hands often; avoid crowds and sick people; no one changes your cat litter or birdcage without gloves (better: someone else does it).","Food safety: cook meat and eggs well; wash produce; avoid raw seafood and unpasteurized foods.","Good mouth care; a soft toothbrush; daily bathing; don’t share personal items."]}]},{"id":"c-neutro","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recheck temperature, VS, mental status and lactate; review culture results to narrow antibiotics; follow the daily ANC. She is ready for discharge when she is afebrile and her counts are recovering."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-neutro:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Nine days after chemotherapy, a patient has a temperature of 38.3 °C and an ANC of 400/mm³. What is the nurse’s priority?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Give acetaminophen and recheck in 4 hours","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Febrile neutropenia is an emergency. Cultures, then broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour."},{"id":"o1","text":"Obtain cultures and start IV antibiotics within 1 hour","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Febrile neutropenia is an emergency. Cultures, then broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour."},{"id":"o2","text":"Apply a cooling blanket","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Febrile neutropenia is an emergency. Cultures, then broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour."},{"id":"o3","text":"Encourage oral fluids and discharge","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Febrile neutropenia is an emergency. Cultures, then broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 hour."}]},{"id":"c-neutro:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"52-year-old woman 9 days after her second cycle of chemotherapy for breast cancer. She calls the clinic: her temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), she had a chill, and she feels “a bit tired but otherwise fine.” In the ED: HR 108, BP 112/68, RR 20, no cough, no redness at her port. ANC 300/mm³, platelets 62,000/mm³, hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: infection and risk for sepsis (get cultures and antibiotics in fast). Then risk for bleeding (platelets 62,000), fatigue, and anxiety.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-neutro:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"52-year-old woman 9 days after her second cycle of chemotherapy for breast cancer. She calls the clinic: her temperature is 38.4 °C (101.1 °F), she had a chill, and she feels “a bit tired but otherwise fine.” In the ED: HR 108, BP 112/68, RR 20, no cough, no redness at her port. ANC 300/mm³, platelets 62,000/mm³, hemoglobin 10.1 g/dL.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Draw cultures (blood from each line and a vein, urine, others as ordered) before the first antibiotic dose, then give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour; don’t delay antibiotics waiting for results. Monitor VS often for sepsis. Neutropenic precautions: hand hygiene (the most important), a private room, no one ill visiting, no fresh flowers, plants or standing water, dedicated equipment, a clean room. Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories, enemas and unnecessary invasive procedures. Inspect the mouth, skin, perineum and lines every shift. Give growth factors as ordered (filgrastim, pegfilgrastim: bone pain is common). Recheck temperature, VS, mental status and lactate; review culture results to narrow antibiotics; follow the daily ANC. She is ready for discharge when she is afebrile and her counts are recovering.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"When treatment kills a large number of cancer cells quickly, their contents flood the blood: potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (which become uric acid). Highest risk: leukemias and lymphomas, bulky or fast-growing tumors, high WBC, existing kidney problems, dehydration. It usually starts within 1–5 days of starting treatment. Cues: hyperkalemia (weakness, cramps, dysrhythmias, peaked T waves), hyperphosphatemia with hypocalcemia (tingling, muscle spasms, tetany, seizures), hyperuricemia, and acute kidney injury (falling urine output, rising creatinine)."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for dysrhythmia and cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia (decreased cardiac output). Then acute kidney injury (fluid and electrolyte imbalance), and injury from hypocalcemia (seizures, tetany)."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Recheck K⁺, phosphorus, calcium, uric acid and creatinine every 4–6 hours; check the ECG, hourly urine output, weight and lung sounds (fluid overload), and neuromuscular signs."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Day 2 after starting chemo for leukemia: muscle cramps, tingling lips, little urine, K⁺ 6.4.","rule":"Priority: risk for dysrhythmia and cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia (decreased cardiac output). Then acute kidney injury (fluid and electrolyte imbalance), and injury from hypocalcemia (seizures, tetany).","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-tls","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Tumor lysis syndrome","notes":["c-tls"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Day 2 of induction for leukemia with K⁺ 6.4 and peaked T waves, high phosphate, low calcium, high uric acid and oliguria with rising creatinine = tumor lysis syndrome with life-threatening hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury.","Priority: Priority: risk for dysrhythmia and cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia (decreased cardiac output). Then acute kidney injury (fluid and electrolyte imbalance), and injury from hypocalcemia (seizures, tetany).","Act: Continuous cardiac monitoring. Treat hyperkalemia as ordered: IV calcium (protects the heart), insulin with glucose (moves K⁺ into cells), a potassium binder, loop diuretic, and dialysis if severe. Aggressive IV hydration to flush the kidneys (watch for fluid overload). Rasburicase or allopurinol for uric acid; phosphate binders. Strict I&O and daily weights; labs every few hours; seizure precautions.","Reassess: Recheck K⁺, phosphorus, calcium, uric acid and creatinine every 4–6 hours; check the ECG, hourly urine output, weight and lung sounds (fluid overload), and neuromuscular signs."],"terms":["Why calcium falls","Uric acid kidney injury"],"termDefinitions":{"Why calcium falls":"Tingling lips, cramps, positive Chvostek or Trousseau signs. Excess phosphate binds calcium, and the calcium-phosphate crystals also damage the kidneys. Seizure precautions; replace calcium only if symptomatic (as ordered).","Uric acid kidney injury":"Dark, scant urine; rising creatinine. Uric acid crystallizes in the renal tubules and blocks them. Prevent with hydration, allopurinol (stops uric acid forming) or rasburicase (breaks it down)."},"patient":"19-year-old man with acute lymphoblastic leukemia (very high WBC), on day 2 of induction chemotherapy. He has muscle cramps, tingling around his mouth, nausea, and only 120 mL of dark urine in the last 8 hours. Labs: K⁺ 6.4 mEq/L, phosphorus 7.2 mg/dL, calcium 6.9 mg/dL, uric acid 13 mg/dL, creatinine 2.1 mg/dL (baseline 0.8). His monitor shows tall, peaked T waves.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-tls","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["When treatment kills a large number of cancer cells quickly, their contents flood the blood: potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (which become uric acid). Highest risk: leukemias and lymphomas, bulky or fast-growing tumors, high WBC, existing kidney problems, dehydration. It usually starts within 1–5 days of starting treatment. Cues: hyperkalemia (weakness, cramps, dysrhythmias, peaked T waves), hyperphosphatemia with hypocalcemia (tingling, muscle spasms, tetany, seizures), hyperuricemia, and acute kidney injury (falling urine output, rising creatinine)."]}]},{"id":"c-tls","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Day 2 of induction for leukemia with K⁺ 6.4 and peaked T waves, high phosphate, low calcium, high uric acid and oliguria with rising creatinine = tumor lysis syndrome with life-threatening hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury."]}]},{"id":"c-tls","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for dysrhythmia and cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia (decreased cardiac output). Then acute kidney injury (fluid and electrolyte imbalance), and injury from hypocalcemia (seizures, tetany)."]}]},{"id":"c-tls","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Potassium falls below 5.0 with normal T waves; urine output is at least 0.5 mL/kg/hour (often goals are 100 mL/hour or more); creatinine returns toward baseline; no seizures or tetany."]}]},{"id":"c-tls","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Continuous cardiac monitoring. Treat hyperkalemia as ordered: IV calcium (protects the heart), insulin with glucose (moves K⁺ into cells), a potassium binder, loop diuretic, and dialysis if severe. Aggressive IV hydration to flush the kidneys (watch for fluid overload). Rasburicase or allopurinol for uric acid; phosphate binders. Strict I&O and daily weights; labs every few hours; seizure precautions."]},{"title":"Prevention (before chemo starts)","lines":["IV hydration before and during treatment, starting 1–2 days ahead.","Allopurinol (or rasburicase for highest risk) before chemotherapy.","Teach: drink 2–3 L a day if allowed, and report less urine, cramps, weakness, palpitations or numbness."]}]},{"id":"c-tls","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recheck K⁺, phosphorus, calcium, uric acid and creatinine every 4–6 hours; check the ECG, hourly urine output, weight and lung sounds (fluid overload), and neuromuscular signs."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-tls:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which lab pattern indicates tumor lysis syndrome?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Low potassium, low phosphate, high calcium","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Dying cells release potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (uric acid); phosphate binds calcium."},{"id":"o1","text":"High potassium, high phosphate, low calcium, high uric acid","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Dying cells release potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (uric acid); phosphate binds calcium."},{"id":"o2","text":"Low sodium, high glucose","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Dying cells release potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (uric acid); phosphate binds calcium."},{"id":"o3","text":"High hemoglobin, low WBC","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Dying cells release potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids (uric acid); phosphate binds calcium."}]},{"id":"c-tls:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"19-year-old man with acute lymphoblastic leukemia (very high WBC), on day 2 of induction chemotherapy. He has muscle cramps, tingling around his mouth, nausea, and only 120 mL of dark urine in the last 8 hours. Labs: K⁺ 6.4 mEq/L, phosphorus 7.2 mg/dL, calcium 6.9 mg/dL, uric acid 13 mg/dL, creatinine 2.1 mg/dL (baseline 0.8). His monitor shows tall, peaked T waves.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for dysrhythmia and cardiac arrest from hyperkalemia (decreased cardiac output). Then acute kidney injury (fluid and electrolyte imbalance), and injury from hypocalcemia (seizures, tetany).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-tls:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"19-year-old man with acute lymphoblastic leukemia (very high WBC), on day 2 of induction chemotherapy. He has muscle cramps, tingling around his mouth, nausea, and only 120 mL of dark urine in the last 8 hours. Labs: K⁺ 6.4 mEq/L, phosphorus 7.2 mg/dL, calcium 6.9 mg/dL, uric acid 13 mg/dL, creatinine 2.1 mg/dL (baseline 0.8). His monitor shows tall, peaked T waves.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Continuous cardiac monitoring. Treat hyperkalemia as ordered: IV calcium (protects the heart), insulin with glucose (moves K⁺ into cells), a potassium binder, loop diuretic, and dialysis if severe. Aggressive IV hydration to flush the kidneys (watch for fluid overload). Rasburicase or allopurinol for uric acid; phosphate binders. Strict I&O and daily weights; labs every few hours; seizure precautions. Recheck K⁺, phosphorus, calcium, uric acid and creatinine every 4–6 hours; check the ECG, hourly urine output, weight and lung sounds (fluid overload), and neuromuscular signs.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"A tumor (usually a metastasis to the spine: from breast, lung, prostate, kidney cancer, myeloma or lymphoma) presses on the spinal cord. The first symptom in most patients is back pain, often worse lying down, with movement, coughing or straining. Later: weakness of the legs, sensory loss (numbness, tingling, a “level” of lost feeling), and bowel and bladder dysfunction (retention, incontinence, constipation)."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for permanent paralysis (protect neurological function: treatment within hours). Then urinary retention, acute pain, risk for falls, and skin breakdown from immobility."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Compare leg strength, sensation, bladder and bowel function and pain with the last check every few hours. Any decline goes to the provider immediately."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Prostate cancer with bone metastases: back pain for 2 weeks, now legs feel heavy.","rule":"Priority: risk for permanent paralysis (protect neurological function: treatment within hours). Then urinary retention, acute pain, risk for falls, and skin breakdown from immobility.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-scc","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Spinal cord compression","notes":["c-scc"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Worsening back pain, new leg weakness, a sensory level and urinary retention in a man with spinal metastases = spinal cord compression, a neurological emergency: the outcome depends on how much function he has when treatment starts.","Priority: Priority: risk for permanent paralysis (protect neurological function: treatment within hours). Then urinary retention, acute pain, risk for falls, and skin breakdown from immobility.","Act: Report immediately; prepare for an urgent MRI. Give high-dose corticosteroids (dexamethasone) as ordered to reduce swelling around the cord, then radiation and/or surgery (decompression). Neuro checks (strength, sensation, bowel and bladder) often; fall precautions; catheterize for retention; pain control; bowel program; turn and protect the skin; keep the spine aligned as ordered.","Reassess: Compare leg strength, sensation, bladder and bowel function and pain with the last check every few hours. Any decline goes to the provider immediately."],"terms":["New back pain in a patient with cancer"],"termDefinitions":{"New back pain in a patient with cancer":"Back pain that keeps getting worse, or wakes the person at night. It may be the only early sign of a tumor growing into the spinal canal. Report it promptly. Waiting until weakness appears risks permanent paralysis."},"patient":"70-year-old man with prostate cancer that has spread to his bones. He has had steadily worsening mid-back pain for 2 weeks that is worse lying down and when he coughs. Today his legs “feel heavy,” he stumbled twice, and he has numbness around his waist. He hasn’t urinated since this morning.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-scc","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["A tumor (usually a metastasis to the spine: from breast, lung, prostate, kidney cancer, myeloma or lymphoma) presses on the spinal cord. The first symptom in most patients is back pain, often worse lying down, with movement, coughing or straining. Later: weakness of the legs, sensory loss (numbness, tingling, a “level” of lost feeling), and bowel and bladder dysfunction (retention, incontinence, constipation)."]}]},{"id":"c-scc","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Worsening back pain, new leg weakness, a sensory level and urinary retention in a man with spinal metastases = spinal cord compression, a neurological emergency: the outcome depends on how much function he has when treatment starts."]}]},{"id":"c-scc","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for permanent paralysis (protect neurological function: treatment within hours). Then urinary retention, acute pain, risk for falls, and skin breakdown from immobility."]}]},{"id":"c-scc","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Treatment begins within hours; leg strength and sensation stop declining (and may improve); his bladder is emptied and pain is controlled; no falls or pressure injuries."]}]},{"id":"c-scc","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Report immediately; prepare for an urgent MRI. Give high-dose corticosteroids (dexamethasone) as ordered to reduce swelling around the cord, then radiation and/or surgery (decompression). Neuro checks (strength, sensation, bowel and bladder) often; fall precautions; catheterize for retention; pain control; bowel program; turn and protect the skin; keep the spine aligned as ordered."]},{"title":"Other oncologic emergencies at a glance","lines":["Superior vena cava syndrome: a tumor (lung cancer, lymphoma) blocks the SVC: facial and neck swelling (worse in the morning), puffy eyes, a tight shirt collar, distended neck and chest veins, arm swelling; late: stridor and confusion. Keep the head up, give oxygen, prepare for radiation or a stent.","Hypercalcemia: bone metastases or tumor hormones: fatigue, weakness, constipation, confusion, polyuria and dehydration, ECG changes. Treat with IV normal saline, then diuretics, calcitonin, bisphosphonates (zoledronic acid) or denosumab; encourage walking.","SIADH: a tumor (often small cell lung cancer) makes ADH: water retention with dilutional hyponatremia (headache, confusion, seizures), concentrated urine, weight gain. Fluid restriction, daily weights, seizure precautions; hypertonic saline or ADH blockers if severe.","Sepsis and DIC: neutropenia allows infection; DIC = widespread clotting that uses up platelets and clotting factors, then bleeding (oozing from IV sites, petechiae, low platelets and fibrinogen, high D-dimer). Treat the cause; replace blood products."]}]},{"id":"c-scc","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Compare leg strength, sensation, bladder and bowel function and pain with the last check every few hours. Any decline goes to the provider immediately."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-scc:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"70-year-old man with prostate cancer that has spread to his bones. He has had steadily worsening mid-back pain for 2 weeks that is worse lying down and when he coughs. Today his legs “feel heavy,” he stumbled twice, and he has numbness around his waist. He hasn’t urinated since this morning.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for permanent paralysis (protect neurological function: treatment within hours). Then urinary retention, acute pain, risk for falls, and skin breakdown from immobility.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-scc:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"70-year-old man with prostate cancer that has spread to his bones. He has had steadily worsening mid-back pain for 2 weeks that is worse lying down and when he coughs. Today his legs “feel heavy,” he stumbled twice, and he has numbness around his waist. He hasn’t urinated since this morning.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Report immediately; prepare for an urgent MRI. Give high-dose corticosteroids (dexamethasone) as ordered to reduce swelling around the cord, then radiation and/or surgery (decompression). Neuro checks (strength, sensation, bowel and bladder) often; fall precautions; catheterize for retention; pain control; bowel program; turn and protect the skin; keep the spine aligned as ordered. Compare leg strength, sensation, bladder and bowel function and pain with the last check every few hours. Any decline goes to the provider immediately.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Anaphylaxis is the most severe type I (IgE-mediated) hypersensitivity reaction: mast cells and basophils release histamine and other mediators all over the body within minutes of exposure (drugs such as antibiotics, contrast dye, latex, insect stings, foods such as peanuts and shellfish). Early cues: a feeling of “impending doom,” anxiety, itching, flushing, hives, a lump in the throat. Then airway signs (hoarseness, stridor, wheezing, swelling of lips, tongue, throat) and circulatory signs (hypotension, tachycardia, weak pulse, dizziness), plus GI cramps, vomiting and diarrhea."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: airway (ineffective airway clearance from laryngeal edema and bronchospasm), then breathing (impaired gas exchange), then circulation (decreased cardiac output from shock). Anxiety comes later."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Reassess airway sounds, SpO₂, BP and HR every few minutes after each dose. Watch her for several hours (and up to 24) for a biphasic reaction. Document the allergy and update her allergy band."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Ten minutes into IV ceftriaxone: itchy, hoarse, wheezing, BP 82/50.","rule":"Priority: airway (ineffective airway clearance from laryngeal edema and bronchospasm), then breathing (impaired gas exchange), then circulation (decreased cardiac output from shock). Anxiety comes later.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-anaphylaxis","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Anaphylaxis","notes":["c-anaphylaxis"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Hives, lip swelling, hoarseness, wheezing, hypotension and hypoxemia within minutes of a new IV antibiotic = anaphylaxis with airway compromise and shock. It can kill within minutes.","Priority: Priority: airway (ineffective airway clearance from laryngeal edema and bronchospasm), then breathing (impaired gas exchange), then circulation (decreased cardiac output from shock). Anxiety comes later.","Act: Stop the trigger (stop the infusion but keep the IV access). Call the rapid response team. Assess the airway. Give epinephrine IM first: 0.3–0.5 mg of 1 mg/mL in the anterolateral thigh; repeat every 5–15 minutes as needed. High-flow oxygen (100% by non-rebreather); be ready for intubation or an emergency airway. Position: lying flat with legs raised for shock, or sitting up if she struggles to breathe. IV normal saline bolus. Then the add-on drugs: diphenhydramine (H1 blocker), an H2 blocker, inhaled albuterol for wheezing, and corticosteroids (to prevent a late reaction).","Reassess: Reassess airway sounds, SpO₂, BP and HR every few minutes after each dose. Watch her for several hours (and up to 24) for a biphasic reaction. Document the allergy and update her allergy band.","Suspected anaphylaxis needs immediate emergency help and prompt IM epinephrine under protocol. Airway and breathing support proceed together; antihistamines do not replace epinephrine. Avoid having the patient stand or walk during the reaction."],"terms":["Stridor and hoarseness","Hypotension and tachycardia"],"termDefinitions":{"Stridor and hoarseness":"A high-pitched sound on breathing in; her voice changes. Laryngeal edema: histamine makes capillaries leak, and the upper airway swells. The airway is closing. It is the most urgent finding; act now.","Hypotension and tachycardia":"BP falls; HR climbs; skin may be flushed, then pale and cool. Massive vasodilation and fluid leaking out of vessels = distributive shock. Epinephrine, a fluid bolus, and lie her flat with legs raised if breathing allows."},"patient":"45-year-old woman admitted with pneumonia. Ten minutes after IV ceftriaxone is started she says her skin is itchy and “my throat feels tight.” She has hives on her chest, a hoarse voice, audible wheezing and swollen lips. HR 128, BP 82/50, RR 28, SpO₂ 89%. She takes metoprolol for hypertension. She is anxious and says, “Something terrible is happening.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-anaphylaxis","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Anaphylaxis is the most severe type I (IgE-mediated) hypersensitivity reaction: mast cells and basophils release histamine and other mediators all over the body within minutes of exposure (drugs such as antibiotics, contrast dye, latex, insect stings, foods such as peanuts and shellfish). Early cues: a feeling of “impending doom,” anxiety, itching, flushing, hives, a lump in the throat. Then airway signs (hoarseness, stridor, wheezing, swelling of lips, tongue, throat) and circulatory signs (hypotension, tachycardia, weak pulse, dizziness), plus GI cramps, vomiting and diarrhea."]},{"title":"Who is at higher risk of a bad outcome","lines":["People on beta blockers (epinephrine works less well; glucagon can help), and on ACE inhibitors.","People with asthma or heart disease, and older adults.","A biphasic reaction: symptoms can come back hours later without new exposure."]}]},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Hives, lip swelling, hoarseness, wheezing, hypotension and hypoxemia within minutes of a new IV antibiotic = anaphylaxis with airway compromise and shock. It can kill within minutes."]}]},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: airway (ineffective airway clearance from laryngeal edema and bronchospasm), then breathing (impaired gas exchange), then circulation (decreased cardiac output from shock). Anxiety comes later."]}]},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Within minutes: the airway stays open (no stridor), SpO₂ is 95% or higher, SBP is above 90 with HR falling, hives and swelling fade. Later: no biphasic recurrence, and she can name the drug she must avoid and how to report it."]}]},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Stop the trigger (stop the infusion but keep the IV access). Call the rapid response team. Assess the airway. Give epinephrine IM first: 0.3–0.5 mg of 1 mg/mL in the anterolateral thigh; repeat every 5–15 minutes as needed. High-flow oxygen (100% by non-rebreather); be ready for intubation or an emergency airway. Position: lying flat with legs raised for shock, or sitting up if she struggles to breathe. IV normal saline bolus. Then the add-on drugs: diphenhydramine (H1 blocker), an H2 blocker, inhaled albuterol for wheezing, and corticosteroids (to prevent a late reaction)."]},{"title":"Why epinephrine comes first","lines":["It constricts vessels (raises BP, reduces swelling), opens the airways (beta-2) and stabilizes mast cells.","Antihistamines and steroids don’t reverse airway swelling or shock; they are add-ons, never a substitute."]},{"title":"Teaching for home","lines":["Avoid the allergen; tell every provider; wear a medical alert bracelet.","Carry two epinephrine auto-injectors at all times; use one at the first sign of a reaction, into the outer thigh (it works through clothing), then call 911: a second dose or care may be needed.","Check the expiration date; keep at room temperature."]}]},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Reassess airway sounds, SpO₂, BP and HR every few minutes after each dose. Watch her for several hours (and up to 24) for a biphasic reaction. Document the allergy and update her allergy band."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Suspected anaphylaxis needs immediate emergency help and prompt IM epinephrine under protocol. Airway and breathing support proceed together; antihistamines do not replace epinephrine. Avoid having the patient stand or walk during the reaction.","sources":[{"title":"Resuscitation Council UK: emergency treatment of anaphylaxis","url":"https://www.resus.org.uk/library/additional-guidance/guidance-anaphylaxis/emergency-treatment"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-anaphylaxis:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient receiving IV penicillin develops hives, hoarseness and wheezing. After stopping the infusion, which drug should the nurse give first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Diphenhydramine IV","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Epinephrine is first-line: it reverses airway swelling, bronchospasm and shock. The others are add-ons."},{"id":"o1","text":"Epinephrine IM","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Epinephrine is first-line: it reverses airway swelling, bronchospasm and shock. The others are add-ons."},{"id":"o2","text":"Methylprednisolone IV","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Epinephrine is first-line: it reverses airway swelling, bronchospasm and shock. The others are add-ons."},{"id":"o3","text":"Albuterol nebulizer","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Epinephrine is first-line: it reverses airway swelling, bronchospasm and shock. The others are add-ons."}]},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"45-year-old woman admitted with pneumonia. Ten minutes after IV ceftriaxone is started she says her skin is itchy and “my throat feels tight.” She has hives on her chest, a hoarse voice, audible wheezing and swollen lips. HR 128, BP 82/50, RR 28, SpO₂ 89%. She takes metoprolol for hypertension. She is anxious and says, “Something terrible is happening.”","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: airway (ineffective airway clearance from laryngeal edema and bronchospasm), then breathing (impaired gas exchange), then circulation (decreased cardiac output from shock). Anxiety comes later.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-anaphylaxis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"45-year-old woman admitted with pneumonia. Ten minutes after IV ceftriaxone is started she says her skin is itchy and “my throat feels tight.” She has hives on her chest, a hoarse voice, audible wheezing and swollen lips. HR 128, BP 82/50, RR 28, SpO₂ 89%. She takes metoprolol for hypertension. She is anxious and says, “Something terrible is happening.”","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Stop the trigger (stop the infusion but keep the IV access). Call the rapid response team. Assess the airway. Give epinephrine IM first: 0.3–0.5 mg of 1 mg/mL in the anterolateral thigh; repeat every 5–15 minutes as needed. High-flow oxygen (100% by non-rebreather); be ready for intubation or an emergency airway. Position: lying flat with legs raised for shock, or sitting up if she struggles to breathe. IV normal saline bolus. Then the add-on drugs: diphenhydramine (H1 blocker), an H2 blocker, inhaled albuterol for wheezing, and corticosteroids (to prevent a late reaction). Reassess airway sounds, SpO₂, BP and HR every few minutes after each dose. Watch her for several hours (and up to 24) for a biphasic reaction. Document the allergy and update her allergy band.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Angioedema is deep swelling of the skin and mucous membranes, usually the lips, tongue, face, eyelids, throat, and sometimes the bowel. ACE inhibitors (the “-prils”) are a leading cause: they block the breakdown of bradykinin, which builds up and makes vessels leak. It can start any time, even after years on the drug, and is more common in Black patients. ACE-inhibitor angioedema usually has no hives or itching (not histamine). Watch for airway signs: drooling, trouble swallowing, a muffled or hoarse voice, stridor."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for airway obstruction (ineffective airway clearance). Then anxiety, and teaching to prevent recurrence."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Recheck voice, swallowing, drooling, stridor and SpO₂ often until the swelling is clearly going down. Confirm the allergy is charted and his new BP drug is not an ACE inhibitor."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"On lisinopril for 3 years. Woke with a huge lower lip and swollen tongue; no hives.","rule":"Priority: risk for airway obstruction (ineffective airway clearance). Then anxiety, and teaching to prevent recurrence.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-angio","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Angioedema from an ACE inhibitor","notes":["c-angio"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Growing lip and tongue swelling with drooling and a muffled voice in a man on lisinopril, with no hives = ACE-inhibitor angioedema with risk of airway obstruction.","Priority: Priority: risk for airway obstruction (ineffective airway clearance). Then anxiety, and teaching to prevent recurrence.","Act: Assess the airway continuously and keep him sitting upright; give oxygen and keep suction and intubation (or emergency airway) equipment ready, and call the provider and anesthesia early (intubation gets harder as swelling grows). Stop the ACE inhibitor. Give drugs as ordered: diphenhydramine, corticosteroids and epinephrine are often tried; specific options for bradykinin swelling (such as icatibant or C1 esterase inhibitor) may be used. Stay with him.","Reassess: Recheck voice, swallowing, drooling, stridor and SpO₂ often until the swelling is clearly going down. Confirm the allergy is charted and his new BP drug is not an ACE inhibitor."],"terms":["Bradykinin vs histamine swelling"],"termDefinitions":{"Bradykinin vs histamine swelling":"Swelling without hives or itching, slower to build and slower to go away. Bradykinin, not histamine, causes it, so antihistamines and epinephrine help less. The airway is the priority; stop the drug; some patients need intubation (often with fiberoptic tools)."},"patient":"58-year-old Black man who has taken lisinopril for 3 years. He woke up with a swollen lower lip and tongue that has grown over 2 hours. He is drooling a little and his voice is muffled. No hives, no itching, no new foods or drugs. RR 22, SpO₂ 95%, BP 150/92.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-angio","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Angioedema is deep swelling of the skin and mucous membranes, usually the lips, tongue, face, eyelids, throat, and sometimes the bowel. ACE inhibitors (the “-prils”) are a leading cause: they block the breakdown of bradykinin, which builds up and makes vessels leak. It can start any time, even after years on the drug, and is more common in Black patients. ACE-inhibitor angioedema usually has no hives or itching (not histamine). Watch for airway signs: drooling, trouble swallowing, a muffled or hoarse voice, stridor."]}]},{"id":"c-angio","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Growing lip and tongue swelling with drooling and a muffled voice in a man on lisinopril, with no hives = ACE-inhibitor angioedema with risk of airway obstruction."]}]},{"id":"c-angio","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for airway obstruction (ineffective airway clearance). Then anxiety, and teaching to prevent recurrence."]}]},{"id":"c-angio","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The airway stays open and SpO₂ stays 95% or above; the swelling stops growing and then goes down over hours to days; he never takes an ACE inhibitor again and knows to tell every provider."]}]},{"id":"c-angio","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Assess the airway continuously and keep him sitting upright; give oxygen and keep suction and intubation (or emergency airway) equipment ready, and call the provider and anesthesia early (intubation gets harder as swelling grows). Stop the ACE inhibitor. Give drugs as ordered: diphenhydramine, corticosteroids and epinephrine are often tried; specific options for bradykinin swelling (such as icatibant or C1 esterase inhibitor) may be used. Stay with him."]},{"title":"Teaching","lines":["Never take any ACE inhibitor again (all “-prils”); record it as an allergy in the chart.","Use ARBs cautiously (a small cross-risk), only if the provider decides.","Seek emergency care for any lip, tongue or throat swelling."]}]},{"id":"c-angio","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recheck voice, swallowing, drooling, stridor and SpO₂ often until the swelling is clearly going down. Confirm the allergy is charted and his new BP drug is not an ACE inhibitor."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-angio:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"58-year-old Black man who has taken lisinopril for 3 years. He woke up with a swollen lower lip and tongue that has grown over 2 hours. He is drooling a little and his voice is muffled. No hives, no itching, no new foods or drugs. RR 22, SpO₂ 95%, BP 150/92.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for airway obstruction (ineffective airway clearance). Then anxiety, and teaching to prevent recurrence.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-angio:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"58-year-old Black man who has taken lisinopril for 3 years. He woke up with a swollen lower lip and tongue that has grown over 2 hours. He is drooling a little and his voice is muffled. No hives, no itching, no new foods or drugs. RR 22, SpO₂ 95%, BP 150/92.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Assess the airway continuously and keep him sitting upright; give oxygen and keep suction and intubation (or emergency airway) equipment ready, and call the provider and anesthesia early (intubation gets harder as swelling grows). Stop the ACE inhibitor. Give drugs as ordered: diphenhydramine, corticosteroids and epinephrine are often tried; specific options for bradykinin swelling (such as icatibant or C1 esterase inhibitor) may be used. Stay with him. Recheck voice, swallowing, drooling, stridor and SpO₂ often until the swelling is clearly going down. Confirm the allergy is charted and his new BP drug is not an ACE inhibitor.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"SLE is a chronic autoimmune disease: the body makes autoantibodies against its own cells, and immune complexes deposit in tissues (a type III reaction), causing inflammation in the skin, joints, kidneys, heart, lungs, blood and brain. It is most common in women of childbearing age and more common and severe in Black, Hispanic, Asian and Indigenous women. It flares and remits. Look for the butterfly (malar) rash, photosensitivity, joint pain, fatigue, fever, mouth ulcers, hair loss, Raynaud’s, and signs of organ damage."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for kidney injury (and fluid excess). Then acute pain and fatigue, risk for infection (the disease and its drugs suppress immunity), impaired skin integrity, and body image."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Track weight, BP, edema, urine protein and creatinine, joint pain and fatigue scores, skin, temperature and signs of infection. Check that she knows sun protection and the warning signs to report."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Butterfly rash, aching joints, fatigue, and now swollen ankles and foamy urine.","rule":"Priority: risk for kidney injury (and fluid excess). Then acute pain and fatigue, risk for infection (the disease and its drugs suppress immunity), impaired skin integrity, and body image.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-sle","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)","notes":["c-sle"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Malar rash, arthritis, fever and fatigue after sun exposure = an SLE flare; new edema, proteinuria, hypertension and rising creatinine = probable lupus nephritis.","Priority: Priority: risk for kidney injury (and fluid excess). Then acute pain and fatigue, risk for infection (the disease and its drugs suppress immunity), impaired skin integrity, and body image.","Act: Give drugs as ordered and teach them: hydroxychloroquine (an antimalarial and the backbone of treatment; needs regular eye exams for retinal damage), NSAIDs for joint pain, corticosteroids for flares, and immunosuppressants (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, belimumab) for organ involvement. Balance rest and activity; protect the skin; prevent infection.","Reassess: Track weight, BP, edema, urine protein and creatinine, joint pain and fatigue scores, skin, temperature and signs of infection. Check that she knows sun protection and the warning signs to report."],"terms":["Lupus nephritis","Serositis"],"termDefinitions":{"Lupus nephritis":"Swelling, foamy urine (protein), blood in urine, rising BP and creatinine, weight gain. Immune complexes inflame the glomeruli. A leading cause of death in SLE (kidney failure). Report it; track daily weight, I&O, BP and labs.","Serositis":"Sharp chest pain worse when lying down or breathing in; a friction rub. Inflammation of the pericardium (pericarditis) or pleura. Report new chest pain; heart disease is the other major cause of death."},"patient":"29-year-old woman diagnosed with SLE 2 years ago, on hydroxychloroquine. After a beach vacation she has a red rash across her cheeks and nose, painful swollen hand joints, extreme fatigue and a low-grade fever. Today her ankles are puffy, her urine is foamy, BP is 148/94 and she has gained 2 kg in a week. She asks whether she can get pregnant.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-sle","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["SLE is a chronic autoimmune disease: the body makes autoantibodies against its own cells, and immune complexes deposit in tissues (a type III reaction), causing inflammation in the skin, joints, kidneys, heart, lungs, blood and brain. It is most common in women of childbearing age and more common and severe in Black, Hispanic, Asian and Indigenous women. It flares and remits. Look for the butterfly (malar) rash, photosensitivity, joint pain, fatigue, fever, mouth ulcers, hair loss, Raynaud’s, and signs of organ damage."]},{"title":"Labs","lines":["ANA positive in almost everyone (but not specific); anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith antibodies are more specific.","Low complement levels, raised ESR during flares; low blood counts (anemia, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia).","Urinalysis (protein, cells), creatinine."]}]},{"id":"c-sle","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Malar rash, arthritis, fever and fatigue after sun exposure = an SLE flare; new edema, proteinuria, hypertension and rising creatinine = probable lupus nephritis."]}]},{"id":"c-sle","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for kidney injury (and fluid excess). Then acute pain and fatigue, risk for infection (the disease and its drugs suppress immunity), impaired skin integrity, and body image."]}]},{"id":"c-sle","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Kidney function stabilizes (protein falls, BP and weight controlled); joint pain and fatigue are manageable; she avoids sun and infection; she plans pregnancy with her team when the disease has been quiet for at least 6 months."]}]},{"id":"c-sle","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Give drugs as ordered and teach them: hydroxychloroquine (an antimalarial and the backbone of treatment; needs regular eye exams for retinal damage), NSAIDs for joint pain, corticosteroids for flares, and immunosuppressants (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, belimumab) for organ involvement. Balance rest and activity; protect the skin; prevent infection."]},{"title":"Teaching","lines":["Avoid sun: protective clothing, broad-spectrum sunscreen (SPF 30+), avoid midday sun (UV triggers flares).","Report fever or other signs of infection right away (immunosuppressants mask and raise risk); hand hygiene; vaccines (no live vaccines on immunosuppressants).","Don’t stop steroids suddenly (adrenal crisis).","Pregnancy is high-risk: plan it when the disease is quiet; some drugs (methotrexate, mycophenolate) cause birth defects.","Mild soap, no harsh skin products; cover mouth ulcers with gentle oral care."]}]},{"id":"c-sle","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Track weight, BP, edema, urine protein and creatinine, joint pain and fatigue scores, skin, temperature and signs of infection. Check that she knows sun protection and the warning signs to report."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-sle:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which teaching is most important for a patient with SLE?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Spend time in the sun to boost vitamin D","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. UV light triggers flares. Steroids are never stopped abruptly; balanced activity is encouraged."},{"id":"o1","text":"Avoid sun exposure and use broad-spectrum sunscreen","correct":true,"why":"Supported. UV light triggers flares. Steroids are never stopped abruptly; balanced activity is encouraged."},{"id":"o2","text":"Stop prednisone abruptly once the rash fades","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. UV light triggers flares. Steroids are never stopped abruptly; balanced activity is encouraged."},{"id":"o3","text":"Avoid all exercise","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. UV light triggers flares. Steroids are never stopped abruptly; balanced activity is encouraged."}]},{"id":"c-sle:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"29-year-old woman diagnosed with SLE 2 years ago, on hydroxychloroquine. After a beach vacation she has a red rash across her cheeks and nose, painful swollen hand joints, extreme fatigue and a low-grade fever. Today her ankles are puffy, her urine is foamy, BP is 148/94 and she has gained 2 kg in a week. She asks whether she can get pregnant.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for kidney injury (and fluid excess). Then acute pain and fatigue, risk for infection (the disease and its drugs suppress immunity), impaired skin integrity, and body image.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-sle:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"29-year-old woman diagnosed with SLE 2 years ago, on hydroxychloroquine. After a beach vacation she has a red rash across her cheeks and nose, painful swollen hand joints, extreme fatigue and a low-grade fever. Today her ankles are puffy, her urine is foamy, BP is 148/94 and she has gained 2 kg in a week. She asks whether she can get pregnant.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Give drugs as ordered and teach them: hydroxychloroquine (an antimalarial and the backbone of treatment; needs regular eye exams for retinal damage), NSAIDs for joint pain, corticosteroids for flares, and immunosuppressants (methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, belimumab) for organ involvement. Balance rest and activity; protect the skin; prevent infection. Track weight, BP, edema, urine protein and creatinine, joint pain and fatigue scores, skin, temperature and signs of infection. Check that she knows sun protection and the warning signs to report.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"HIV is a retrovirus that infects and destroys CD4+ T cells (helper T cells), the coordinators of immunity. It spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk (sex, shared needles, birth or breastfeeding, rarely needlesticks), not saliva, tears, sweat, hugging or sharing dishes. Stages: acute HIV infection (flu-like illness 2–4 weeks after infection; very high viral load and very infectious), chronic HIV (few symptoms for years), and AIDS: CD4 below 200 cells/mm³ or an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection or cancer. Look for weight loss, thrush, fever, diarrhea, night sweats, cough and changes in thinking."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: impaired gas exchange (PJP). Then risk for infection (other opportunistic infections), imbalanced nutrition, activity intolerance, and the barriers that stopped his treatment (cost, stigma, depression)."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Track SpO₂, RR and temperature, weight, viral load (should become undetectable) and CD4 (should rise), side effects, adherence, and whether cost and support barriers were solved before discharge."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"CD4 140, stopped his pills months ago; dry cough, fever and breathless walking to the bathroom.","rule":"Priority: impaired gas exchange (PJP). Then risk for infection (other opportunistic infections), imbalanced nutrition, activity intolerance, and the barriers that stopped his treatment (cost, stigma, depression).","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-hiv","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"HIV and AIDS","notes":["c-hiv"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: CD4 140, off treatment, with dry cough, fever, exertional hypoxemia, diffuse infiltrates and thrush = AIDS with probable Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP).","Priority: Priority: impaired gas exchange (PJP). Then risk for infection (other opportunistic infections), imbalanced nutrition, activity intolerance, and the barriers that stopped his treatment (cost, stigma, depression).","Act: Oxygen, rest periods and position for breathing; treat PJP (TMP-SMX, with corticosteroids for severe hypoxemia). Use Standard Precautions (HIV needs no special isolation). Restart combination antiretroviral therapy (cART): several drugs from different classes, taken every day on schedule to prevent resistance. Prevent infection: hand hygiene, food safety (no raw eggs, meat or seafood; wash produce), avoid cat litter and bird cages, avoid sick contacts. Offer nutrition support, mouth care, and nonjudgmental support for stigma and depression.","Reassess: Track SpO₂, RR and temperature, weight, viral load (should become undetectable) and CD4 (should rise), side effects, adherence, and whether cost and support barriers were solved before discharge."],"terms":["CD4 count and viral load","Opportunistic infection"],"termDefinitions":{"CD4 count and viral load":"CD4 is normally about 500–1,500 cells/mm³; viral load is the amount of HIV RNA in the blood. The CD4 count shows how much immunity is left; viral load shows how fast HIV is multiplying and how well treatment works. Treatment aims for an undetectable viral load; falling CD4 means rising risk of opportunistic infections.","Opportunistic infection":"PJP pneumonia, thrush, CMV retinitis (blurred vision, floaters), toxoplasmosis (headache, confusion, seizures), MAC, cryptococcal meningitis, TB. Organisms a healthy immune system controls cause disease when CD4 cells are few. Prophylaxis by CD4 level: TMP-SMX below 200 for PJP (it also covers toxoplasmosis below about 100)."},"patient":"36-year-old man with HIV who stopped his antiretroviral therapy 8 months ago after losing his job and insurance. For 3 weeks he has had a dry cough, fever and shortness of breath that gets worse on exertion. He has white patches in his mouth and has lost 7 kg. SpO₂ 88% on room air after walking, RR 26. His CD4 count is 140 cells/mm³ and his viral load is high.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-hiv","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["HIV is a retrovirus that infects and destroys CD4+ T cells (helper T cells), the coordinators of immunity. It spreads through blood, semen, vaginal fluid and breast milk (sex, shared needles, birth or breastfeeding, rarely needlesticks), not saliva, tears, sweat, hugging or sharing dishes. Stages: acute HIV infection (flu-like illness 2–4 weeks after infection; very high viral load and very infectious), chronic HIV (few symptoms for years), and AIDS: CD4 below 200 cells/mm³ or an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection or cancer. Look for weight loss, thrush, fever, diarrhea, night sweats, cough and changes in thinking."]},{"title":"AIDS-related cancers and other problems","lines":["Kaposi sarcoma (purple skin or mouth lesions), lymphomas, cervical cancer.","Wasting syndrome, chronic diarrhea, HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder."]}]},{"id":"c-hiv","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["CD4 140, off treatment, with dry cough, fever, exertional hypoxemia, diffuse infiltrates and thrush = AIDS with probable Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PJP)."]}]},{"id":"c-hiv","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: impaired gas exchange (PJP). Then risk for infection (other opportunistic infections), imbalanced nutrition, activity intolerance, and the barriers that stopped his treatment (cost, stigma, depression)."]}]},{"id":"c-hiv","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["SpO₂ is 92% or above at rest and with activity; fever resolves; he restarts cART with a plan to take it daily, reaching an undetectable viral load within months; he regains weight and has affordable access to his drugs."]}]},{"id":"c-hiv","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Oxygen, rest periods and position for breathing; treat PJP (TMP-SMX, with corticosteroids for severe hypoxemia). Use Standard Precautions (HIV needs no special isolation). Restart combination antiretroviral therapy (cART): several drugs from different classes, taken every day on schedule to prevent resistance. Prevent infection: hand hygiene, food safety (no raw eggs, meat or seafood; wash produce), avoid cat litter and bird cages, avoid sick contacts. Offer nutrition support, mouth care, and nonjudgmental support for stigma and depression."]},{"title":"Prevention you should know","lines":["PrEP (pre-exposure prophylaxis): HIV-negative people at risk take a daily pill (or a long-acting injection); they need HIV testing every few months.","PEP (post-exposure prophylaxis): start as soon as possible, within 72 hours, and take for 28 days. For a needlestick: wash with soap and water, report immediately, get tested.","Treatment as prevention (TasP): a durably undetectable viral load means HIV is not transmitted sexually (“undetectable = untransmittable”).","Condoms, never sharing needles, testing at least once for everyone 13–64."]},{"title":"cART side effects to teach","lines":["Nausea, diarrhea, fatigue, rash (report a severe rash), high lipids and glucose, liver and kidney effects.","Many drug interactions (including St. John’s wort): check every new drug and supplement.","Missed doses let HIV become resistant."]}]},{"id":"c-hiv","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Track SpO₂, RR and temperature, weight, viral load (should become undetectable) and CD4 (should rise), side effects, adherence, and whether cost and support barriers were solved before discharge."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-hiv:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A person with HIV is diagnosed with AIDS when the CD4 count falls below:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"1,000 cells/mm³","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. AIDS = CD4 below 200 or an AIDS-defining illness."},{"id":"o1","text":"500 cells/mm³","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. AIDS = CD4 below 200 or an AIDS-defining illness."},{"id":"o2","text":"200 cells/mm³","correct":true,"why":"Supported. AIDS = CD4 below 200 or an AIDS-defining illness."},{"id":"o3","text":"50 cells/mm³","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. AIDS = CD4 below 200 or an AIDS-defining illness."}]},{"id":"c-hiv:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"36-year-old man with HIV who stopped his antiretroviral therapy 8 months ago after losing his job and insurance. For 3 weeks he has had a dry cough, fever and shortness of breath that gets worse on exertion. He has white patches in his mouth and has lost 7 kg. SpO₂ 88% on room air after walking, RR 26. His CD4 count is 140 cells/mm³ and his viral load is high.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: impaired gas exchange (PJP). Then risk for infection (other opportunistic infections), imbalanced nutrition, activity intolerance, and the barriers that stopped his treatment (cost, stigma, depression).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-hiv:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"36-year-old man with HIV who stopped his antiretroviral therapy 8 months ago after losing his job and insurance. For 3 weeks he has had a dry cough, fever and shortness of breath that gets worse on exertion. He has white patches in his mouth and has lost 7 kg. SpO₂ 88% on room air after walking, RR 26. His CD4 count is 140 cells/mm³ and his viral load is high.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Oxygen, rest periods and position for breathing; treat PJP (TMP-SMX, with corticosteroids for severe hypoxemia). Use Standard Precautions (HIV needs no special isolation). Restart combination antiretroviral therapy (cART): several drugs from different classes, taken every day on schedule to prevent resistance. Prevent infection: hand hygiene, food safety (no raw eggs, meat or seafood; wash produce), avoid cat litter and bird cages, avoid sick contacts. Offer nutrition support, mouth care, and nonjudgmental support for stigma and depression. Track SpO₂, RR and temperature, weight, viral load (should become undetectable) and CD4 (should rise), side effects, adherence, and whether cost and support barriers were solved before discharge.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"C. difficile is a spore-forming bacterium. Antibiotics (especially clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins and broad-spectrum drugs) wipe out normal gut flora so C. diff can overgrow and release toxins that inflame the colon. Other risks: older age, hospital or long-term care stays, PPIs, a weak immune system. Cues: 3 or more watery stools in 24 hours, foul odor, abdominal cramps, fever, high WBC, dehydration. Severe disease: pseudomembranous colitis, toxic megacolon (a distended, tender abdomen with fewer stools), perforation, sepsis."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: deficient fluid volume (diarrhea, low BP, rising creatinine) and infection (treat it and stop it spreading). Then risk for impaired skin integrity (perineal skin) and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium)."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Count and describe stools, check I&O, daily weight, BP and HR, creatinine and potassium, temperature and WBC, abdominal girth and tenderness, and perineal skin."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Day 6 of clindamycin: eight watery stools today, cramping, WBC 18,000.","rule":"Priority: deficient fluid volume (diarrhea, low BP, rising creatinine) and infection (treat it and stop it spreading). Then risk for impaired skin integrity (perineal skin) and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium).","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-cdiff","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"Clostridioides difficile infection","notes":["c-cdiff"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 19; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Eight watery stools, cramping, fever and a WBC of 18,000 on day 6 of clindamycin in an older woman on a PPI, with low BP and rising creatinine = C. difficile infection with dehydration and acute kidney injury.","Priority: Priority: deficient fluid volume (diarrhea, low BP, rising creatinine) and infection (treat it and stop it spreading). Then risk for impaired skin integrity (perineal skin) and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium).","Act: Contact Precautions (gown and gloves on entering the room; private room or cohorting; dedicated equipment such as a stethoscope and BP cuff), soap-and-water hand hygiene, and bleach cleaning. Stop the triggering antibiotic if possible, as ordered. Give oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole is no longer first choice). Avoid antidiarrheals (loperamide): they keep toxins in the colon. Replace fluids and electrolytes (potassium). Perineal care and barrier cream. Watch for toxic megacolon. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) is used for recurrent infection.","Reassess: Count and describe stools, check I&O, daily weight, BP and HR, creatinine and potassium, temperature and WBC, abdominal girth and tenderness, and perineal skin.","Use contact precautions, appropriate gown/glove use, dedicated equipment and sporicidal environmental cleaning. Soap and water is important for visibly soiled hands and during C. difficile outbreaks. CDC still prefers alcohol-based hand rub in most routine clinical situations; follow the facility’s infection-prevention policy."],"terms":["Spores","Stool testing"],"termDefinitions":{"Spores":"They survive for months on bed rails, commodes, call lights and hands. Spores resist drying and alcohol. Wash hands with soap and water (alcohol hand rub doesn’t kill spores); clean with a sporicidal (bleach) disinfectant; use dedicated equipment.","Stool testing":"Only unformed (liquid) stool is tested. Many people carry C. diff without disease; testing formed stool finds carriers, not infection. Send the sample promptly; don’t test for cure after treatment."},"patient":"78-year-old woman in a long-term care facility, admitted with a hip wound infection, now on day 6 of clindamycin. She also takes omeprazole. Today she has had 8 watery, foul-smelling stools, crampy lower abdominal pain and a temperature of 38.3 °C. HR 106, BP 98/60; her mouth is dry. WBC 18,000/mm³; creatinine up to 1.6 mg/dL.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-cdiff","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["C. difficile is a spore-forming bacterium. Antibiotics (especially clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins and broad-spectrum drugs) wipe out normal gut flora so C. diff can overgrow and release toxins that inflame the colon. Other risks: older age, hospital or long-term care stays, PPIs, a weak immune system. Cues: 3 or more watery stools in 24 hours, foul odor, abdominal cramps, fever, high WBC, dehydration. Severe disease: pseudomembranous colitis, toxic megacolon (a distended, tender abdomen with fewer stools), perforation, sepsis."]}]},{"id":"c-cdiff","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Eight watery stools, cramping, fever and a WBC of 18,000 on day 6 of clindamycin in an older woman on a PPI, with low BP and rising creatinine = C. difficile infection with dehydration and acute kidney injury."]}]},{"id":"c-cdiff","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: deficient fluid volume (diarrhea, low BP, rising creatinine) and infection (treat it and stop it spreading). Then risk for impaired skin integrity (perineal skin) and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium)."]}]},{"id":"c-cdiff","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["BP and HR normalize, urine output is adequate and creatinine falls; stools drop to fewer than 3 a day and become formed; no spread to other patients; perineal skin stays intact."]}]},{"id":"c-cdiff","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Contact Precautions (gown and gloves on entering the room; private room or cohorting; dedicated equipment such as a stethoscope and BP cuff), soap-and-water hand hygiene, and bleach cleaning. Stop the triggering antibiotic if possible, as ordered. Give oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole is no longer first choice). Avoid antidiarrheals (loperamide): they keep toxins in the colon. Replace fluids and electrolytes (potassium). Perineal care and barrier cream. Watch for toxic megacolon. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) is used for recurrent infection."]},{"title":"Teaching and prevention","lines":["Antimicrobial stewardship: take antibiotics only when needed and exactly as prescribed.","Visitors wash with soap and water and wear gowns and gloves.","At home: clean the bathroom with a bleach product; wash hands well; recurrence is common in the first 2 months, so report new diarrhea."]}]},{"id":"c-cdiff","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Count and describe stools, check I&O, daily weight, BP and HR, creatinine and potassium, temperature and WBC, abdominal girth and tenderness, and perineal skin."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Use contact precautions, appropriate gown/glove use, dedicated equipment and sporicidal environmental cleaning. Soap and water is important for visibly soiled hands and during C. difficile outbreaks. CDC still prefers alcohol-based hand rub in most routine clinical situations; follow the facility’s infection-prevention policy.","sources":[{"title":"CDC: C. difficile prevention in acute care","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/c-diff/hcp/clinical-guidance/index.html"},{"title":"CDC: healthcare hand hygiene and C. difficile","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/clean-hands/hcp/clinical-safety/"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-cdiff:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which hand hygiene is correct after caring for a patient with C. difficile?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Alcohol-based hand rub","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Alcohol does not kill C. diff spores; soap, water and friction remove them."},{"id":"o1","text":"Soap and water","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Alcohol does not kill C. diff spores; soap, water and friction remove them."},{"id":"o2","text":"Chlorhexidine wipes","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Alcohol does not kill C. diff spores; soap, water and friction remove them."},{"id":"o3","text":"No hand hygiene if gloves were worn","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Alcohol does not kill C. diff spores; soap, water and friction remove them."}]},{"id":"c-cdiff:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"78-year-old woman in a long-term care facility, admitted with a hip wound infection, now on day 6 of clindamycin. She also takes omeprazole. Today she has had 8 watery, foul-smelling stools, crampy lower abdominal pain and a temperature of 38.3 °C. HR 106, BP 98/60; her mouth is dry. WBC 18,000/mm³; creatinine up to 1.6 mg/dL.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: deficient fluid volume (diarrhea, low BP, rising creatinine) and infection (treat it and stop it spreading). Then risk for impaired skin integrity (perineal skin) and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-cdiff:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"78-year-old woman in a long-term care facility, admitted with a hip wound infection, now on day 6 of clindamycin. She also takes omeprazole. Today she has had 8 watery, foul-smelling stools, crampy lower abdominal pain and a temperature of 38.3 °C. HR 106, BP 98/60; her mouth is dry. WBC 18,000/mm³; creatinine up to 1.6 mg/dL.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Contact Precautions (gown and gloves on entering the room; private room or cohorting; dedicated equipment such as a stethoscope and BP cuff), soap-and-water hand hygiene, and bleach cleaning. Stop the triggering antibiotic if possible, as ordered. Give oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole is no longer first choice). Avoid antidiarrheals (loperamide): they keep toxins in the colon. Replace fluids and electrolytes (potassium). Perineal care and barrier cream. Watch for toxic megacolon. Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) is used for recurrent infection. Count and describe stools, check I&O, daily weight, BP and HR, creatinine and potassium, temperature and WBC, abdominal girth and tenderness, and perineal skin.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Know the signs of a local infection (redness, warmth, swelling, pain, purulent drainage, loss of function) and the signs it has become systemic (fever or hypothermia, chills, tachycardia, tachypnea, low BP, new confusion, low urine output, mottled skin, high lactate). MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) resists most beta-lactam antibiotics. Older adults may have no fever; confusion or a fall can be the first sign."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: infection and ineffective tissue perfusion (sepsis). Then risk for injury (confusion), impaired tissue integrity at the wound, and preventing spread to others."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Track MAP, HR, RR, temperature, mental status, urine output and repeat lactate; review culture and sensitivity results; trend WBC and the wound’s appearance and drainage; check vancomycin levels and creatinine."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Post-op day 4: red, hot incision with pus; now 39 °C, HR 118, confused.","rule":"Priority: infection and ineffective tissue perfusion (sepsis). Then risk for injury (confusion), impaired tissue integrity at the wound, and preventing spread to others.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-mrsa","ch":"19","chapters":["19"],"title":"MRSA wound infection progressing to sepsis","notes":["c-mrsa"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 19; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: An MRSA-infected wound plus fever, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, new confusion, a left shift and lactate 3.2 = sepsis from the wound, at risk of septic shock.","Priority: Priority: infection and ineffective tissue perfusion (sepsis). Then risk for injury (confusion), impaired tissue integrity at the wound, and preventing spread to others.","Act: Sepsis bundle: blood cultures before antibiotics, then IV antibiotics within 1 hour (vancomycin for MRSA), 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate of 4 or more, repeat lactate, vasopressors if BP doesn’t respond. Monitor VS and urine output often. Contact Precautions (gown and gloves, dedicated equipment). Wound care as ordered (the surgeon may open and drain it). Vancomycin: monitor trough levels and kidney function; infuse slowly (at least 60 minutes) to prevent infusion reaction (flushing, itching, low BP).","Reassess: Track MAP, HR, RR, temperature, mental status, urine output and repeat lactate; review culture and sensitivity results; trend WBC and the wound’s appearance and drainage; check vancomycin levels and creatinine."],"terms":["Left shift","Lactate"],"termDefinitions":{"Left shift":"More bands (immature neutrophils) than normal on the differential (normal bands about 0–5%). The marrow releases young neutrophils early when the body is fighting a serious bacterial infection. Supports acute bacterial infection; trend it with the WBC.","Lactate":"Above 2 mmol/L is abnormal; 4 or more means severe hypoperfusion. Poorly perfused tissues switch to anaerobic metabolism. Give fluids; repeat the lactate to see if perfusion improves."},"patient":"64-year-old man, post-op day 4 after a colectomy, with diabetes. His incision is red, hot and swollen, with thick yellow drainage; a wound culture grew MRSA. This evening his temperature is 39.1 °C, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, and he is newly confused. WBC 19,500 with 14% bands; lactate 3.2 mmol/L.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-mrsa","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Know the signs of a local infection (redness, warmth, swelling, pain, purulent drainage, loss of function) and the signs it has become systemic (fever or hypothermia, chills, tachycardia, tachypnea, low BP, new confusion, low urine output, mottled skin, high lactate). MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) resists most beta-lactam antibiotics. Older adults may have no fever; confusion or a fall can be the first sign."]},{"title":"Other resistant organisms","lines":["VRE (vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus): treated with linezolid or daptomycin.","CRE (carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales): very hard to treat, high death rate.","All are spread mainly by hands and contact; use Contact Precautions and strict hand hygiene."]}]},{"id":"c-mrsa","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["An MRSA-infected wound plus fever, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, new confusion, a left shift and lactate 3.2 = sepsis from the wound, at risk of septic shock."]}]},{"id":"c-mrsa","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: infection and ineffective tissue perfusion (sepsis). Then risk for injury (confusion), impaired tissue integrity at the wound, and preventing spread to others."]}]},{"id":"c-mrsa","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Within hours: MAP is 65 or above, HR and RR fall, lactate clears, urine output is at least 0.5 mL/kg/hour and mental status returns to normal. Over days: the wound improves and no one else acquires MRSA."]}]},{"id":"c-mrsa","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Sepsis bundle: blood cultures before antibiotics, then IV antibiotics within 1 hour (vancomycin for MRSA), 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate of 4 or more, repeat lactate, vasopressors if BP doesn’t respond. Monitor VS and urine output often. Contact Precautions (gown and gloves, dedicated equipment). Wound care as ordered (the surgeon may open and drain it). Vancomycin: monitor trough levels and kidney function; infuse slowly (at least 60 minutes) to prevent infusion reaction (flushing, itching, low BP)."]},{"title":"Preventing spread","lines":["Hand hygiene before and after every patient contact is the single most effective measure.","Chlorhexidine bathing and nasal decolonization (mupirocin) are used in some settings.","Clean shared equipment between patients; cover draining wounds."]}]},{"id":"c-mrsa","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Track MAP, HR, RR, temperature, mental status, urine output and repeat lactate; review culture and sensitivity results; trend WBC and the wound’s appearance and drainage; check vancomycin levels and creatinine."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-mrsa:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with an MRSA wound infection has HR 118, RR 24, BP 90/52, temperature 39 °C and new confusion. What is the priority action?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Change the wound dressing","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. This is sepsis. Cultures, then antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids and lactate."},{"id":"o1","text":"Obtain blood cultures and start IV antibiotics within the hour","correct":true,"why":"Supported. This is sepsis. Cultures, then antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids and lactate."},{"id":"o2","text":"Give oral acetaminophen and recheck in 4 hours","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. This is sepsis. Cultures, then antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids and lactate."},{"id":"o3","text":"Restrict fluids","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. This is sepsis. Cultures, then antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids and lactate."}]},{"id":"c-mrsa:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"64-year-old man, post-op day 4 after a colectomy, with diabetes. His incision is red, hot and swollen, with thick yellow drainage; a wound culture grew MRSA. This evening his temperature is 39.1 °C, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, and he is newly confused. WBC 19,500 with 14% bands; lactate 3.2 mmol/L.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: infection and ineffective tissue perfusion (sepsis). Then risk for injury (confusion), impaired tissue integrity at the wound, and preventing spread to others.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-mrsa:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"64-year-old man, post-op day 4 after a colectomy, with diabetes. His incision is red, hot and swollen, with thick yellow drainage; a wound culture grew MRSA. This evening his temperature is 39.1 °C, HR 118, RR 24, BP 92/54, and he is newly confused. WBC 19,500 with 14% bands; lactate 3.2 mmol/L.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Sepsis bundle: blood cultures before antibiotics, then IV antibiotics within 1 hour (vancomycin for MRSA), 30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate of 4 or more, repeat lactate, vasopressors if BP doesn’t respond. Monitor VS and urine output often. Contact Precautions (gown and gloves, dedicated equipment). Wound care as ordered (the surgeon may open and drain it). Vancomycin: monitor trough levels and kidney function; infuse slowly (at least 60 minutes) to prevent infusion reaction (flushing, itching, low BP). Track MAP, HR, RR, temperature, mental status, urine output and repeat lactate; review culture and sensitivity results; trend WBC and the wound’s appearance and drainage; check vancomycin levels and creatinine.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"In the first 24 hours after surgery the biggest danger is the airway and breathing. Watch sedation level first: excess sleepiness comes before the respiratory rate drops. Look for snoring or noisy breathing, slow or shallow breaths, falling SpO₂ or rising end-tidal CO₂, pinpoint pupils, and who is pressing the PCA button. Know who is at risk: older adults, obstructive sleep apnea, obesity, opioid-naive patients, lung or kidney disease, and anyone also on sedatives."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: ineffective breathing pattern and impaired gas exchange (airway and breathing come before pain). Then risk for injury from opioid excess, and later acute pain once he is safe."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Recheck his sedation, RR and depth, SpO₂ and end-tidal CO₂ every few minutes until stable, then closely for the next hours (naloxone wears off). Confirm the new pain plan keeps him at POSS 1–2 with tolerable pain."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Two hours after a knee replacement: hard to wake, snoring, RR 8, SpO₂ 86%.","rule":"Priority: ineffective breathing pattern and impaired gas exchange (airway and breathing come before pain). Then risk for injury from opioid excess, and later acute pain once he is safe.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-postresp","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Post-op opioid-induced respiratory depression","notes":["c-postresp"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 9; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Deep sedation (POSS 4), RR 8, SpO₂ 86% and a slow heart rate in an older man with untreated sleep apnea on a PCA pushed by someone else = opioid-induced respiratory depression with hypoventilation and hypoxemia. This can progress to respiratory arrest.","Priority: Priority: ineffective breathing pattern and impaired gas exchange (airway and breathing come before pain). Then risk for injury from opioid excess, and later acute pain once he is safe.","Act: Stop the opioid (pause the PCA). Stimulate him (call his name loudly, sternal rub), tell him to take deep breaths, raise the head of the bed or turn him on his side, open the airway and increase oxygen. Call the rapid response team. Give naloxone per protocol (dilute and give slowly, titrated to breathing, not to full wakefulness, so you don’t cause severe pain or withdrawal). Stay with him and monitor continuously (SpO₂ and capnography).","Reassess: Recheck his sedation, RR and depth, SpO₂ and end-tidal CO₂ every few minutes until stable, then closely for the next hours (naloxone wears off). Confirm the new pain plan keeps him at POSS 1–2 with tolerable pain.","Opioid-related shallow or slow breathing with reduced alertness needs immediate assessment, emergency escalation, breathing support and naloxone as indicated by protocol. Supplemental oxygen alone does not correct inadequate ventilation."],"terms":["Sedation scale (POSS)","PCA by proxy","Snoring after surgery","naloxone"],"termDefinitions":{"Sedation scale (POSS)":"1 awake and alert; 2 slightly drowsy, easily roused; 3 frequently drowsy, drifts off mid-conversation; 4 somnolent, minimal or no response to touch and voice; S = normal sleep, easy to wake. Opioids blunt the brain’s drive to breathe. Sleepiness shows up first; respiratory depression follows. At 3: hold the opioid, cut the dose and watch closely. At 4: stop the opioid, stimulate, call for help and give naloxone.","PCA by proxy":"A family member or a nurse not following protocol presses the button for the patient. A PCA is safe because a sedated patient can’t press the button, so he gets no more drug. Anyone else pressing it removes that safety. Teach: only the patient presses the button.","Snoring after surgery":"Loud snoring, pauses, obstructive breathing. Relaxed throat muscles from anesthesia and opioids, plus sleep apnea, block the upper airway. Do not assume he is resting comfortably. Assess his arousal and SpO₂; use his CPAP; position him on his side or with the head up."},"patient":"68-year-old man, 2 hours after transfer from PACU following a total knee replacement. He has obstructive sleep apnea (doesn’t use his CPAP at home) and a hydromorphone PCA, and his wife has been pressing the button “so he doesn’t hurt.” He is snoring loudly and only rouses when you shake his shoulder and call his name, then drifts off mid-sentence. RR 8, shallow; SpO₂ 86% on 2 L; HR 58; BP 104/62.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-postresp","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["In the first 24 hours after surgery the biggest danger is the airway and breathing. Watch sedation level first: excess sleepiness comes before the respiratory rate drops. Look for snoring or noisy breathing, slow or shallow breaths, falling SpO₂ or rising end-tidal CO₂, pinpoint pupils, and who is pressing the PCA button. Know who is at risk: older adults, obstructive sleep apnea, obesity, opioid-naive patients, lung or kidney disease, and anyone also on sedatives."]},{"title":"Other early post-op breathing problems","lines":["Atelectasis (collapsed alveoli): diminished breath sounds, crackles, low-grade fever in the first days; prevent with incentive spirometry (about 10 times an hour while awake), deep breathing, turning and walking.","Hypoxemia: restlessness and confusion can be the first sign, especially in older adults.","Pneumonia and pulmonary embolism (sudden dyspnea, chest pain, tachycardia) are later threats."]}]},{"id":"c-postresp","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Deep sedation (POSS 4), RR 8, SpO₂ 86% and a slow heart rate in an older man with untreated sleep apnea on a PCA pushed by someone else = opioid-induced respiratory depression with hypoventilation and hypoxemia. This can progress to respiratory arrest."]}]},{"id":"c-postresp","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: ineffective breathing pattern and impaired gas exchange (airway and breathing come before pain). Then risk for injury from opioid excess, and later acute pain once he is safe."]}]},{"id":"c-postresp","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Within minutes: he is arousable (POSS 1–2), RR 12 or more with adequate depth, SpO₂ 92% or higher and a clear airway. Over the shift: pain is controlled with a plan that doesn’t over-sedate him, and his wife understands why she must not press the button."]}]},{"id":"c-postresp","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Stop the opioid (pause the PCA). Stimulate him (call his name loudly, sternal rub), tell him to take deep breaths, raise the head of the bed or turn him on his side, open the airway and increase oxygen. Call the rapid response team. Give naloxone per protocol (dilute and give slowly, titrated to breathing, not to full wakefulness, so you don’t cause severe pain or withdrawal). Stay with him and monitor continuously (SpO₂ and capnography)."]},{"title":"Naloxone facts","lines":["It reverses opioids within minutes, but it wears off sooner than many opioids (it lasts about 30–90 minutes), so sedation can return: keep monitoring and repeat doses as ordered.","Too much too fast causes pain, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia and hypertension."]},{"title":"Safer pain control after surgery","lines":["Multimodal analgesia: acetaminophen, NSAIDs if allowed, nerve blocks or local anesthetics, plus the lowest effective opioid dose.","Reassess sedation and breathing before each opioid dose and at the drug’s peak.","Patients with sleep apnea bring their CPAP and use it whenever they sleep."]}]},{"id":"c-postresp","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recheck his sedation, RR and depth, SpO₂ and end-tidal CO₂ every few minutes until stable, then closely for the next hours (naloxone wears off). Confirm the new pain plan keeps him at POSS 1–2 with tolerable pain."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Opioid-related shallow or slow breathing with reduced alertness needs immediate assessment, emergency escalation, breathing support and naloxone as indicated by protocol. Supplemental oxygen alone does not correct inadequate ventilation.","sources":[{"title":"FDA: naloxone, emergency help and continued observation","url":"https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/access-naloxone-can-save-life-during-opioid-overdose"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-postresp:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A post-op patient on a hydromorphone PCA is snoring, rouses only to a shoulder shake, and has RR 8. What should the nurse do first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Let him sleep; he is finally comfortable","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Stop the opioid and stimulate him (with the head up and oxygen), then call for help and give diluted naloxone slowly per protocol."},{"id":"o1","text":"Stop the PCA and stimulate the patient","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Stop the opioid and stimulate him (with the head up and oxygen), then call for help and give diluted naloxone slowly per protocol."},{"id":"o2","text":"Give naloxone 2 mg IV push rapidly","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Stop the opioid and stimulate him (with the head up and oxygen), then call for help and give diluted naloxone slowly per protocol."},{"id":"o3","text":"Recheck in 30 minutes","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Stop the opioid and stimulate him (with the head up and oxygen), then call for help and give diluted naloxone slowly per protocol."}]},{"id":"c-postresp:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"68-year-old man, 2 hours after transfer from PACU following a total knee replacement. He has obstructive sleep apnea (doesn’t use his CPAP at home) and a hydromorphone PCA, and his wife has been pressing the button “so he doesn’t hurt.” He is snoring loudly and only rouses when you shake his shoulder and call his name, then drifts off mid-sentence. RR 8, shallow; SpO₂ 86% on 2 L; HR 58; BP 104/62.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: ineffective breathing pattern and impaired gas exchange (airway and breathing come before pain). Then risk for injury from opioid excess, and later acute pain once he is safe.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-postresp:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"68-year-old man, 2 hours after transfer from PACU following a total knee replacement. He has obstructive sleep apnea (doesn’t use his CPAP at home) and a hydromorphone PCA, and his wife has been pressing the button “so he doesn’t hurt.” He is snoring loudly and only rouses when you shake his shoulder and call his name, then drifts off mid-sentence. RR 8, shallow; SpO₂ 86% on 2 L; HR 58; BP 104/62.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Stop the opioid (pause the PCA). Stimulate him (call his name loudly, sternal rub), tell him to take deep breaths, raise the head of the bed or turn him on his side, open the airway and increase oxygen. Call the rapid response team. Give naloxone per protocol (dilute and give slowly, titrated to breathing, not to full wakefulness, so you don’t cause severe pain or withdrawal). Stay with him and monitor continuously (SpO₂ and capnography). Recheck his sedation, RR and depth, SpO₂ and end-tidal CO₂ every few minutes until stable, then closely for the next hours (naloxone wears off). Confirm the new pain plan keeps him at POSS 1–2 with tolerable pain.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Malignant hyperthermia (MH) is a rare, inherited muscle disorder: certain inhaled (volatile) anesthetics and succinylcholine make muscle cells release calcium uncontrollably, so the muscles contract and burn energy. Earliest cues: an unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia and dysrhythmias, muscle rigidity (the jaw, the masseter, first), tachypnea, and mottling. High fever is a late sign. It can start in the OR or hours later in PACU. Ask every patient about personal or family problems with anesthesia."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: hyperthermia and the hypermetabolic state (stop the trigger and give dantrolene), together with impaired gas exchange and risk for decreased cardiac output (hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias). Then risk for acute kidney injury."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Watch end-tidal CO₂, HR and rhythm, temperature, potassium and blood gases, CK and myoglobin, and the color and amount of urine. Watch for recurrence in the ICU for at least 24 hours."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Under general anesthesia: end-tidal CO₂ climbing, HR 140, jaw rigid. Temperature still near normal.","rule":"Priority: hyperthermia and the hypermetabolic state (stop the trigger and give dantrolene), together with impaired gas exchange and risk for decreased cardiac output (hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias). Then risk for acute kidney injury.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-malhyp","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Malignant hyperthermia","notes":["c-malhyp"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 9; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Rising end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia with ectopy, rigidity and climbing temperature after sevoflurane + succinylcholine, with a family history = malignant hyperthermia crisis. It is life-threatening within minutes.","Priority: Priority: hyperthermia and the hypermetabolic state (stop the trigger and give dantrolene), together with impaired gas exchange and risk for decreased cardiac output (hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias). Then risk for acute kidney injury.","Act: Call for help and the MH cart. Stop the triggering agent; hyperventilate with 100% oxygen. Give dantrolene IV (it stops the calcium release; repeat until signs resolve). Cool the patient (ice packs to the groin and armpits, cold IV fluids, cooling blanket). Treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias (insulin and glucose, calcium; avoid calcium channel blockers with dantrolene). Monitor arterial blood gases, potassium, CK, urine output and core temperature. Transfer to the ICU: MH can recur in the next 24–72 hours.","Reassess: Watch end-tidal CO₂, HR and rhythm, temperature, potassium and blood gases, CK and myoglobin, and the color and amount of urine. Watch for recurrence in the ICU for at least 24 hours.","Suspected malignant hyperthermia requires immediate help, discontinuing triggering agents, 100% oxygen and rapid IV dantrolene under the crisis protocol. Do not wait for fever to begin the response."],"terms":["Rising end-tidal CO₂","Masseter (jaw) rigidity","dantrolene"],"termDefinitions":{"Rising end-tidal CO₂":"The capnography number climbs even though the patient is being ventilated faster. Hypermetabolic muscle produces huge amounts of CO₂. Often the first sign. Don’t wait for the temperature.","Masseter (jaw) rigidity":"The jaw won’t open after succinylcholine. Sustained muscle contraction from uncontrolled calcium release. A warning; general muscle rigidity follows."},"patient":"24-year-old man having an ACL repair under general anesthesia with sevoflurane; he received succinylcholine for intubation. Twenty minutes in, his end-tidal CO₂ rises from 38 to 62 mm Hg despite more ventilation, HR is 142 and irregular, his jaw and chest are rigid, and his skin is mottled. Temperature is 37.9 °C and climbing. His mother mentioned “a cousin who almost died during surgery.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-malhyp","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Malignant hyperthermia (MH) is a rare, inherited muscle disorder: certain inhaled (volatile) anesthetics and succinylcholine make muscle cells release calcium uncontrollably, so the muscles contract and burn energy. Earliest cues: an unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia and dysrhythmias, muscle rigidity (the jaw, the masseter, first), tachypnea, and mottling. High fever is a late sign. It can start in the OR or hours later in PACU. Ask every patient about personal or family problems with anesthesia."]},{"title":"What follows if it isn’t treated","lines":["Hyperthermia (temperature can rise by about 1 °C every 5 minutes), hyperkalemia from damaged muscle cells (dysrhythmias, cardiac arrest), metabolic and respiratory acidosis.","Rhabdomyolysis: CK and myoglobin rise; myoglobin can cause acute kidney injury (dark, cola-colored urine).","DIC and death."]}]},{"id":"c-malhyp","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Rising end-tidal CO₂, tachycardia with ectopy, rigidity and climbing temperature after sevoflurane + succinylcholine, with a family history = malignant hyperthermia crisis. It is life-threatening within minutes."]}]},{"id":"c-malhyp","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: hyperthermia and the hypermetabolic state (stop the trigger and give dantrolene), together with impaired gas exchange and risk for decreased cardiac output (hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias). Then risk for acute kidney injury."]}]},{"id":"c-malhyp","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["End-tidal CO₂ and heart rate fall, rigidity resolves and temperature drops toward normal (cooling stops at about 38 °C so he doesn’t overcorrect); potassium and acid–base return to normal; urine output stays at least 0.5 mL/kg/hour and clear."]}]},{"id":"c-malhyp","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Call for help and the MH cart. Stop the triggering agent; hyperventilate with 100% oxygen. Give dantrolene IV (it stops the calcium release; repeat until signs resolve). Cool the patient (ice packs to the groin and armpits, cold IV fluids, cooling blanket). Treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias (insulin and glucose, calcium; avoid calcium channel blockers with dantrolene). Monitor arterial blood gases, potassium, CK, urine output and core temperature. Transfer to the ICU: MH can recur in the next 24–72 hours."]},{"title":"Prevention and teaching","lines":["Ask about personal and family reactions to anesthesia before every surgery.","Known or suspected MH: a trigger-free anesthetic (no volatile gases, no succinylcholine), with dantrolene ready.","The patient and blood relatives should be tested and wear a medical alert ID."]}]},{"id":"c-malhyp","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Watch end-tidal CO₂, HR and rhythm, temperature, potassium and blood gases, CK and myoglobin, and the color and amount of urine. Watch for recurrence in the ICU for at least 24 hours."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Suspected malignant hyperthermia requires immediate help, discontinuing triggering agents, 100% oxygen and rapid IV dantrolene under the crisis protocol. Do not wait for fever to begin the response.","sources":[{"title":"MHAUS: managing a malignant hyperthermia crisis","url":"https://www.mhaus.org/healthcare-professionals/managing-a-crisis/"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-malhyp:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Under general anesthesia, which finding is the earliest sign of malignant hyperthermia?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Temperature 40 °C","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂ (with tachycardia and rigidity) comes first. Fever is late; dark urine (myoglobin) follows muscle breakdown."},{"id":"o1","text":"Rising end-tidal carbon dioxide","correct":true,"why":"Supported. An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂ (with tachycardia and rigidity) comes first. Fever is late; dark urine (myoglobin) follows muscle breakdown."},{"id":"o2","text":"Dark, cola-colored urine","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂ (with tachycardia and rigidity) comes first. Fever is late; dark urine (myoglobin) follows muscle breakdown."},{"id":"o3","text":"Hypotension","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂ (with tachycardia and rigidity) comes first. Fever is late; dark urine (myoglobin) follows muscle breakdown."}]},{"id":"c-malhyp:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old man having an ACL repair under general anesthesia with sevoflurane; he received succinylcholine for intubation. Twenty minutes in, his end-tidal CO₂ rises from 38 to 62 mm Hg despite more ventilation, HR is 142 and irregular, his jaw and chest are rigid, and his skin is mottled. Temperature is 37.9 °C and climbing. His mother mentioned “a cousin who almost died during surgery.”","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: hyperthermia and the hypermetabolic state (stop the trigger and give dantrolene), together with impaired gas exchange and risk for decreased cardiac output (hyperkalemia, dysrhythmias). Then risk for acute kidney injury.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-malhyp:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old man having an ACL repair under general anesthesia with sevoflurane; he received succinylcholine for intubation. Twenty minutes in, his end-tidal CO₂ rises from 38 to 62 mm Hg despite more ventilation, HR is 142 and irregular, his jaw and chest are rigid, and his skin is mottled. Temperature is 37.9 °C and climbing. His mother mentioned “a cousin who almost died during surgery.”","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Call for help and the MH cart. Stop the triggering agent; hyperventilate with 100% oxygen. Give dantrolene IV (it stops the calcium release; repeat until signs resolve). Cool the patient (ice packs to the groin and armpits, cold IV fluids, cooling blanket). Treat hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias (insulin and glucose, calcium; avoid calcium channel blockers with dantrolene). Monitor arterial blood gases, potassium, CK, urine output and core temperature. Transfer to the ICU: MH can recur in the next 24–72 hours. Watch end-tidal CO₂, HR and rhythm, temperature, potassium and blood gases, CK and myoglobin, and the color and amount of urine. Watch for recurrence in the ICU for at least 24 hours.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Dehiscence = the wound edges separate; evisceration = organs (usually bowel) push out through the open wound. It usually happens about 5–10 days after surgery, often after straining, coughing, sneezing or vomiting. Cues: a sudden increase in serosanguineous (pink, watery) drainage, the patient feeling something “pop” or “give way,” separated edges, visible tissue. Risk factors: obesity, diabetes, poor nutrition (low protein), smoking, steroids, wound infection, older age, abdominal distension."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: protect the exposed organs (impaired tissue integrity and risk for infection), and watch for hypovolemic shock. Then anxiety (stay with him)."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Check that the dressing stays moist, watch vital signs and urine output for shock, and assess his anxiety until he goes to surgery. Afterward, watch the wound for infection and teach prevention."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Post-op day 6 after a laparotomy: “something popped” when he coughed; bowel is visible.","rule":"Priority: protect the exposed organs (impaired tissue integrity and risk for infection), and watch for hypovolemic shock. Then anxiety (stay with him).","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-evisc","ch":"9","chapters":["9"],"title":"Wound dehiscence and evisceration","notes":["c-evisc"],"book":"Ignatavicius chapter 9; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: An open abdominal incision with bowel exposed after coughing on post-op day 6, in a man with obesity and diabetes, plus tachycardia = evisceration, a surgical emergency (risk of infection, bowel injury and shock).","Priority: Priority: protect the exposed organs (impaired tissue integrity and risk for infection), and watch for hypovolemic shock. Then anxiety (stay with him).","Act: Stay with him and call for help (the surgeon or rapid response). Cover the wound with sterile dressings moistened with warm sterile normal saline (keep them moist). Do not try to push organs back in. Position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent (or hips flexed) to relax the abdominal muscles. Keep him NPO, take vital signs often, watch for shock, and prepare for surgery. Stay calm and reassure him.","Reassess: Check that the dressing stays moist, watch vital signs and urine output for shock, and assess his anxiety until he goes to surgery. Afterward, watch the wound for infection and teach prevention."],"terms":["Serosanguineous drainage"],"termDefinitions":{"Serosanguineous drainage":"Pink, watery fluid on the dressing. Plasma mixed with a little blood; a sudden gush can mean the deep layers are separating. Look at the incision and tell the surgeon about any sudden increase."},"patient":"61-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes, post-op day 6 after an open bowel resection. His incision had pink drainage yesterday. During a coughing fit he says, “Something just popped.” The dressing is soaked with serosanguineous fluid, the edges of the incision have separated, and loops of bowel are visible. He is pale and anxious; HR 112, BP 108/70.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-evisc","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Dehiscence = the wound edges separate; evisceration = organs (usually bowel) push out through the open wound. It usually happens about 5–10 days after surgery, often after straining, coughing, sneezing or vomiting. Cues: a sudden increase in serosanguineous (pink, watery) drainage, the patient feeling something “pop” or “give way,” separated edges, visible tissue. Risk factors: obesity, diabetes, poor nutrition (low protein), smoking, steroids, wound infection, older age, abdominal distension."]},{"title":"How wounds heal","lines":["First intention: edges closed (sutures, staples); least scarring.","Second intention: left open to heal from the bottom up (granulation tissue); infected or large wounds.","Third intention (delayed closure): left open first, then closed once infection is cleared."]}]},{"id":"c-evisc","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["An open abdominal incision with bowel exposed after coughing on post-op day 6, in a man with obesity and diabetes, plus tachycardia = evisceration, a surgical emergency (risk of infection, bowel injury and shock)."]}]},{"id":"c-evisc","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: protect the exposed organs (impaired tissue integrity and risk for infection), and watch for hypovolemic shock. Then anxiety (stay with him)."]}]},{"id":"c-evisc","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The bowel stays moist, covered and uninjured until surgery; vital signs stay stable; he is prepared for the OR and calmer. Later: the wound heals without infection."]}]},{"id":"c-evisc","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Stay with him and call for help (the surgeon or rapid response). Cover the wound with sterile dressings moistened with warm sterile normal saline (keep them moist). Do not try to push organs back in. Position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent (or hips flexed) to relax the abdominal muscles. Keep him NPO, take vital signs often, watch for shock, and prepare for surgery. Stay calm and reassure him."]},{"title":"Prevention","lines":["Teach splinting the incision with a pillow when coughing or sneezing; avoid straining (stool softeners) and heavy lifting.","An abdominal binder for high-risk patients.","Good protein, vitamin C and zinc intake; tight glucose control; stop smoking."]},{"title":"Wound infection and drains","lines":["Infection usually shows 3–6 days after surgery: redness, warmth, swelling, tenderness, purulent drainage, fever, rising WBC.","Closed drains (Jackson-Pratt, Hemovac) work by suction: empty when half full, then compress the bulb or bellows before closing; measure and record drainage.","Drainage colors: serous (clear, yellow), sanguineous (red, fresh blood), serosanguineous (pink), purulent (thick, cloudy, white, yellow or green: infection)."]}]},{"id":"c-evisc","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Check that the dressing stays moist, watch vital signs and urine output for shock, and assess his anxiety until he goes to surgery. Afterward, watch the wound for infection and teach prevention."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-evisc:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient’s abdominal wound has opened and loops of bowel are visible. What should the nurse do first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Gently push the bowel back into the abdomen","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Keep the organs moist and protected with saline-moistened sterile dressings, then call the surgeon. Never push organs back; position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent."},{"id":"o1","text":"Cover the wound with sterile dressings moistened with sterile normal saline","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Keep the organs moist and protected with saline-moistened sterile dressings, then call the surgeon. Never push organs back; position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent."},{"id":"o2","text":"Apply a dry sterile dressing and tape tightly","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Keep the organs moist and protected with saline-moistened sterile dressings, then call the surgeon. Never push organs back; position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent."},{"id":"o3","text":"Have the patient sit upright with legs straight","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Keep the organs moist and protected with saline-moistened sterile dressings, then call the surgeon. Never push organs back; position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent."}]},{"id":"c-evisc:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"61-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes, post-op day 6 after an open bowel resection. His incision had pink drainage yesterday. During a coughing fit he says, “Something just popped.” The dressing is soaked with serosanguineous fluid, the edges of the incision have separated, and loops of bowel are visible. He is pale and anxious; HR 112, BP 108/70.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: protect the exposed organs (impaired tissue integrity and risk for infection), and watch for hypovolemic shock. Then anxiety (stay with him).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-evisc:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"61-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes, post-op day 6 after an open bowel resection. His incision had pink drainage yesterday. During a coughing fit he says, “Something just popped.” The dressing is soaked with serosanguineous fluid, the edges of the incision have separated, and loops of bowel are visible. He is pale and anxious; HR 112, BP 108/70.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Stay with him and call for help (the surgeon or rapid response). Cover the wound with sterile dressings moistened with warm sterile normal saline (keep them moist). Do not try to push organs back in. Position in low Fowler’s with the knees bent (or hips flexed) to relax the abdominal muscles. Keep him NPO, take vital signs often, watch for shock, and prepare for surgery. Stay calm and reassure him. Check that the dressing stays moist, watch vital signs and urine output for shock, and assess his anxiety until he goes to surgery. Afterward, watch the wound for infection and teach prevention.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""}]}};
